    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189207": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Department of Genetics",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Genetics Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Genetics at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for several Research Associate positions in the Academic Support Staff. This appointment will be initially for one (1) year and continuation during that time period and renewal are based on satisfactory performance and availability of funding (limited to three (3) years). Expertise is required in the specific area of human genetics, developmental genetics, epigenetics, functional genomics, or computational genomics. Applicants must have an M.D and/or Ph.D or equivalent degree. Responsibilities may include designing and carrying out genetic experiments, as well as preparation of manuscripts and supervision of students. The successful applicant will have an opportunity to receive mentorship in grants writing and grants proposals suitable to early-stage academic investigators."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/187556": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Department of Physiology",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Physiology Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Physiology at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for a Research Associate position in the Academic Support Staff. This appointment will be initially for one (1) year and continuation during that time period and renewal are based on satisfactory performance and availability of funding (limited to three (3) years). Expertise is required in the specific area of mass spectrometry-based proteomics, including experience with LC-MS/MS instrumentation, experimental design, and proteomics data analysis. Candidates should possess strong expertise in protein biochemistry, molecular biology, and quantitative proteomics approaches. A strong record of original peer-reviewed publications, scientific leadership, communication, and interpersonal skills is required. Applicants must have a Ph.D. or M.D./Ph.D. degree. Responsibilities may include conducting independent and collaborative research in structural proteomics, chemical biology, and mitochondrial biology; developing and implementing mass spectrometry-based workflows including chemical crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS), quantitative proteomics, and protein interaction mapping; operating and maintaining advanced LC-MS/MS instrumentation; analyzing complex proteomics datasets; mentoring students and trainees; contributing to manuscript preparation, grant writing, and collaborative research projects. The successful applicant will have an opportunity to establish and lead new structural proteomics and crosslinking mass spectrometry capabilities within a newly established research program; develop independent research projects at the interface of proteomics, structural biology, neuroscience, and reproductive biology; collaborate with investigators across Penn's structural biology, proteomics, mitochondrial biology, and neuroscience communities; receive mentorship in laboratory leadership, grant writing, project development, and career advancement; and contribute to high-impact interdisciplinary research programs. Candidates should have demonstrated experience designing and executing independent research projects and working effectively within collaborative multidisciplinary teams. Experience with Orbitrap-based mass spectrometry platforms, quantitative proteomics workflows, and advanced proteomics software tools is required. Preferred qualifications include expertise in chemical crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS), structural proteomics, protein interaction mapping, chemical biology, development of novel analytical methodologies, and computational analysis of proteomics datasets. Experience with mitochondrial biology, neuroscience, reproductive biology, cryo-EM, or integrative structural biology is desirable but not required. Ph.D. or M.D./Ph.D."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/161705": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Genetics of Psychiatric Disorders",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Psychiatry Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Psychiatry at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for a Research Associate position in the Academic Support Staff. This appointment will be initially for one (1) year and continuation during that time period and renewal are based on satisfactory performance and availability of funding (limited to three (3) years). Expertise is required in the specific area of neuroscience. Applicants must have a Ph.D. degree. Responsibilities may include designing and conducting experimental studies to investigate the brain mechanisms underlying pain and drug addiction. This includes employing advanced neuroscience techniques such as rodent behavioral assays, immunohistochemistry, in vivo calcium imaging, optogenetics, chemogenetics, and single-cell RNA sequencing. The position also involves analyzing complex datasets, interpreting findings, and preparing scientific manuscripts and grant proposals. The successful applicant will have an opportunity to to contribute significantly to translational neuroscience and the Corder lab’s mission. We seek candidates who embrace and reflect diversity in the broadest sense. The University of Pennsylvania is an EOE. Minorities/women/individuals with disabilities/protected veterans are encouraged to apply."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/170067": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Human Immunology - Laura Su's lab",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Department of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Medicine - Rheumatology Location: Philadelphia, PA The Division of Rheumatology in the Department of Medicine at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for a Research Associate position in the Academic Support Staff. This appointment will be initially for one (1) year and continuation during that time period and renewal are based on satisfactory performance and availability of funding (limited to three (3) years). Expertise is required in the specific area of T cell biology and bioinformatics. The ideal candidate will bring deep expertise in tissue culture and flow cytometry, and a broad range of immunologic techniques. A proven track record in analyzing bulk and single-cell RNA-seq, TCR-seq, and BCR-seq data is essential. Candidates must be proficient in advanced statistical and computational methods and have a solid understanding of adaptive immunity. We are looking for a highly self-motivated individual with strong problem-solving skills, effective communication, and excellent organizational abilities. The candidate must be able to work independently and collaboratively. Applicants must have a Ph.D. degree. Responsibilities may include performing T cell stimulation, antibody staining, cell sorting, preparing bulk and single-cell sequencing libraries, analyzing and integrating RNA-seq, ATAC-seq, TCR-seq, and BCR-seq multi-omic datasets using R or Python. Experience with advanced statistical modeling is expected. Familiarity with spatial transcriptomics is a plus. The successful applicant will have an opportunity to contribute to publication-driven projects aimed at understanding immune dysregulation in autoimmune diseases and identifying novel biomarkers for type 1 diabetes (T1D). This position offers significant independence, leadership opportunities on high-impact projects, mentorship of junior lab members, and active involvement in cutting-edge translational immunology research. The role includes strong support for authorship and professional development to foster research independence within a highly collaborative and intellectually stimulating environment."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/187059": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Neuroimmunology",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Psychiatry Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Psychiatry at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for a Research Associate position in the Academic Support Staff. This appointment will be initially for one (1) year and continuation during that time period and renewal are based on satisfactory performance and availability of funding (limited to three (3) years). Applicants must have a Ph.D. degree. Responsibilities may include managing and executing multiple research projects in the field of neuroimmunology, using murine models to understand and treat brain diseases using immune cell therapies, and analyzing and interpreting results of those projects. Writing peer reviewed manuscripts and grant applications in the field of neuroimmunology. The successful applicant will have an opportunity to publish first-author manuscripts, manage and mentor trainees at the staff and graduate student level, and present their work at conferences."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192277": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Neuroscience",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Neuroscience Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Neuroscience at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for a Research Associate position in the Academic Support Staff. This appointment will be initially for one (1) year and continuation during that time period and renewal are based on satisfactory performance and availability of funding (limited to three (3) years). Expertise is required in the specific area of neural development, axon guidance, neuronal specification, or neural circuit assembly; experience working with Drosophila and/or mouse model systems; a strong background in molecular genetics, genomics, transcriptional regulation, and developmental neuroscience; demonstrated ability to conduct independent research and publish findings in peer-reviewed journals; and excellent written and verbal communication skills. Applicants must have a Ph.D. or equivalent degree. Responsibilities may include designing and conducting independent research projects; performing molecular, genetic, genomic, and biochemical experiments; analyzing and interpreting data; preparing manuscripts and presentations; collaborating with laboratory members; and contributing to the broader scientific mission of the laboratory. The successful applicant will have an opportunity to develop and lead studies focused on the mechanisms controlling axon guidance and neuronal specification during nervous system development in both fly and mouse models. Research projects will investigate transcriptional and signaling networks downstream of the Frazzled (Fra) and Deleted in Colorectal Cancer (DCC) receptors that direct neural circuit assembly and neuronal differentiation. These studies aim to elucidate how receptor-mediated transcriptional programs contribute to the formation of functional neural circuits and how disruptions in these pathways lead to neurodevelopmental disorders. Candidates with experience investigating transcriptional networks regulated by Fra/DCC receptor signaling and their roles in neural circuit development are especially encouraged to apply. Such expertise will provide valuable contributions to ongoing studies examining the genetic and molecular mechanisms governing neuronal connectivity and will support the candidate's development toward an independent research career."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/178978": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Neuroscience",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Neuroscience Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Neuroscience at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for a Research Associate position in the Academic Support Staff. This appointment will be initially for one (1) year and continuation during that time period and renewal are based on satisfactory performance and availability of funding (limited to three (3) years). Expertise is required in the specific area of in vivo electrophysiology and optogenetic manipulation in freely behaving mice, as well as in Deeplabcut and MATLAB/Python-based data analysis. Applicants must have a Ph.D. or equivalent degree. Responsibilities may include working independently and as part of collaborative research groups studying the neuronal basis of interbrain synchrony during the social interaction of freely behaving mice using behavioral, neurophysiological, and computational techniques. The successful applicant will have an opportunity to develop independence in research through writing grants and peer-reviewed publications."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/165382": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Neuroscience",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Neuroscience Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Neuroscience at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for a Research Associate position in the Academic Support Staff. This appointment will be initially for one (1) year and continuation during that time period and renewal are based on satisfactory performance and availability of funding (limited to three (3) years). Expertise is required in the specific area of in vivo electrophysiology in NHPs and Matlab/Python-based data analysis. Applicants must have a Ph.D. or equivalent degree. Responsibilities may include working independently and as part of collaborative research groups studying the neuronal basis of decision-making in the primate cortico-basal ganglia pathway using behavioral, neurophysiological, and computational techniques. The successful applicant will have an opportunity to develop independence in research through writing grants and peer-reviewed publications."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/167238": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Neuroscience",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Neuroscience Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Neuroscience at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for a Research Associate position in the Academic Support Staff. This appointment will be initially for one (1) year and continuation during that time period and renewal are based on satisfactory performance and availability of funding (limited to three (3) years). Expertise is required in the specific area of in vivo electrophysiology in rodents and Matlab/Python-based data analysis. Applicants must have a Ph.D. or equivalent degree. Responsibilities may include working independently and as part of collaborative research groups studying the neuronal basis of motor control in the rodent cortico-basal ganglia pathway using behavioral, neurophysiological, and viral techniques. The successful applicant will have an opportunity to develop independence in research through writing grants and peer-reviewed publications."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/180815": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Neuroscience",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Neuroscience Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Neuroscience at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for a Research Associate position in the Academic Support Staff. This appointment will be initially for one (1) year and continuation during that time period and renewal are based on satisfactory performance and availability of funding (limited to three (3) years). Expertise is required in the specific area of in vivo electrophysiology and data analysis using Python or Matlab, as well as experience with behavioral experiments. Applicants must have a Ph.D. or equivalent degree. Responsibilities may include include working independently and as part of collaborative research groups on basic science research projects to elucidate the mechanisms underlying the regulation of REM sleep in mice and to understand how disturbances in REM sleep contribute to the development of psychiatric disorders. The successful applicant will have an opportunity to develop independence in research through designing and supervising research projects, writing grants, and peer-reviewed publications."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/172988": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Neuroscience",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Neuroscience Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Neuroscience at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for a Research Associate position in the Academic Support Staff. This appointment will be initially for one (1) year and continuation during that time period and renewal are based on satisfactory performance and availability of funding (limited to three (3) years). Expertise is required in the specific area of peripheral sensory neuron biology, evaluation of skin immunity, and helminth parasitology work. Applicants must have a Ph.D. or equivalent degree. Responsibilities may include working independently and as part of collaborative research groups studying the effects of helminth parasitic formulations on calcium influx, neuropeptide release, and behavior induced by specific subsets of itch-inducing neurons. Moreover, this position will evaluate the in vivo transcriptional effects induced by itch-transmitting neurons on sort-purified keratinocytes. The successful applicant will have an opportunity to develop independence in research through leadership and mentorship training as well as grant and peer-reviewed manuscript preparation."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189086": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Neuroscience",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Neuroscience Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Neuroscience at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for a Research Associate position in the Academic Support Staff. This appointment will be initially for one (1) year and continuation during that time period and renewal are based on satisfactory performance and availability of funding (limited to three (3) years). Expertise is required in the specific area of epigenetics, neuronal activity-induced gene expression and related brain disorders. Applicants must have a Ph.D. or equivalent degree. Responsibilities may include working independently and as part of collaborative research groups studying epigenetic mechanisms in neuronal gene expression and associated brain disorders. The successful applicant will have an opportunity to develop independence in research through writing grants and peer-reviewed publications."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/185700": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Neuroscience",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Neuroscience Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Neuroscience at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for a Research Associate position in the Academic Support Staff. This appointment will be initially for one (1) year and continuation during that time period and renewal are based on satisfactory performance and availability of funding (limited to three (3) years). Expertise is required in the specific area of Drosophila genetics, circadian rhythms, and sleep. Applicants must have a Ph.D. or equivalent degree. Responsibilities may include working independently and as part of collaborative research groups studying the metabolic basis of sleep using genetic, cell biology and molecular biology techniques. The successful applicant will have an opportunity to develop independence in research through writing grants and peer-reviewed publications."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/165095": {
      "title": "Research Associate in the Academic Support Staff",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Dermatology Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Dermatology at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for several Research Associate positions in the Academic Support Staff. This appointment will be initially for one (1) year and continuation during that time period and renewal are based on satisfactory performance and availability of funding (limited to three (3) years). Applicants must have a Ph.D. degree. Responsibilities may include planning and executing experiments to study cell signaling and epigenetic mechanisms controlling development, regeneration, stem cell activity, and tumorigenesis of the epidermis and its appendages; generation and phenotypic analysis of genetic mouse models; use of genomics techniques including ChIP-seq, RNA-seq and ATAC-seq; presenting the results of experiments at laboratory meetings, departmental seminars, and national and international meetings. The successful applicant will have an opportunity to aide in the preparation of scientific papers; and supervising junior lab members including technicians and students."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/185356": {
      "title": "Research Associate in the Department of Neurosurgery at the University of Pennsylvania specializing in brain tumor biology and human genetics",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Neurosurgery Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Neurosurgery at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for several Research Associate positions in the Academic Support Staff. This appointment will be initially for one (1) year and continuation during that time period and renewal are based on satisfactory performance and availability of funding (limited to three (3) years). Expertise is required in the specific area of omics (transcriptomics and computational skills), immunologic assays, and animal experiments/creation of novel genetic models. Successful candidates will have a demonstrated track record of project completion and publications, as well as a high level of scientific rigor. Applicants must have a Ph.D. or M.D./Ph.D. degree. Preferred applicants must have 3-5 years of experience. Responsibilities may include working with the Principal Investigator on designing, carrying out, and analyzing complex experiments in the area of brain tumor biology as well as leading the implementation of experimental projects, design of the translational/basic science experiments to test hypotheses related to our research goals, using in vitro (cell culture/organoids), in vivo animal models, and primary patient samples. Preferred applicants will learn novel computational platforms required for analysis of single, bulk, and multiplatform chromatin, RNA, proteomic, and metabolic profiles and conduct thorough data analysis, manuscript preparation, and responsibilities related to grant preparation. The successful applicant will have an opportunity to to learn leadership roles related to the supervision of more junior lab members and ensure the day-to-day operations of the lab, organize lab meetings, and ensure that individual lab members are ready for their presentations at the weekly lab meetings. In addition, preferred applicants will have an opportunity to develop independence in research through writing grants and peer-reviewed publications. This position requires excellent communication and interpersonal skills as well as a highly motivated and independent thinker and worker, with a desire to work in a complex and high energy environment."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/184012": {
      "title": "Research Associate in the Department of Neurosurgery at the University of Pennsylvania specializing in Electrophysiology and Neuromodulation in Animal Models",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Neurosurgery Location: Philadelphia, PA The Philadelphia VA Medical Center (PVAMC) and the Department of Neurosurgery at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seek candidates for a Research Associate position in the Academic Support Staff. This appointment will be initially for one (1) year and continuation during that time period and renewal are based on satisfactory performance and availability of funding (limited to three (3) years). Expertise is required in the specific area of coding, EEG and signals analysis, as well as electrophysiology in animal models and neuromodulation. Applicants must have a Ph.D. or equivalent degree. Preferred applicant should have 3-5 years of experience in these areas of expertise. Responsibilities may include working with the Principal Investigator on designing, carrying out, and analyzing complex electrophysiological experiments in the area of cognition, TBI and PTE. The successful applicant will have an opportunity to develop independence in the areas of experimental design and analysis as well as preparation of grants and manuscripts."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/164270": {
      "title": "Research Associate Position - Division of Rheumatology",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Department of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Medicine - Rheumatology Location: Philadelphia, PA The Division of Rheumatology in the Department of Medicine at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for a Research Associate position in the Academic Support Staff. This appointment will be initially for one (1) year and continuation during that time period and renewal are based on satisfactory performance and availability of funding (limited to three (3) years). Expertise is required in the specific area of antigen-specific CD4+ T cell analysis using peptide-MHC multimers, protein biochemistry, and spectral flow cytometry. Five years of postdoctoral experience in human immunology is required at the start of this position. Applicants must have a Ph.D. degree. Responsibilities may include the ability to lead projects and work cooperatively with others, carry out and troubleshoot experiments, and assist in manuscript and grant writing. Previous computational and wet-lab experience is required. The successful applicant will have an opportunity to collaborate on grant proposals and journal publications, participate in the department's educational programs as assigned, and mentor/teach junior lab members. The selected candidate will work in an interdisciplinary environment with experts in immunology, autoimmunity, and statistical modeling. The successful applicant will be self-motivated and have a strong background in standard molecular and biological techniques, cell culture, immunological assays, and recombinant protein production."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/185531": {
      "title": "Research Associate Position - Lazar laboratory",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Department of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Medicine - Endocrinology, Diabetes and Metabolism Location: Philadelphia, PA The Division of Endocrinology, Diabetes and Metabolism in the Department of Medicine at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for a Research Associate position in the Academic Support Staff. This appointment will be initially for one (1) year and continuation during that time period and renewal are based on satisfactory performance and availability of funding (limited to three (3) years). Applicants must have a Ph.D. degree. Position requires a minimum of three years of postdoctoral research experience. Responsibilities may include experiments related to determining mechanisms underlying the functions of thyroid hormone receptors in cells and in vivo by developing novel methods of gene transcription and analysis in liver and other tissues. Applicant must have experience working with animal models of hypothyroidism and hyperthyroidism, and expertise in molecular biology techniques including ChIP-seq, single cell RNA-seq, ATAC-seq, and generation of mutant mice models. The longer-term goal is understanding mechanisms relating to diabetes, obesity, and their comorbidities including metabolic liver disease. The successful applicant will have an opportunity to develop a customized training experience in research that allows the individual to attain personal career objectives while developing a career in an academic setting. The Research Associate will be expected to collaborate with other members of the lab in their research areas and give their contributions."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/172358": {
      "title": "Research Associate Position - Margulies laboratory",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Department of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Medicine - Cardiovascular Medicine Location: Philadelphia, PA The Division of Cardiovascular Medicine in the Department of Medicine at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for several Research Associate positions in the Academic Support Staff. This appointment will be initially for one (1) year and continuation during that time period and renewal are based on satisfactory performance and availability of funding (limited to three (3) years). Applicants must have an M.D. or Ph.D degree. Responsibilities may include designing and executing studies focused on mechanobiology and myocardial tissue engineering, manuscript preparation, and development of applications for extramural funding. The successful applicant will have an opportunity to grow as an independent researcher in a collaborative environment with opportunities for mentorship. Selected candidates will be encouraged to interact and potentially form collaborative opportunities with fellow researchers within the field, as well as have opportunities to present data to the scientific community. Candidates will have at least 3 years of full-time post-doctoral research experience."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191396": {
      "title": "Research Associate Position in Kidney Genetics and Genomics",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Department of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Medicine - Renal-Electrolyte and Hypertension Location: Philadelphia, PA The Division of Renal-Electrolyte and Hypertension in the Department of Medicine at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for a Research Associate position in the Academic Support Staff. This appointment will be initially for one (1) year and continuation during that time period and renewal are based on satisfactory performance and availability of funding (limited to three (3) years). Expertise is required in the specific area of human genetics, genomics, genetic epidemiology, molecular genetics, or a closely related biomedical research field, with demonstrated research experience applying genetic and genomic approaches to human disease. Experience in kidney genetics and rare kidney disease research is preferred, including research involving nephrotic syndrome, focal segmental glomerulosclerosis, congenital kidney and urinary tract abnormalities, or related disorders. Experience with exome and/or genome sequencing, analysis and interpretation of genetic variants, and functional characterization of disease-associated variants is also preferred. Applicants must have a Ph.D. degree. Responsibilities may include leading research projects directed toward the discovery and characterization of rare genetic variants predisposing to rare kidney diseases, including congenital anomalies of the kidney and urinary tract, ADAMTS13-related diseases, and nephrotic syndrome. Responsibilities will include conducting functional studies in cellular and vertebrate disease models; utilizing DNA microarrays, exome and genome sequencing technologies, mouse models, and transcriptomic, epigenomic, and proteomic approaches; analyzing and interpreting complex genomic and molecular data; providing scientific leadership for research projects; supervising laboratory technicians, junior bioinformaticians, and students; presenting research findings at national and international scientific meetings; and publishing results in peer-reviewed scientific journals. The successful applicant will have an opportunity to advance their expertise in kidney genetics, genomics, and rare kidney disease research within a highly collaborative academic research environment. The successful applicant will have opportunities to lead multidisciplinary research projects, expand expertise in genomic and multi-omic approaches, conduct functional studies of disease-associated genetic variants, mentor junior research staff and trainees, collaborate with investigators across Penn Medicine, and disseminate findings through peer-reviewed publications and presentations at national and international scientific meetings. The successful candidate will join the Division of Renal-Electrolyte and Hypertension in the Department of Medicine at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania and participate in a collaborative research program focused on understanding the genetic and molecular mechanisms underlying rare kidney diseases."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/171565": {
      "title": "Research Associate position in Nephrology",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Department of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Medicine - Renal-Electrolyte and Hypertension Location: Philadelphia, PA The Division of Renal-Electrolyte and Hypertension in the Department of Medicine at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for several Research Associate positions in the Academic Support Staff. This appointment will be initially for one (1) year and continuation during that time period and renewal are based on satisfactory performance and availability of funding (limited to three (3) years). Expertise is required in the specific area of human genetics, including statistical genetics, computational biology, pharmacogenetics/genomics, functional genomics, genome editing, structural variation analysis, kidney genetics and familiarity with studies addressing translational genomic medicine. Applicants must have a Ph.D. or M.D./Ph.D. degree. Responsibilities may include bioinformatics analysis of genetic, genomics, epigenetic datasets associated with kidney disease development. Working with collaborators and being part of the Penn/CHOP Kidney Innovation Center to facilitate collaborative efforts. The successful applicant will have an opportunity to receive mentorship in genetics, genomics and bioinformatics. The applicant will be responsible for primary data analysis and publish and present results. Mentorship will also be provided for grant application and independent faculty position application."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193866": {
      "title": "Research Associate position, Division of Endocrinology, Diabetes, and Metabolism",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Department of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Medicine - Endocrinology, Diabetes and Metabolism Location: Philadelphia, PA The Division of Endocrinology, Diabetes and Metabolism in the Department of Medicine at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for a Research Associate position in the Academic Support Staff. This appointment will be initially for one (1) year and continuation during that time period and renewal are based on satisfactory performance and availability of funding (limited to three (3) years). Expertise is required in the specific area of nuclear receptor signaling, including PPARα and HNF4α, hepatic lipid metabolism, genetic regulation of metabolism, circadian biology, epigenetic and transcriptomic data analysis, and metabolic disease research using genetically engineered mouse models and advanced genomic approaches. Applicants must have a Ph.D. degree. Responsibilities may include conducting independent and collaborative research on the role of nuclear receptors, including PPARα and HNF4α, in hepatic lipid metabolism and metabolic disease; analyzing epigenomic and transcriptomic datasets generated from genetically engineered mouse models; investigating mechanisms underlying circadian regulation in the liver and the role of the hepatic vagus nerve in maintaining circadian rhythms; contributing to experimental design, data interpretation, manuscript preparation, and presentation of research findings; and collaborating with laboratory members on related research projects. The successful applicant will have an opportunity to develop advanced expertise in metabolic disease research, genomic and epigenomic data analysis, circadian biology, and nuclear receptor signaling. The position provides training and mentorship in experimental design, data interpretation, scientific communication, and collaborative research, supporting the individual's long-term career development in academic biomedical research."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/164884": {
      "title": "Research Associate Position, Division of Hematology-Oncology",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Department of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Medicine - Hematology/Oncology Location: Philadelphia, PA The Division of Hematology-Oncology in the Department of Medicine at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for several Research Associate positions in the Academic Support Staff. This appointment will be initially for one (1) year and continuation during that time period and renewal are based on satisfactory performance and availability of funding (limited to three (3) years). Expertise is required in the specific area of hematology/oncology. Applicants must have a Ph.D. degree. Responsibilities may include the ability to lead projects and work cooperatively with others, carry out and troubleshoot experiments, and assist in manuscript and grant writing. Building relationships with supervisors, collaborators, and co-workers are also important in this position. The successful applicant will have an opportunity to work on the interface of basic and translational research and develop the ability to handle research projects independently at intellectual as well as technical levels."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/181297": {
      "title": "Research Associate Position, Pulmonary, Allergy & Critical Care Division - Beers Lab",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Department of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Medicine - Pulmonary, Allergy and Critical Care Location: Philadelphia, PA The Division of Pulmonary, Allergy and Critical Care in the Department of Medicine at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for several Research Associate positions in the Academic Support Staff. This appointment will be initially for one (1) year and continuation during that time period and renewal are based on satisfactory performance and availability of funding (limited to three (3) years). Applicants must have an M.D. or Ph.D. or M.D./Ph.D. degree. Responsibilities may include scientific research in the field of pulmonary medicine with a focus on experimental design and implementation and preparation of scientific papers, grants writing and grants proposals. The successful applicant will have an opportunity to characterize alveolar type II biology and dysfunction in model of pulmonary fibrosis. Candidates will have demonstrated excellent qualifications in research. This position requires expertise in murine modeling of pulmonary fibrosis, as demonstrated by publication record. The candidate must have previous experience with bioinformatics, from the generation of samples, processing of raw data, and the production of publication quality visual representations. A significant goal of this position is the translation of novel epithelial and mesenchymal populations in mice to the relevant equivalent in human models. To accomplish this the candidate must also have previous experience with flow cytometry and primary cell isolation from human lungs. This is a mentored position in the lab space a senior member of the division with preferred candidates having opportunities to prepare manuscripts, mentor students, and write grants."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/184409": {
      "title": "Research Associate – Causal",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Department of Biostatistics, Epidemiology and Informatics Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Biostatistics and Epidemiology at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for several Research Associate positions in the Academic Support Staff. This appointment will be initially for one (1) year and continuation during that time period and renewal are based on satisfactory performance and availability of funding (limited to three (3) years). Applicants must have a Ph.D. or equivalent degree. Responsibilities may include : (1) development and application of statistical and machine learning methods for analyzing electronic health records (EHRs) data, claims data, and biobank data; (2) manage the development and documentation and technical support of industry grade open access software implementing state-of-the-art causal inference methods developed by researchers at the Penn Center for Causal Inference; and (3) collaboration on cutting-edge translational biomedical research with CCI trainees and other researchers working with the Penn School of Medicine and Children’s Hospital of Philadelphia. The successful applicant will have an opportunity to work under the supervision of PI Dr. Eric Tchetgen Tchetgen, with a focus on methodological developments for Causal Inference and related Missing Data Problems. The candidate is expected to build a clear path towards an independent researcher in preparation for the next stage of their career."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/177926": {
      "title": "Research Associate – Epidemiology",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Department of Biostatistics, Epidemiology and Informatics Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Biostatistics and Epidemiology at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for several Research Associate positions in the Academic Support Staff. This appointment will be initially for one (1) year and continuation during that time period and renewal are based on satisfactory performance and availability of funding (limited to three (3) years). Expertise is required in the specific area of longitudinal causal inference methods and/or the design, evaluation, and application of research methods, as well as a specific area of epidemiology, (bio)statistics, or a related field of quantitative or computational science. Applicants must have at least one year of postdoctoral experience. Applicants must have a Ph.D. or equivalent degree. Responsibilities may include : 1.\tDevelopment and application of statistical, causal, and machine learning methods for analyzing complex longitudinal data structures, including electronic health records (EHRs), registry data, biobanks, pragmatic trials and cohort studies; 2.\tCollaboration on research projects across the palliative care, critical care, and health services research spectrum, working alongside faculty in the Department and a dynamic team of clinician-scientists, epidemiologists, and biostatisticians; and 3.\tSupport for methodological development in clinical trials design, analysis, and monitoring; observational studies; and Bayesian causal inference applications. The successful applicant will have an opportunity to work under the supervision of Dr. Michael Harhay and collaborators at the Center for Clinical Trials Innovation, with increasing autonomy to independently lead innovative methodological developments (particularly Bayesian components of funded projects) and author peer-reviewed publications. Collaborative contributions to team science are expected alongside building a clear path toward independent research in preparation for the next stage of their career."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194627": {
      "title": "Research Associate – Yim Lab",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Systems Pharmacology and Translational Therapeutics Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Systems Pharmacology and Translational Therapeutics at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for a Research Associate position in the Academic Support Staff. This appointment will be initially for one (1) year and continuation during that time period and renewal are based on satisfactory performance and availability of funding (limited to three (3) years). Expertise is required in the specific area of In vivo CRISPR targeting, Flow cytometry and General molecular cell biology techniques. Applicants must have a Ph.D. or equivalent degree in Biological Sciences. Responsibilities may include developing a vigorous research program focused on neuroimmunology and neurodevelopment projects. Interacting and training technicians and graduate students. Assisting with the submission of grant applications/contracts and funding of research projects. Will also participate in free discussion, research progress, and overall morale. The successful applicant will have an opportunity to primarily carry out a research program in Dr. Yeong Shin Yim’s lab. The position will allow the candidate to grow as an independent scientist and gain eligibility to apply for funding through different mechanisms. Also, successful candidates will benefit from the thriving and collegial Penn research community and tailored career development guidance."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/167762": {
      "title": "Research Associate, Gynecologic Oncology at the Penn Ovarian Cancer Research Center",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Obstetrics and Gynecology Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for a Research Associate position in the Academic Support Staff. This appointment will be initially for one (1) year and continuation during that time period and renewal are based on satisfactory performance and availability of funding (limited to three (3) years). Expertise is required in the specific area of ovarian cancer biology, biomarker development, animal studies, and genetic screens. A strong background in molecular biology, drug development, mouse models, and cancer genomics is required. Applicants must have a Ph.D. degree. Specifically, a Ph.D. in Cell and Molecular Biology and 5 years of relevant postdoctoral training are required. Must have demonstrated prior certification for: human subjects research, institutional animal care and use committee (IACUC) approval to work with animals, viral vector use, and recombinant DNA technology. Responsibilities may include planning, organizing, and executing in vivo and in vitro experiments; data analysis; securing approval from regulatory authorities; writing grants, progress reports, and manuscripts for publication and presentation; and participating in Center activities. The successful applicant will have an opportunity to conduct research while working directly with investigators in the Penn Ovarian Cancer Research Center, presenting research findings at national meetings, mentoring undergraduate and graduate students, and having input on the direction of the science. We seek candidates who embrace and reflect diversity in the broadest sense. The University of Pennsylvania is an EOE. Minorities/women/individuals with disabilities/protected veterans are encouraged to apply."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/175181": {
      "title": "Research Associate-Orthopaedic Surgery",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Orthopaedic Surgery Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Orthopaedic Surgery at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for a Research Associate position in the Academic Support Staff. This appointment will be initially for one (1) year and continuation during that time period and renewal are based on satisfactory performance and availability of funding (limited to three (3) years). Expertise is required in the specific area of tissue engineering, biomaterials, mechanical testing, cartilage/meniscus, large animal models, and biomaterials synthesis. Applicants must have a Ph.D. degree in Biomedical Engineering (or equivalent) and postdoctoral experience in research fields related to regenerative medicine, tissue engineering, and/or translational animal models. Strong written and oral communication skills are essential, including a demonstrated ability to published research results in peer-reviewed journal articles. Responsibilities may include scientific research using in vitro and in vivo models for a variety of cartilage and meniscus tissue engineering approaches, and more generally across the musculoskeletal system. The application is expected to submit and collaborate on grant proposals and journal publications, participate in the department’s educational programs as assigned, and mentor and teach junior lab members. The successful applicant will have an opportunity to work in an interdisciplinary environment with experts in engineering, biology, and medicine. We are seeking candidates with research interests and expertise in musculoskeletal tissue engineering and translational animal models, with a specific focus on cartilage and/or meniscus."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193197": {
      "title": "Section Chief, Division of Hematology-Oncology, Pennsylvania Hospital - Academic Clinician",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Department of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Medicine - Hematology/Oncology Location: Philadelphia, PA The Division of Hematology-Oncology in the Department of Medicine at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for an Associate or Full Professor position in the non-tenure academic clinician track. Expertise is required in the specific area of hematology/oncology. Applicants must have an M.D. or equivalent degree. Candidates must be board certified or board eligible in medical oncology and/or hematology. Teaching responsibilities may include teaching hematology/oncology fellows, house staff and medical students. The academic clinician track requires 100 credits per year of active and high-quality teaching. Clinical responsibilities may include care of patients with solid tumors, hematologic malignancies, or classical hematologic disorders. Patients will be seen in an outpatient and inpatient setting at Pennsylvania Hospital (PAH). Candidates will have strong administrative, clinical, and teaching skills, and a commitment to the missions of the division, fostering outstanding patient care, clinical operations, safe and efficient infusion operations, clinical research, program development, and coordination across practice locations. Pennsylvania Hospital offers many advanced therapies including CAR-T therapy, bloodless medicine, and autologous hematopoietic cell transplantation in a FACT certified program. The section chief will lead the growing Division of Hematology-Oncology at Pennsylvania Hospital, while building on a strong foundation of excellence in patient care, education and clinical research. Leadership responsibilities will include coordinating and integrating the core mission areas within the Division of Hematology-Oncology and in coordination with the Abramson Cancer Center. Rank will be commensurate with experience."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/186780": {
      "title": "Senior Rank - Academic Clinician - Surgical Retina",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Ophthalmology Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Ophthalmology at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for an Associate or Full Professor position in the non-tenure academic clinician track. Applicants must have an M.D. or equivalent degree. Rank will be commensurate with experience. Teaching responsibilities may include teaching residents, students, and fellows at the Scheie Eye Institute and the Philadelphia VA. In addition, mentorship of junior faculty members, as well as trainees, is expected. Clinical responsibilities may include providing medical and surgical retina care for patients at the Scheie Eye Institute, the Philadelphia VA, and one of Scheie's satellite offices."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/186775": {
      "title": "Senior Rank - Tenure or Clinician Educator - Retina Surgeon",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Ophthalmology Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Ophthalmology at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for several Associate and Full Professor positions in either the non-tenure clinician educator track or the tenure track. Applicants must have an M.D. or equivalent degree.  Rank will be commensurate with experience. Teaching responsibilities may include teaching residents, students, and fellows at Scheie. In addition, mentorship of junior faculty members, as well as trainees, is expected. Clinical responsibilities may include providing medical and surgical retina care for patients at Scheie and one of Scheie's satellite offices. Research or scholarship responsibilities may include collaborative or independent research within the specific area of retina. While both tenure and clinician-educator faculty conduct methodological and collaborative research, tenure track faculty focus primarily on independent, investigator-driven research, while clinician educator-track focus primarily on collaboration."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/183308": {
      "title": "Shoulder & Elbow Surgeon/Assistant Professor-AC Track",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Orthopaedic Surgery Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Orthopaedic Surgery at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for an Assistant Professor position in the non-tenure academic clinician track. Expertise is required in the specific area of Orthopaedic Shoulder & Elbow Surgery. Applicants must have an M.D. degree and must be Board certified or Board eligible. Fellowship training in Shoulder & Elbow is required. Teaching responsibilities may include resident, medical student and fellow education in Shoulder & Elbow surgery. Clinical responsibilities include shoulder and elbow practice in the PPMC Medical Center and on call responsibilities."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/167337": {
      "title": "Tenure Track Faculty Position in Medicine - Junior rank",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Department of Medicine Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Medicine at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for several Assistant Professor positions in the tenure track. Applicants must have an M.D. or Ph.D. or M.D./Ph.D. degree. ABIM board certified/eligible in Internal Medicine or its specialties is required. Teaching responsibilities may include teaching students, residents, and/or fellows while engaged in clinical activities or serving as lecturer for divisional and/or school educational programs. Clinical responsibilities may include working in the outpatient clinics and as an attending on the inpatient service within the hospitals at the University of Pennsylvania Health System and/or Corporal Michael J. Crescenz VA Medical Center (CMC VAMC). Research or scholarship responsibilities may include any aspect of basic science, clinical, translational, health policy, or health services research. The successful candidate should demonstrate a productive track record during post-doctoral research training with an ability to perform collaborative interdisciplinary science. As a new faculty member, the successful applicant will be expected to establish an innovative, independent research program that will attract and maintain extramural funding from government agencies, private foundations, and/or industry. Selected applicants will hold an academic appointment in the Department of Medicine within the division of his/her specialty."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/167336": {
      "title": "Tenure Track Faculty Position in Medicine - Senior ranks",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Department of Medicine Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Medicine at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for several Associate or Full Professor positions in the tenure track. Applicants must have an M.D. or Ph.D. or M.D./Ph.D. degree. ABIM certification in Internal Medicine or its specialties is required. Teaching responsibilities may include teaching students, residents, and/or fellows while engaged in clinical activities and/or serving as lecturer for divisional and/or school educational programs. Clinical responsibilities may include working in the outpatient clinics and as an attending on the inpatient service within the hospitals at the University of Pennsylvania Health System and/or Corporal Michael J. Crescenz VA Medical Center (CMC VAMC). Research or scholarship responsibilities may include any aspect of basic science, clinical, translational, health policy, or health services research. The successful candidate will be expected to establish an innovative, independent research program that will attract and maintain extramural funding from government agencies, private foundations, and/or industry. There will be an expectation of the ability of the candidate to serve in management and/or leadership roles. Rank will be commensurate with experience. Selected applicants will hold an academic appointment in the Department of Medicine within the division of his/her specialty."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/187680": {
      "title": "Tenure Track Faculty Position, Assistant Professor – Pulmonary, Allergy and Critical Care Division",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Department of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Medicine - Pulmonary, Allergy and Critical Care Location: Philadelphia, PA The Division of Pulmonary, Allergy and Critical Care in the Department of Medicine at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for an Assistant Professor position in the tenure track. Applicants must have an M.D./Ph.D degree. Selected candidates will be board certified/eligible in Pulmonary Medicine and/or Critical Care Medicine. Teaching responsibilities may include teaching within the health system and/or VA system, as well as trainee mentorship and participation as an active member of Penn graduate programs based on the candidate’s research focus. Clinical responsibilities may include care for patients at the outpatient clinics and/or as an attending on the inpatient service. Research or scholarship responsibilities may include establishing an innovative, independent research program that will attract and maintain extramural funding from various sources. Selected candidates will demonstrate a productive track record with an ability to perform fundamental lung biology research. Candidates with a research program focused on pulmonary vascular biology or airways biology are particularly encouraged to apply."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/161019": {
      "title": "Tenure Track Faculty Position, Renal, Electrolyte and Hypertension Division - Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Department of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Medicine - Renal-Electrolyte and Hypertension Location: Philadelphia, PA The Division of Renal-Electrolyte and Hypertension in the Department of Medicine at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for several Assistant Professor positions in the tenure track. Expertise is required in the specific area of investigation of mechanisms of kidney disease. The successful candidate will be expected to develop, grow and maintain an independently funded research effort focused in this area. The ideal candidate will have completed rigorous training, will have evidence of outstanding scientific productivity, and will have demonstrated success as an independent investigator. Applicants must have an M.D. or M.D./Ph.D. or equivalent degree. Exceptional Ph.D. (or equivalent degree) applicants with a focus on kidney-related science will also be considered. Physician scientist candidates should be board certified/eligible in Nephrology. Teaching responsibilities may include teaching medical students, residents, or fellows, and participating in graduate student teaching within one or more inter-departmental, interdisciplinary graduate groups of the Biomedical Graduate Studies (BGS) program. Clinical responsibilities for candidates who are physician scientists may include patient care on both inpatient consultative services and in outpatient nephrology clinical at the Hospital of the University of Pennsylvania. Research or scholarship responsibilities may include studying the molecular and cellular basis of immunology, physiology, cell biology, metabolism, genetics or pathology, or applying modern experimental or discovery approaches to problems of kidney disease. Appropriate resources will be made available to accelerate the applicant's research program. We seek candidates who embrace and reflect diversity in the broadest sense. The University of Pennsylvania is an EOE. Minorities/women/individuals with disabilities/protected veterans are encouraged to apply."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/188953": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Assistant Professor of Neuroscience",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Neuroscience Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Neuroscience at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for an Assistant Professor position in the tenure track. Expertise is required in basic neuroscience. Applicants must have a Ph.D. or equivalent degree. Teaching responsibilities may include participating in medical school basic science education as well as graduate training through affiliation with one or more of the inter-departmental, interdisciplinary graduate groups of the Biomedical Graduate Studies (BGS) program. Research or scholarship responsibilities may include developing an independent research program, synergistic with the scientific interests of one or more research institutes and centers, and other laboratories in the department, school, and university. Special attention will be given to those who incorporate innovative machine-learning-based approaches in their studies or apply human cell-based models for mechanistic studies. Highly competitive lab space and start-up funds are available for successful candidates. The Department of Neuroscience is embedded within a vibrant and highly collaborative research environment on the Penn campus, including the Mahoney Institute for Neurosciences (MINS), which brings together more than 200 labs spanning the full breadth of neuroscience research and fosters collaborative and multidisciplinary approaches to advance our understanding of the nervous system. Faculties in the Department of Neuroscience enjoy extensive collaborations across basic science and clinical departments, including Neurosurgery, Neurology, and Psychiatry, and are members of many interdisciplinary institutes and centers within the Penn and Children's Hospital of Philadelphia (CHOP) community, such as the Lurie Autism Institute, the Institute for Regenerative Medicine, the Penn Epigenetics Institute, the Chronobiology and Sleep Institute, the Institute for RNA Innovation, and the Institute for Translational Medicine & Therapeutics. Together, these integrative programs provide exceptional opportunities for innovative, cross-disciplinary research, and scientific discovery. For full consideration, applications should be received by October 15, 2026; however, applications will continue to be accepted until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/181423": {
      "title": "Thoracic Imaging - Academic Clinician - Junior Rank",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Radiology Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Radiology at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for several Assistant Professor positions in the non-tenure academic clinician track. Expertise is required in the specific area of thoracic imaging. Applicants must have an M.D. or M.D./Ph.D. or equivalent degree. Applicants must be American Board of Radiology certified or eligible with sub-specialty training in Cardiothoracic Radiology. Teaching responsibilities may include daily clinical supervision of residents and cardiothoracic imaging fellows and lecturing to medical students, radiology residents, fellows, and trainees from other departments. Clinical responsibilities may include interpretation of chest radiographs, thoracic CT, and thoracic MRI. Performance of thoracic needle biopsies is preferred. There is an option to participate in the cardiovascular CT and MRI clinical practice. The successful candidate will also participate in shared after-hours and weekend coverage. Penn Radiology provides clinical services at the Hospital of the University of Pennsylvania, Penn Presbyterian Medical Center, Pennsylvania Hospital, the Philadelphia Veterans Affairs Medical Center, and affiliated outpatient imaging centers in Pennsylvania, New Jersey, and Delaware."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/181152": {
      "title": "Thoracic Imaging - Academic Clinician - Senior Ranks",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Radiology Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Radiology at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for several Associate or Full Professor positions in the non-tenure academic clinician track. Expertise is required in the specific area of thoracic imaging. Applicants must have an M.D. or M.D./Ph.D. or equivalent degree. Applicants must be American Board of Radiology certified or eligible with sub-specialty training in Cardiothoracic Radiology. Teaching responsibilities may include daily clinical supervision of residents and cardiothoracic imaging fellows and lecturing to medical students, radiology residents, fellows, and trainees from other departments. The successful candidate will be expected to serve in an educational leadership role within the division. Clinical responsibilities may include interpretation of chest radiographs, thoracic CT and thoracic MRI. Performance of thoracic needle biopsies is preferred. There is an option to participate in the cardiovascular CT and MRI clinical practice. The successful candidate will also participate in shared after hour and weekend coverage. Penn Radiology provides clinical services for the Hospital of the University of Pennsylvania, Penn Presbyterian Medical Center, Pennsylvania Hospital, Philadelphia Veteran Affairs Medical Center, and affiliated outpatient imaging centers located in Pennsylvania and New Jersey. This position requires a minimum of 3-5 years experience.  Rank will be commensurate with experience."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/171558": {
      "title": "Transplant Nephrologist at the Hospital of the University of Pennsylvania - Junior rank",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Department of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Medicine - Renal-Electrolyte and Hypertension Location: Philadelphia, PA The Division of Renal-Electrolyte and Hypertension in the Department of Medicine at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for several Assistant Professor positions in the non-tenure academic clinician track. Expertise is required in the specific area of evaluation and managing kidney transplant patients. Applicants must have an M.D. or equivalent degree. Applicants must have additional training and expertise in transplant nephrology. Teaching responsibilities may include teaching Penn medical students, residents, and fellows. The academic clinician track requires 100 credits per year of active and high-quality teaching responsibilities. Applicants should be outstanding educators. Clinical responsibilities may include providing clinical care for patients on the inpatient and outpatient services as a transplant nephrologist at the Hospital of the University of Pennsylvania."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193182": {
      "title": "University of Pennsylvania Assistant Professor of Neurology - AC Track",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Neurology Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Neurology at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for several Assistant Professor positions in the non-tenure academic clinician track. Expertise is required in the specific area of Headache Neurology. Applicants must have an M.D. or M.D./Ph.D. or equivalent degree. Applicants must be Board Eligible in Neurology. Completion of Neurology residency and headache fellowship in the USA is required. Teaching responsibilities may include teaching medical students, residents and headache fellows in the outpatient clinic and instructing clinical residents, fellows in allied specialties such as ENT, pain management, internal medicine, and family medicine. Teaching didactics in the resident curriculum and in the preclinical Brain and Behavior course also are provided. Clinical responsibilities may include maintaining personal clinics at Perelman Center for Advanced Medicine, which would include clinical visits and procedural visits. Faculty are also expected to provide attending coverage for resident clinics at HUP. Participation in clinical trial recruitment, CME activities, and quality improvement initiatives is encouraged."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192413": {
      "title": "University of Pennsylvania Nephrology Faculty Position with the Philadelphia (CMC) VA Medical Center",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Department of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Medicine - Renal-Electrolyte and Hypertension Location: Philadelphia, PA The Philadelphia VA Medical Center (PVAMC) and the Division of Renal-Electrolyte and Hypertension in the Department of Medicine at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seek candidates for an Assistant Professor position in the non-tenure academic clinician track. Applicants must have an M.D. or equivalent degree. Applicants should be board certified in Nephrology. Teaching responsibilities may include teaching Penn medical students, residents, and clinical fellows. Applicants should be outstanding clinicians with demonstrated excellence in patient care and education. The academic clinician track requires 100 credits per year of active and high-quality teaching. Clinical responsibilities may include providing clinical care in both inpatient and outpatient settings as a general nephrology consultant, in a freestanding VA outpatient hemodialysis unit, and in an acute inpatient dialysis unit. A specific area of interest such as home dialysis, glomerular disease, or onco-nephrology is highly desirable. Participation in clinical research is encouraged but not required. This position may require split employment by the University of Pennsylvania and the Department of Veterans Affairs."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/171566": {
      "title": "University of Pennsylvania Nephrology Faculty Position, Academic Clinician - Senior ranks",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Department of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Medicine - Renal-Electrolyte and Hypertension Location: Philadelphia, PA The Division of Renal-Electrolyte and Hypertension in the Department of Medicine at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for several Associate or Full Professor positions in the non-tenure academic clinician track. Expertise is required in the specific area of care of patients with glomerular disease and additional training and expertise in transplant nephrology. Applicants must have an M.D. degree. Candidates should be board certified in Nephrology. Teaching responsibilities may include teaching Penn students, residents, and fellows. The academic clinician track required 100 credits per year of active and high-quality teaching. Applicants should be outstanding educators. Clinical responsibilities may include providing direct clinical inpatient and outpatient nephrology patient care at the Hospital of the University of Pennsylvania as a renal consultant, managing transplant patients and patients with glomerular diseases, nephrolithiasis, electrolyte disorders, complex hypertension, chronic kidney diseases, and acute kidney injury, and attending at an outpatient hemodialysis unit and inpatient dialysis unit. There will be an expectation of the ability of the candidate to provide management and/or leadership. Rank will be commensurate with experience."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/182017": {
      "title": "Urogynecologist, Assistant Professor of Ob/Gyn, Academic Clinician",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Obstetrics and Gynecology Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for an Assistant Professor position in the non-tenure academic clinician track. Expertise is required in the specific area of Urogynecology and Pelvic Reconstructive Surgery. Applicants must have an M.D. or equivalent degree. Candidate must be board certified or active candidate for certification by the American Board of Obstetrics and Gynecology and subspecialty trained and board certified or active candidate for certification in Urogynecology and Reconstructive Pelvic Surgery. Teaching responsibilities may include clinical training and supervision of medical students, residents and fellows and participation in formal teaching curriculum. Clinical responsibilities may include clinical and surgical treatment of women with the full range of pelvic floor disorders. Candidate would join established Urogynecology practice in Philadelphia and the surrounding counties. Excellent clinical and interpersonal skills are required. The successful candidate will enjoy fully-covered malpractice insurance, flexibility and a supportive environment that provides professional and personal fulfillment and career-long learning."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/170809": {
      "title": "Vascular Anomalies Assistant Professor, Academic Clinician, Pediatric Oncology",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Pediatrics: Perelman School of Medicine - Pediatrics - Oncology Location: Philadelphia, PA Children's Hospital of Philadelphia and the Division of Oncology in the Department of Pediatrics at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seek candidates for an Assistant Professor position in the non-tenure academic clinician track. Expertise is required in the specific area of clinical care and research directly related to patients with complex vascular anomalies and rare vascular tumors. Applicants must have an M.D. or equivalent degree. Board-certified or eligible in pediatric hematology/oncology. Teaching responsibilities may include didactic and clinical instruction of medical students, residents, medical fellows in pediatric hematology/oncology or related disciplines, and post-doctoral fellows. Clinical responsibilities may include Attending the interdisciplinary Comprehensive Vascular Anomalies Programs Clinic and some joint IR clinic as well as oncology clinic for Vascular anomaly patients and other rare tumors. Research opportunities may present themselves to contribute to a generation of new knowledge regarding care of patients with vascular anomalies, leadership and participation in QI projects, registries and SOP. Research is not required for faculty on the AC Track."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192706": {
      "title": "Vascular Anomalies Assistant Professor, Clinician Educator, Pediatric Oncology (CHOP)",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Pediatrics: Perelman School of Medicine - Pediatrics - Oncology Location: Philadelphia, PA Children's Hospital of Philadelphia and the Division of Oncology in the Department of Pediatrics at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seek candidates for an Assistant Professor position in the non-tenure clinician educator track. Expertise is required in the specific area of clinical care and research directly related to patients with complex vascular anomalies with an opportunity to play a leadership role in this program. Applicants must have an M.D. or M.D./Ph.D. or equivalent degree. Board-certified or eligible in pediatric hematology/oncology. Teaching responsibilities may include didactic and clinical instruction of medical students, graduate students, residents, medical fellows in pediatric hematology/oncology or related disciplines, and post-doctoral fellows. Clinical responsibilities may include inpatient and outpatient care focused on children, adolescents and young adults with complex vascular anomalies at the Children’s Hospital of Philadelphia, and active involvement in fellowship training. The successful candidate must be able to collaborate with members of a large interdisciplinary team. Research or scholarship responsibilities may include expanding translational and clinical research efforts in our Complex Vascular Anomalies Program with a particular interest in candidates with a focus on developing clinical trials testing innovative therapies for these disorders . The successful candidate will have an established record of peer reviewed scholarship and extramural funding."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/170835": {
      "title": "Vascular Anomalies Associate or Full Professor, Academic Clinician, Pediatric Oncology",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Pediatrics: Perelman School of Medicine - Pediatrics - Oncology Location: Philadelphia, PA Children's Hospital of Philadelphia and the Division of Oncology in the Department of Pediatrics at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seek candidates for an Associate or Full Professor position in the non-tenure academic clinician track. Expertise is required in the specific area of clinical care and research directly related to patients with complex vascular anomalies and rare vascular tumors. Applicants must have an M.D. or equivalent degree. Board-certified or eligible in pediatric hematology/oncology. Rank commensurate with experience. Teaching responsibilities may include didactic and clinical instruction of medical students, residents, medical fellows in pediatric hematology/oncology or related disciplines, and post-doctoral fellows. Clinical responsibilities may include attending the interdisciplinary Comprehensive Vascular Anomalies Programs Clinic and some joint IR clinic as well as oncology clinic for Vascular anomaly patients and other rare tumors. Research opportunities may present themselves to contribute to a generation of new knowledge regarding care of patients with vascular anomalies, leadership and participation in QI projects, registries and SOP. Research is not required for faculty on the AC Track."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192708": {
      "title": "Vascular Anomalies Associate Professor or Professor, Clinician Educator, Pediatric Oncology (CHOP)",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Pediatrics: Perelman School of Medicine - Pediatrics - Oncology Location: Philadelphia, PA Children's Hospital of Philadelphia and the Division of Oncology in the Department of Pediatrics at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seek candidates for an Associate or Full Professor position in the non-tenure clinician educator track. Expertise is required in the specific area of clinical care and research directly related to patients with complex vascular anomalies , with an opportunity to play a leadership role in this program . Applicants must have an M.D. or M.D./Ph.D. or equivalent degree. Board-certified or eligible in pediatric hematology/oncology. Rank will be commensurate with experience. Teaching responsibilities may include didactic and clinical instruction of medical students, graduate students, residents, medical fellows in pediatric hematology/oncology or related disciplines, and post-doctoral fellows. Clinical responsibilities may include inpatient and outpatient care focused on children, adolescents and young adults with complex vascular anomalies at the Children’s Hospital of Philadelphia, and active involvement in fellowship training. The successful candidate must be able to collaborate with members of a large interdisciplinary team. Research or scholarship responsibilities may include expanding translational and clinical research efforts in our Complex Vascular Anomalies Program with a particular interest in candidates with a focus on developing clinical trials testing innovative therapies for these disorders. The successful candidate will have an established record of peer reviewed scholarship and extramural funding."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/178166": {
      "title": "Vice Chair for Quality and Safety and Informatics Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Associated Faculty Penn Medicine",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Obstetrics and Gynecology Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for an Associate or Full Professor position in the non-tenure academic clinician track. Expertise is required in the specific area of quality and safety and informatics. The selected candidate will also serve as the Vice Chair for Quality, Safety and Informatics in the Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology. Applicants must have an M.D. or equivalent degree. Rank will be commensurate with experience. Teaching responsibilities may include an active role in the training of medical students, residents and fellows including participation in the formal curriculum. Clinical responsibilities may include inpatient and/or ambulatory care in obstetrics and gynecology or a subspecialty of Ob/Gyn depending on the candidate’s expertise and interests. The Department of Obstetrics & Gynecology has established a leadership role for a Vice-chair of Quality and Safety and Informatics to promote the development and implementation of clinical and educational programs designed to promote a culture of safety and continuous quality improvement, and related scholarly activity. We are seeking candidates who are dynamic and highly collaborative with strong leadership skills. The responsibilities of the Vice-Chair of Quality and Safety and Informatics include the following: • Oversee efforts to improve the culture of safety and continuous quality improvement • Work with the Women’s Health Clinical Effectiveness Team, Ob and Gyn QI committee chairs, and the OB Patient Safety Committee, faculty and residents to develop and implement quality and safety initiatives, and to develop clinical guidelines and pathways to improve the quality and delivery of care • Monitor quality metrics • Serve as an advisor to the division chiefs/program directors on quality improvement in the CPUP ambulatory practices and inpatient services • Assist the chairs at HUP and PAH with the development and implementation of risk reduction initiatives (RRI) • Assist the residency program director to facilitate educational programs on quality and safety (i.e. monthly Quality Forum, resident lecture series) • Promote and facilitate scholarship focused on quality, safety and health care delivery • Work with Penn Medicine’s chief medical officer and vice-president for quality and safety to ensure alignment of our goals and to support system-wide initiatives to improve quality and safety • Serve on the departmental claims review committee, executive committee, and Penn Ob/Gyn Simulation and Team Training Steering committee • Work with the OBGYN chair, CIO, the CMIO and AVP of IT Informatics to evolve the clinical practice of medicine and enhance translational research with the Divisions and the Department through optimized use of technology • Focus on developing strategies and optimizing the use of the EMR from the perspective of the end user • Provide semi-annual or quarterly updates as needed on new technologies • Lead implementation of major new technology initiatives in the health system • Maintain the scope and quality of the projects • Act as an escalation point for decision making • Work with Epic representatives, leadership team and the operational areas to ensure the application meets the organization's business and clinical needs • Ensure that the clinical and business communities are engaged in the project • Drive decisions through governance committees • Bring people to consensus across the organization The Vice-chair will receive assistance from the department’s Perinatal Safety Nurse, Senior Process Improvement Advisor and Lead Epic Clinical Analyst. The University of Pennsylvania's Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology is a highly collegial academic community, and faculty members are encouraged to engage in collaborative and interdisciplinary studies. Penn Medicine is a world class academic institution with superb clinical facilities and programs."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/159000": {
      "title": "Vice-Chief, Division of Genomic Diagnostics at the Children's Hospital of Philadelphia (CHOP)",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Pathology and Laboratory Medicine Location: Philadelphia, PA Children's Hospital of Philadelphia and the Department of Pathology and Laboratory Medicine at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seek candidates for an Associate or Full Professor position in the non-tenure clinician educator track. Applicants must have an M.D. and/or Ph.D degree. Molecular Genetic Pathology certification following Pathology residency, or American Board of Medical Genetics certification in Laboratory Genetics and Genomics, is required. Physicians must be eligible/licensed in the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania. Teaching responsibilities may include teaching fellows, residents, medical and graduate students, and staff in a variety of settings. Clinical responsibilities may include the development and implementation of tools for diagnostic and translational medicine, establishing and improving standards and methods for the interpretation of multi-omic data, assessing risk factors, and appropriate quantitative interpretation of data in clinical lab settings. Research or scholarship responsibilities may include work in the broad area of genomic diagnostics. We seek candidates with proven leadership skills and expertise in Genomic Diagnostics. The successful applicant will serve as Vice Chief of CHOP’s Division of Genomic Diagnostics (DGD), reporting to the Division Chief. The DGD encompasses cancer genomics, constitutional genomics and immunogenetics. With 14 faculty and 69 staff, the DGD offers a significant depth and breadth of clinical diagnostic tests including rapid genome, whole exome, gene panels, SNP arrays, cytogenetics, liquid biopsy and NGS-based HLA typing tests. As a key contributor to CHOP’s Omics and Big Data Initiative, the DGD plays an essential role in supporting the hospital’s precision medicine strategy. In addition, the Division’s faculty are engaged in a broad range of research and educational activities. The successful applicant will be expected to help lead with a clear vision for the future of genomic diagnostics, driving innovation by developing new tests and technologies and supporting clinical research initiatives. A key leadership responsibility will involve proactively building and fostering collaborations both within CHOP and with external academic and industry partners to expand the Division’s impact and capabilities working closely with the Division Chief and other key leaders at CHOP. We seek candidates who embrace and reflect diversity in the broadest sense. The University of Pennsylvania and Children’s Hospital of Philadelphia are EOEs. Minorities/women/individuals with disabilities/protected veterans are encouraged to apply."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195422": {
      "title": "Causal Inference Postdoctoral Researchers - Center for Causal Inference",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: Perelman School of Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania - Perelman School of Medicine Faculty Mentors: Drs. Ashkan Ertefaie,  Dylan Small, Dr. Enrique Schisterman, Dr. Aimin Chen, and Dr. Eric Tchetgen Tchetgen Department: Biostatistics Number of positions: 2 Open to applications from U.S. citizens and foreign nationals. The Center for Causal Inference (CCI) is a research center in the Department of Biostatistics, Epidemiology and Informatics at the University of Pennsylvania’s Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania with close collaboration with the Wharton Statistics and Data Science Department.  The CCI is a multidisciplinary center that includes faculty, postdocs, and graduate students from Biostatistics, Statistics, Epidemiology, Computer Science, Criminology, Philosophy, and other disciplines. The mission of the CCI is to be the leading center for research and training in the development and application of causal inference theory and methods, with far-ranging applications in Health and Social Sciences. The CCI is seeking two Postdoctoral Researchers in Causal Inference. The positions offer considerable flexibility in research focus and opportunities to collaborate with faculty affiliated with the CCI on methodological and applied research. Position 1 will be jointly mentored by Drs. Ashkan Ertefaie and Dylan Small . Potential research areas include: · Developing methods to evaluate the effects of antidiabetic treatments on recurrent hospitalizations for T2DM-related complications. · Developing methods for constructing just-in-time adaptive interventions using mobile health (mHealth) data. · Developing novel causal inference methods to reconcile potentially conflicting findings across randomized trials and observational studies, particularly in pregnancy research. Position 2 is a joint position with CCI and the Center for Health Innovations in Reproductive and Perinatal Population Research (CHIRP) and will be mentored by Drs. Enrique Schisterman, Aimin Chen, and Eric Tchetgen Tchetgen . Potential research areas include: · Developing methods for measuring exposures below the limit of detection, with applications in reproductive and environmental epidemiology. Both researchers will receive professional development mentorship from CCI Co-Directors Nandita Mitra and Eric Tchetgen Tchetgen, as well as project-specific mentorship designed to support their career development. Responsibilities: Postdoctoral researchers will develop new theory and methods in causal inference; develop software; and potentially collaborate on applied projects in Health and Social Sciences. In addition, the fellow is expected to be an active member of the CCI, by, for example, participating in the weekly working group meeting and the annual Causal Inference Summer Institute. Candidates should hold a doctoral degree in biostatistics, statistics, or a related field. Strong computational skills and expertise in causal inference are preferred but not required. Start date and term are negotiable. Applicants should submit a cover letter, CV, Research Statement (2 pages max), and contact information for three references."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191056": {
      "title": "Liver Cancer and Ultrasound Imaging Postdoc Fellowship",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: Perelman School of Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania – Perelman School of Medicine Description Mentor: Chandra Sehgal, PhD Hiring Department: Radiology Funding Source: NIH Number of positions: 1 U.S. Citizens and foreign nationals may apply Project overview The ultrasound research lab is developing hydralazine-augmented contrast ultrasound (HyCUS), a novel functional imaging technique that exploits differences in tumor versus liver vasculature to improve early detection of hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC), the fastest-growing cause of cancer death in the U.S. Our preliminary data show HyCUS more than triples lesion visibility compared to standard B-mode and contrast-enhanced ultrasound, and detects small HCC lesions missed entirely by current clinical methods. We are seeking a motivated postdoctoral fellow to help translate this promising discovery from bench to bedside. Research Activity The candidate will lead validation studies in an autochthonous rat model of HCC with liver fibrosis, working across three interconnected aims: establishing the biological basis of HyCUS, defining an optimal hydralazine dosing regimen, and benchmarking HyCUS against existing sonographic surveillance methods. The investigator will work at the intersection of cancer biology, vascular pharmacology, and noninvasive imaging. The position provides an opportunity to shape a translational imaging technology from preclinical proof of concept toward eventual clinical application. PhD, MD/PhD, or equivalent doctoral degree in Biomedical Engineering, Bioengineering, Chemical Engineering, Pharmacology, Imaging Sciences, or a related field. Background and interest in tumor biology, vascular physiology, or pharmacology Hands-on experience with animal models and histology is required Interest in contrast-enhanced ultrasound imaging Strong analytical and writing skills; interest in building an independent research trajectory We will provide training in any missing technical skills for motivated candidates. To apply, please submit the following through Interfolio: Brief statement of your research experience and interests Curriculum Vitae Name and contact information of three references."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189637": {
      "title": "Nanomedicine and Breast Cancer Imaging Postdoc Fellowship",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: Perelman School of Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania - Perelman School of Medicine The Cormode lab in the Radiology Department is seeking a highly motivated Postdoctoral Fellow to join an interdisciplinary research team developing next-generation nanoparticle contrast agents for the early detection of breast cancer. The successful candidate will contribute to an NIH-funded project focused on the translation of novel silver telluride nanoparticles (Ag₂Te-NP) for contrast-enhanced mammographic imaging in women with dense breasts. The project aims to advance a promising imaging technology toward clinical translation and eventual first-in-human studies. Project Overview Breast cancer remains a leading cause of cancer-related mortality among women. While mammography has significantly improved early detection, its sensitivity is reduced in women with dense breast tissue, who also face a substantially elevated risk of developing breast cancer. This project seeks to address this important clinical challenge by developing and clinically translating a novel nanoparticle-based contrast agent optimized for dual-energy mammography. Our laboratory has demonstrated that silver telluride nanoparticles provide substantially improved imaging performance compared with conventional iodinated contrast agents while exhibiting favorable clearance and safety characteristics. The current project will focus on scalable manufacturing and preclinical evaluation. Research Areas The fellow will work within a collaborative team that includes experts in nanomedicine, microfluidics, breast imaging, clinical radiology, regulatory science, and biostatistics. Research activities may include: Development and optimization of microfluidic synthesis methods for nanoparticle production. Physicochemical characterization of nanoparticle formulations. Evaluation of imaging performance using x-ray imaging, dual-energy mammography, and computed tomography (CT). In vitro studies of nanoparticle biocompatibility and performance. In vivo imaging, biodistribution, clearance, and safety studies. Quantitative image analysis and pharmacokinetic modeling. More information on the lab can be found here: https://hosting.med.upenn.edu/namil/ Candidates requiring visa sponsorship are welcome to apply. PhD, MD/PhD, or equivalent doctoral degree in Biomedical Engineering, Bioengineering, Chemical Engineering, Materials Science, Nanotechnology, Medical Physics, Chemistry, Pharmacology, Imaging Sciences, or a related field. Strong record of scientific productivity, demonstrated by peer-reviewed publications. Experience in one or more of the following areas: Nanoparticle synthesis and characterization Microfluidics Molecular or biomedical imaging Preclinical imaging Drug delivery or nanomedicine Biomaterials research Excellent written and oral communication skills. Applicants should submit a cover letter indicating their interest in the position, their CV, and the contact information of three references through Interfolio."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/188892": {
      "title": "Posdoctoral position in Cancer Biology / HPV Virology / Translational therapeutics",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: Perelman School of Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania, Perelman School of Medicine A postdoctoral position is available to work under the mentorship of Dr. Devraj Basu , Department of Otorhinolaryngology, at the interfaces among cancer biology, immunology, bioinformatics, and molecular epidemiology for HPV-related head and neck cancer. An interdisciplinary mentorship team for these positions includes multiple basic, translational, and clinical researchers who comprise a Translational Center of Excellence for HPV+ Head and Neck Cancer within the Abramson Cancer Center of the University of Pennsylvania. https://www.pennmedicine.org/physicians-hub/physician-article/hpv-opscc-transitional-center-of-excellence-debuts-at-penn-ent https://www.pennmedicine.org/research/translational-centers-of-excellence Trainees will have opportunities to lead projects designed to have major clinical impact on the following topics: (1) The role of heterogeneity in HPV oncoprotein expression within and among HPV+ oropharyngeal cancers in determining their therapy responses. (2) Developing novel targeted therapeutics that promote direct and immune-mediated clearance of HPV+ cancers (3) Developing prognostic and predictive molecular biomarkers for HPV-related cancer from comparative multi-omic analyses Advanced training in cancer biology is essential. Strong foundations in tumor virology, immunology, cellular metabolism, stem cell biology, data science and/or bioinformatics are also highly desirable. Positions will be supported by NIH and private foundation funding as well as philanthropic support. Send CV and cover letter detailing reasons for interest in this specific position through Interfolio. Names and contact information for 2 professional references are required. Also a  letter of recommendation from a recent direct supervisor is strongly encouraged."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/175741": {
      "title": "Post-Doctoral Fellow in Lung Biology",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: Perelman School of Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania Perelman School of Medicine  Mentor:  Michael F. Beers MD Department: Pulmonary and Critical Care Medicine Funding Sources: NIH, Departmnt of Veterans Affairs, and Endowment Open to US Citizens and Forign Nationals The Lung Epithelial Biology Laboratory at the Penn Lung Biology Institute (LBI), under the direction of Dr. Michael Beers, has an exceptional opportunity available for Postdoctoral Fellows to join a collaborative research project directed at unraveling the mysteries of idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis and other parenchytmal lung diseases. The Beers’ Lab has an existing program funded by NIH, the Department of Veterans Affairs, private foundations, and industry to study the molecular mechanisms that regulate tissue injury and repair in focused on discovery, target ID, target validation, and proof or principal studies aimed at uncovering and exploiting novel pathways to treat fibrotic lung diseases. The postdoctoral fellow can focus the project in any of several related areas (epithelial biology, immunological networks, metabolomics, epithelial-mesenchymal crosstalk, and/or transcriptomics/proteomics) using 2 novel published models of lung injury and fibrosis (see https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/29920187 ; https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/30910861 ; https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/30721158 . In addition to the unique mouse reagents, the successful candidate will have access to a variety of cutting edge technologies including RNAseq, single cell and ATAC RNAseq, high resolution proteomics, CRISPR mediated screens, lineage tracing, organoid assays, and iPS cells to identify pathways critical for lung tissue repair and regeneration. The ideal candidate will have an M.D., Ph.D. or MD/PhD with a strong background in basic molecular and cell biology skills. Experience utilizing mouse models and primary cell culture systems is required for the success of this position. Qualifications and Requirements PhD in Cell Biology, Biochemistry, Immunology, or related Biomedical Fields Solid background in molecular biology, biochemistry, genetics and cell biology Hands-on experience with animal models, mammalian tissue culture, molecular biology techniques and tissue imaging procedures are required Excellent record of publications in highly respected international scientific journals Familiarity with advanced imaging techniques, transcriptomic profiling, flow cytometry, and/or bioinformatics is desirable To apply for this position, a pplications should also be uploaded in Ineterfolio with the link Provided In addition please email mfbeers@pennmedicine.upenn.edu with subject line Postdoctoral Fellow Lung Biology with the following items attached: Current CV Cover letter"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189184": {
      "title": "Postdoc fellow – CRISPR screens in hematopoietic stem cells to study replication fitness",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: Perelman School of Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania, Perelman School of Medicine A postdoctoral fellow position is available at the University of Pennsylvania School of Medicine and the Children's Hospital of Philadelphia to perform state-of-the-art CRISPR/Cas9 screens to identify novel therapeutic targets for treating hematopoietic stem cell diseases. Dr. Tong’s laboratory studies cytokine receptor signaling in replication stress , DNA repair , and ubiquitin signaling in hematopoietic stem cells , bone marrow failure , and leukemia . Hematopoietic stem cells give rise to all circulating blood cells throughout life. Maintenance of stem cell self-renewal, proliferation, and differentiation is regulated by complex signaling pathways. Aberrant regulation leads to bone marrow failure or leukemia. Genome integrity plays a critical role in blood development and the prevention of leukemia and ageing. This postdoctoral fellow position will focus on studying the mechanisms that safeguard the genome during DNA replication and investigating how failures in these processes impact hematopoietic stem cell function, which leads to bone marrow failure and/or leukemia. CRISPR screens with our expert collaborators will delineate a new understanding of stem cell fitness upon replication stress and point to potentially druggable pathways for intervention in the treatment of diseases with stem cell dysregulation. Our recent publications include: Cell Stem Cell , 28(7):1275-1290, 2021; Journal of Clinical Investigation 136(1): e191713, 2026; Blood 138(22):2244-2255, 2021; Blood 133(14): 1560-1571, Apr 2019. Nature communications 9(1): 3915, Sep 2018; Genes & developm ent 31(10): 1007-1023, May 2017; You can find more of our recent publications and laboratory information in below website. https://www.med.upenn.edu/apps/faculty/index.php/g275/p8146029 Candidates should have a Ph.D. or equivalent degree and experience with investigating biochemistry, cell biology, and molecular biology, as well as mouse models. Highly motivated candidates with publication records are encouraged to apply. Please submit a brief statement of research experience and interest (cover letter), CV, and contact information for at least two references through interfolio. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis. Questions or additional follow up can be sent to: Wei Tong, Ph.D - wei.tong@pennmedicine.upenn.edu Professor and Distinguished Chair in Pediatrics, Children's Hospital of Philadelphia, University of Pennsylvania School of Medicine (UPENN), Co-Director, Hematopoietic Stem Cell program, Institute for Regenerative Medicine at UPENN Philadelphia, PA, USA"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/186048": {
      "title": "Postdoc fellows in cancer immunology and cardiovascular biology",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: Perelman School of Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania - Perelman School of Medicine The Fan Laboratory in the Department of Radiation Oncology at the University of Pennsylvania''s Perelman School of Medicine is recruiting creative, collaborative, and highly motivated Postdoctoral Scientists to lead research projects in cancer immunology and cardiovascular biology. Our lab investigates how the tissue microenvironment regulates cancer immunity and heart repair. We are particularly interested in: The molecular mechanisms underlying vascular control of immune cell function in cancer and ischemic heart disease; Targeting myeloid cells to overcome cancer-associated immunosuppression; The biology and therapeutic targeting of γδT cell and NKT cell exhaustion in cancer; Advanced radiation strategies, including FLASH and SFRT, to modulate tissue immunity in tumors and normal hearts. Our research integrates mouse models, quantitative molecular and cell biology, immunology, metabolism, and multi-omics approaches—including transcriptomics, epigenomics, and proteomics—as well as advanced 3D imaging to uncover molecular pathways and develop novel therapeutic strategies. https://www.med.upenn.edu/apps/faculty/index.php/g20002840/p8679288. Projects will be tailored to the interests of the successful candidate within broadly defined research themes, including: • Molecular communication among endothelial cells, fibroblasts, macrophages, and dendritic cells • Metabolic and transcriptional mechanisms regulating γδT cell dysfunction in the tumor microenvironment • Combinatorial strategies integrating CAR-T cell therapy with FLASH- and SFRT-based radiotherapy • Targeting endothelial plasticity and myeloid cell transitions to improve cardiac repair following ischemia The Fan Lab emphasizes rigorous science, intellectual independence, and strong mentorship, supporting career trajectories with a strong track record of trainees advancing to independent faculty positions. Required Qualifications • PhD in Cancer Biology, Immunology, Cell Biology, or a related field • Demonstrated research productivity, including first-author primary research publications • Strong experimental and analytical skills in molecular, cellular, or tissue biology • Ability to independently design and execute experiments Preferred Qualifications • Experience working with mouse models • Background in metabolism and/or immunological analysis • Experience in cancer and/or cardiovascular research workflows • Bioinformatics skills (e.g., R, Python, or related tools) Candidates requiring visa sponsorship are welcome to apply To apply, please submit: a curriculum vitae, a cover letter describing their research experience and interests a PDF of relevant publication(s) contact information for three references Any questions can be directed to Dr. Yi Fan (fanyi@upenn.edu)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191001": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: Perelman School of Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania, Perelman School of Medicine The Wherry lab, in the Department of Systems Pharmacology and Translational Therapeutics, has an opening for a postdoctoral fellow to join a CRI-funded research project. This position is part of a larger CRI Initiative to establish a data resource that will enable data-driven innovation in immunotherapy and address the translational gap between preclinical models and clinical trials. This Immunotherapy Altas will incorporate comprehensive immune landscapes across commonly used mouse models as well as how these landscapes are altered when immunotherapy perturbations are applied. The goal for this position is to contribute to the establishment of this resource and to use it to address data-driven hypotheses that are generated based on analyses of this resource. The postdoctoral fellow will lead this project in the Wherry lab and be instrumental in integrating similar data across two additional CRI-Immunotherapy Atlas labs. Experimental approaches to address the tumor-immune microenvironment will include flow cytometry, scRNA (+TCR)-seq, proteomics, and spatial analysis. This position is an excellent opportunity to learn how to master cutting-edge technologies, leverage large datasets, generate data-driven hypotheses, and become a strong collaborative scientific leader. Successful candidates will have a PhD, MD, or MD/PhD degree, experience in immunology and/or cancer biology, a strong record of productivity, and a demonstrated ability to work independently and as a team. To apply, please submit: (1) A curriculum vitae (2) A cover letter summarizing research experience and career goals (3) The contact information of three references Any questions can be directed to Dr. E. John Wherry (wherry@pennmedicine.upenn.edu)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/183045": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow - Reproductive  Biology",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: Perelman School of Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania - Perelman School of Medicine Postdoctoral Fellow - Reproductive  Biology About the Lab The Bomba-Warczak Laboratory at the University of Pennsylvania is recruiting creative, collaborative, and highly independent Postdoctoral Scientists to lead new research projects in reproductive biology. Our lab studies how the ovarian microenvironment supports oocyte development and reproductive function across the lifespan. We are particularly interested in understanding how cellular interactions within the ovarian follicle shape oocyte health, tissue physiology, and reproductive aging. Our research integrates mouse models, quantitative cell and tissue biology, imaging approaches, stable-isotope tracing, and mass-spectrometry–based omics to uncover molecular pathways that regulate reproductive biology and cellular communication in complex tissues. Learn more about the lab at bombawarczaklab.com. Projects will be tailored to the interests of the successful candidate within broadly defined research themes including: • Mechanisms regulating ovarian follicle biology and oocyte development • Molecular communication between somatic and germ cells within reproductive tissues • Systems-level analysis of metabolic and proteomic dynamics in reproductive physiology • Cellular and molecular determinants of reproductive aging and tissue resilience The Bomba-Warczak Lab is based at the Perelman School of Medicine's Department of Phyisology at the University of Pennsylvania, within a highly collaborative scientific environment that provides access to world-class facilities for proteomics, metabolomics, imaging, and mouse genetics. Our lab emphasizes rigorous science, intellectual independence, and strong mentorship supporting diverse career trajectories. Required Qualifications • PhD in Reproductive Biology, Developmental Biology, Physiology, Cell Biology, or a related field • Demonstrated research experience in reproductive science (e.g., ovarian biology, follicle biology, oocyte biology, reproductive physiology, or related areas) • At least one first-author primary research publication in a peer-reviewed journal • Strong experimental and analytical skills in molecular, cellular, or tissue biology • Ability to independently design and execute experiments Preferred Qualifications • Experience working with mammalian systems and/or mouse models • Exposure to mass spectrometry, proteomics, metabolomics, stable-isotope tracing, or metabolic analysis • Experience with ovarian or reproductive tissue workflows (e.g., follicle/oocyte work, reproductive physiology models, imaging-based assays) • Computational/data analysis skills (R, Python, or similar) Applicants should submit a CV, cover letter, a PDF with relevant publication(s), and provide 3 contacts for references. In the cover letter, applicants should describe their rseaech experience and indicate the experimental direction they would like to pursue in the lab. Candidates requiring visa sponsorship are welcome to apply."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/175330": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow in Cancer Biology",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: Perelman School of Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania - Perelman School of Medicine Faculty Mentor: Carman Li, Ph.D. Department: Cancer Biology Funding Source: PI Grants Number of Positions: 2 Lab Website: www.CarmanLiLab.org Open to U.S. citizens and foreign nationals. The Carman Li Lab studies hereditary cancer development and their implications for sporadic cancer interception. A special focus is on breast cancer driven by germline mutations in the tumor suppressor gene BRCA1. The lab is recruiting postdoctoral fellows to investigate the molecular mechanisms and intervention strategies for BRCA1-related breast cancer. The fellows will lead their independent research projects using advanced genetically engineered mouse models, 3D organoid cultures, single-cell omics, patient sample analyses, and other approaches. This is an excellent opportunity to contribute to cutting-edge research and help advance the frontiers of cancer biology. Fellows will have opportunities to present their work broadly and publish their discoveries in peer-reviewed journals. The Li lab also values a supportive and collaborative environment. Successful candidates will have a PhD, MD, or MD/PhD degree, experience in cancer biology and/or mouse genetics, a strong record of productivity, and a demonstrated ability to work independently and as a team. To apply, please submit: (1) a curriculum vitae, (2) a cover letter summarizing research experience and career goals, and (3) the contact information of three references Any questions can be directed to Dr. Carman Li ( CarmanLi@upenn.edu ) You are also welcome to notify Dr. Li once the application materials have been submitted through Interfolio."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/186679": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow in Cardiovascular Disease and Cancer",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: Perelman School of Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania, Perelman School of Medicine  The Ky lab is a National Institutes of Health-, American Heart Association-, and Patient-Centered Outcomes Research Institute -funded internationally recognized program that focuses on advancing the cardiovascular health of patients living with and beyond cancer through clinical science. The Ky Lab has a postdoctoral position in the field of cardiovascular and cancer clinical epidemiology and data science. This position for a clinical researcher involves the use of large datasets comprised of biomarker, echocardiography, genomic, clinical, social determinants of health and other \"-omic\" data to understand individual patient risk. The ideal candidate will have a Ph.D. or M.D. with strong experience in epidemiology, data science, and computational and data analysis. The ideal candidate must also have strong written and oral communication skills, and be able to effectively design an epidemiologic study and develop and execute an analytic plan with close mentorship. Competitive compensation for appropriate expertise. The Ky lab values the mentor-mentee relationship and is highly invested in the academic and professional development of lab members. This is a two-year position with the opportunity to renew for multiple years. Ph.D. or MD with a strong background in epidemiology, longitudinal data analysis, survival analysis, risk prediction, and manipulation of large datasets.  Expertise in biomarkers, genomics, and imaging is also a strength.  A demonstrated ability to work both independently as well as collaboratively with a multi-disciplinary and diverse team is important. The University of Pennsylvania is committed to supporting a diverse and inclusive workforce and, in most cases, is able to sponsor visas for qualified applicants. Visa sponsorship for this position is contingent upon eligibility as determined by the specific funding source and applicable regulations. Please submit a cover letter, CV including a list of your publications, and the names/addresses of 3 references through this Interfolio application."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/182811": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow in computational neuroimaging",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: Perelman School of Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania - Perelman School of Medicine Faculty Mentor: Aristeidis Sotiras, PhD ( Aristeidis (Aris) Sotiras, PhD – AIBIL) Department: Radiology Funding Source: NIH Number of positions: 2 Two postdoctoral positions in computational neuroimaging and machine learning are available at the Center for Biomedical Image Computing and Analytics (CBICA), Radiology, University of Pennsylvania. The successful candidate will join a highly collaborative research environment focused on developing computational methods for analyzing large-scale neuroimaging and clinical datasets to better understand brain health and disease. The postdoctoral fellow will contribute to projects at the intersection of medical image analysis, machine learning, and clinical neuroscience, with opportunities to work in two main research directions: (1) Development of advanced image analysis tools for MRI and PET data to study brain aging and neurodegenerative diseases, including Alzheimer’s disease. These projects involve large multi-cohort datasets and focus on extracting biologically meaningful imaging biomarkers. (2) Application of machine learning methods to imaging and clinical data from patients with neurocognitive disorders, with the goal of improving disease characterization, prediction of clinical outcomes, and personalized diagnostics. NIH grant funded. Applicants requiring visa sponsorship are welcome to apply. The ideal candidate will have a strong background in image processing, statistical analysis, machine learning, or pattern recognition. Experience with deep learning and neuroimaging data (e.g., MRI or PET) is highly desirable. Applicants should hold a PhD in computer science, biomedical engineering, electrical engineering, applied mathematics, neuroscience, or a related quantitative field. Required documents for upload: CV, Research statement, at least 3 referencess"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195388": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow in Social and Psychological Wellbeing and Aging at the University of Pennsylvania",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: Perelman School of Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania - Perelman School of Medicine We are seeking a highly motivated Postdoctoral Research Fellow to join an NIH-funded research program focused on improving outcomes for persons living with dementia and their care partners. The fellow will contribute to the development and validation of the Social and Psychological Outcomes Tally (SPOT) , a novel patient-centered measure of social and psychological quality of life designed for individuals with mild-to-moderate dementia and their caregivers. This multidisciplinary project integrates psychometrics, behavioral science, health services research, qualitative methods, and dementia care to create tools that can guide clinical decision-making and care planning. Responsibilities include participant recruitment and data collection, quantitative and qualitative data analysis, psychometric modeling, manuscript preparation, grant development, and collaboration with clinicians, patients, caregivers, and community stakeholders. Ideal candidates will have training in psychology, public health, epidemiology, health services research, gerontology, biostatistics, or a related field, with interests in aging, dementia, patient-reported outcomes, caregiving, and measurement science. This position offers outstanding opportunities for scientific publication, interdisciplinary mentorship, and career development in dementia research. The postdoc will work closely with Shana Stites (https://pennmemorycenter.org/who-we-are/staff/shana-stites-psyd-ma-ms/) at the University of Pennsylvania and other collaborating partners. The postdoctoral scholar will have the opportunity to engage in this innovative project from participant recruitment through data collection and analysis. Through this project, the postdoctoral fellow will gain access to a range of foundational and advances research skills alongside exceptional research opportunities that facilitate long-term career building. • Ph.D. or equivalent in psychology, epidemiology, economics, demography, sociology, and/or related fields • Interest in Alzheimer’s disease, aging, mixed methods, and/or aging • Skills in quantitative data and statistical analysis, skills also in qualitative analyses a strength • Excellent English verbal and written skills • Excellent reasoning skills • Strong organizational skills and ability to establish priorities to meet deadlines • Ability to work in a multidisciplinary setting with a multidisciplinary research team Responsibilities • Assist with study recruitment and data collection • Support analyses of cross-sectional and longitudinal data • Collaborate with multidisciplinary national and international research teams and with community stakeholders • Disseminate research findings at scientific conferences and stakeholder meetings • Draft manuscripts for scientific publication • Provide technical and written assistance on grants for NIH and other external funding To apply, please submit cover letter, resume/CV, writing sample, Dissertation Abstract, and two letters of recommendation."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/181002": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellowship",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: Perelman School of Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania; Perelman School of Medicine; BRB II/III The Maxwell Laboratory is seeking a highly motivated and creative postdoctoral fellow to study early detection and early cancer development in patients with inherited risk to cancer.  In collaboration with other investigators within the Basser Center for BRCA at Penn Medicine, Children’s Hospital of Philadelphia, Dana Farber Cancer Institute, St Jude’s Children’s Hospital and Ontario institute for Cancer Research, we are studying early cancer detection and initiation in individuals with inherited mutations in TP53 and BRCA1/2 .  The postdoctoral fellow, along with graduate students, research technicians and clinical research coordinators in the lab, will lead projects studying the clinical utility of multi-modal cancer early detection tests and early cancer development in these patients.  Dr. Kara Maxwell Is the Director of the Basser Center for BRCA Men and BRCA Program and has a clinical practice caring for patients with cancer predisposition syndromes.  The laboratory has established both the Li Fraumeni Syndrome (LFS) and Men & BRCA Biobanks containing extensive clinical information and biospecimens from these patients which are utilized for our experiments. Qualified applicants should have a doctoral degree (PhD, MD/PhD, MD or equivalent) and have a strong background in genomics, molecular biology and bioinformatics.  A background in mouse genetics and mouse models of cancer is preferred but not required. Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Applications should be sent through Interfolio using the link below and include; 1. Cover Letter 2. Curriculum vitae 3. Contact information for three references 4. Links to publications and any preprint server manuscripts."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/183891": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellowships in AI & Medical Imaging",
      "description": "Posted: Mar 26, 2026 Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: Perelman School of Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania - Perelman School of Medicine Faculty Mentor: Yong Fan, PhD ( Yong Fan, Ph.D. – AIBIL) Department: Radiology Funding Source: NIH Number of positions: 3 We are recruiting three highly motivated Postdoctoral Fellows for NIH-funded positions to drive innovative research at the intersection of machine learning, medical imaging, and clinical data science. We offer a flexible, highly collaborative environment where fellows can dive deep into a specific domain or pioneer research across multiple fields. Key Research Areas (Candidates may focus on one or bridge multiple domains): Neuroimaging & Predictive Modeling: Develop advanced statistical and ML methods for large-scale neuroimaging to predict brain age, model disease trajectories, and discover neurological biomarkers. Functional Network Modeling & Deep Learning: Apply novel DL architectures and generative models to decode dynamic functional connectivity and brain network organization. CT Analysis & Urological Applications: Utilize computer vision and radiomics for automated segmentation, classification, treatment response prediction, and robust multi-site modeling in urological health. Education: Ph.D. in Computer Science, Biomedical Engineering, Applied Mathematics, Neuroscience, or a related quantitative field. Core Expertise: Strong background in machine learning, deep learning, computer vision, and medical image analysis. Agentic AI Focus: A strong interest in, or willingness to learn and develop, Agentic AI applications to enhance medical imaging workflows and research. Track Record: Proven ability to conduct independent research, collaborate effectively, and publish in top-tier venues. Required documents for upload : Curriculum Vitae (CV), Research Statement (Highlighting your preferred research areas and how your expertise aligns with or bridges them), and References: Names and contact information for three professional references (requests will be sent directly through Interfolio)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/180242": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Position in In Vivo Analysis of Basal Ganglia Circuit Function",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: Perelman School of Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania – Fuccillo Lab The Fuccillo Lab at the University of Pennsylvania is seeking postdoctoral fellows to join our team and contribute to projects investigating how rodent cortico-basal ganglia circuits control valence-, outcome- and sensory-guided actions , and how these processes are disrupted in genetic neurodevelopmental disorder models (including autism, OCD, ADHD, and intellectual disability). Postdocs will work in a collaborative environment using a range of approaches, including: In vivo recording and imaging techniques during behavior Quantitative behavioral modeling Ex vivo biophysical measures of neural circuit function Molecular, genetic and viral tools For more information about our research, visit our lab website. https://www.med.upenn.edu/fuccillolab/ Highly motivated and independent researchers with strong backgrounds in in vivo or in vitro physiological analyses . Preferred skills include: In vivo extracellular recording In vivo calcium imaging Large-scale data analysis Acute slice electrophysiology PhD, MD, or MD/PhD required. Applicants should submit the following materials: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae (CV) Contact information for 3 references including PhD supervisor"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189959": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Position in Physiology and Regeneration",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: Perelman School of Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania - Perelman School of Medicine The problem we work on: We study Physiologic Regeneration : whether a tissue that rebuilds the right cells actually recovers the job those cells are supposed to do. After injury, an epithelium can regenerate the correct cell types and still fail to restore coordinated function. Most of the field checks whether the cells come back. We measure whether the function comes back, and we work on why the two so often diverge. We use the airway surface epithelium because it is constantly injured by the outside world, has to recover to keep the host alive, and crucially, has functions we can measure directly : ion and fluid transport, mucociliary clearance, and antimicrobial killing. Projects pair stem-cell biology with quantitative surface physiology to define how, and whether, function is restored after injury. Who we're looking for: We're recruiting people who want to help build a lab and be part of something, not just come to work. Joining a new group is a real risk, and we take that seriously. The traits we actually select for: You get genuinely excited by a result you didn't predict and you're disciplined enough to chase what it really means, not what you hoped it meant. You're honest about the limits of your own data. You'd rather be accurate than overreach. You keep rigorous written records of every experiment, because here the documentation is part of the science, not paperwork after it. You're someone people want at the next bench: you share credit, you show up when a colleague's experiment is on the line, and you don't trade in drama. You want the work to matter, eventually, to patients, and you're willing to do the slow, careful version to get there. If this is the environment you've been looking for, we should talk. PhD, MD, or MD/PhD, completed or imminent. Command of the scientific process and sound experimental reasoning — the one thing we can't teach quickly. Solid sterile technique and core bench skills. Willingness to learn and do mouse work (prior experience helpful, not required). Willingness to learn and do in vitro and ex vivo work with human tissue (prior experience helpful, not required). A familiarity with molecular biology and genetic tools (CRISPR, conditional mice), microbiology (CFU assays), microscopy (confocal, 2P), electrophysiology (Ussing chambers), or large-dataset analysis (scRNA-seq) is helpful but not required. Please first read https://www.viralshahlab.org/ Particularly review how we run the lab and what we expect of one another. Then please send your CV, a short statement of your interests and goals, and a list of references (including your PhD advisor) via interfolio. Please also include a statement where you describe one result that surprised you, why it surprised you given what you expected, and what you did next."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190196": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher - Health Services and Health Economics Research",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: Perelman School of Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania - Perelman School of Medicine Faculty Mentor: Dr. Michael Harhay Department: Biostatistics, Epidemiology & Informatics Number of Positions: 1 Open to applications from U.S. citizens and foreign nationals The research groups of Drs. Scott Halpern, Michael Harhay, and Ari Friedman at the University of Pennsylvania invite applications for a postdoctoral researcher in health services research (HSR) and health economics. Individuals with >1 year of post-doctoral experience could be considered for research associate roles. We would be particularly interested in (bio)statisticians with training and interests in health economics and cost-effectiveness research. The postdoc researcher will support and lead projects using large, complex electronic health record (EHR) datasets, ranging from short, focused descriptive studies to natural experiments and causal inference. The position suits a candidate with prior experience using healthcare data (ideally including from electronic health records) who combines skills in a variety of econometric and statistical methods, technical sophistication in deploying those methods rigorously, curiosity, and the ability to work with clinicians and engage with clinical literature to inform their work. The role places equal value on ambitious analysis and on the day-to-day discipline of data cleaning and documentation that underpins credible findings. The ideal candidate combines strong quantitative training with sound judgment about when data can and cannot answer a question. Working with colleagues fluent in both clinical practice and rigorous quantitative methods, the postdoc researcher will learn to distinguish mechanical features of healthcare data from substantive economic and meaningful findings and to exploit those features to strengthen causal inference. Research Activities The postdoc researcher will: •         Develop estimation strategies that use multiple sources of plausibly exogenous variation to identify clinically and economically relevant quantities of interest. •         Investigate natural experiments in the emergency department and ICU tied to fundamental economic questions, supported by additional clinical and operational data-gathering infrastructure. •         Explore distributed regression analyses across a coalition of health systems on emergency department boarding and crowding. •         Contribute statistical and cost-effectiveness expertise to ongoing and planned studies across the three research groups. •         Lead manuscript writing aimed at leading medical journals, in addition to health services research outlets. •         Present findings at conferences. Data Stewardship and Documentation •         Clean and structure EHR data for analyses. •         Maintain careful and reproducible documentation of datasets/code and lead the creation of an organized catalog of existing datasets. •         Record methodological decisions in detail and use those notes to draft the Methods sections of manuscripts. •         Write data-sharing and replication plans. •         Prepare cuts of cleaned datasets for rotating students on an occasional basis. Team and Lab Coordination •         Serve as a point of contact between students in the lab and the faculty, assigning weekly tasks, monitoring progress, and supporting students' development. •         Maintain coding infrastructure for weekly reports tracking study progress and data completeness across active projects. •         Coordinate co-author feedback and track manuscript submissions. •         Develop and update training guides, study manuals, and materials supporting consistent research operations. Qualifications •         Doctoral degree in biostatistics, statistics, economics, epidemiology, health services research, or a related quantitative field, ideally with training or demonstrated interest in economics and cost-effectiveness analysis. •         Experience working with healthcare data and large relational datasets, with an understanding of the level of rigor the work requires. •         Proficiency in a statistical programming language such as R or Python. •         Skill in data cleaning and management, statistical and econometric analysis (including cost-effectiveness methods), manuscript writing and editing, research operations coordination, and supervision and mentorship of students. •         A view of data cleaning as essential to good research rather than as a chore. •         Ability to work well in teams and to manage time independently while coordinating among several cooperative principal investigators. •         A firm commitment to preserving the privacy of patient health information, including guarding against inadvertent disclosure within complex data-processing pipelines. Please include the following with your application: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae 3 references"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193626": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher - Interdisciplinary",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: Perelman School of Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania - Perelman School of Medicine Faculty Mentor: Dr. Michael Levy Department: Biostatistics, Epidemiology & Informatics (DBEI) Number of position: 1 Open to applications from the US citizens and foreign nationals We are recruiting a Postdoctoral Researcher in Epidemiology, Entomology or Architecture for an interdisciplinary project on 3D printed housing and health.  We are seeking individuals with experience conducting fieldwork, and the skills to oversee fieldwork, develop protocols and data collection methods. The project will include household sampling of insects, mold, and other abiotic public health threats. The project includes adaptive sampling methods, familiarity with coding and quantitative skills will be necessary, but are not required to apply. Field sites are not yet determined, and may include 3d printed and conventional housing in a number of sites across the US. Please note that eligibility for appointment as a postdoc requires a candidate to hold, or to have completed the requirements for, an advanced degree, e.g., PhD, MD, or equivalent. Candidates from non-U.S. universities must hold advanced degrees equivalent to those awarded in U.S. institutions to qualify for appointment as postdoctoral fellows. It is the candidate’s responsibility to provide transcripts and/or a diploma certifying that the PDT has received their degree, and although this may be delegated, it is the mentor’s responsibility to review this documentation and ensure that it is satisfactory and included in the candidate’s file. Candidates who have completed the requirements for an advanced degree, but have not yet been awarded such a degree, must be able to demonstrate in a manner verifiable by the University that such requirements have been completed. All candidate application materials are collected and managed through Interfolio. Required documents are for this position are: Cover letter Curriculum vitae Contact information for three references"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/187547": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher in AI-Driven Data Curation and Data Integration",
      "description": "Posted: Jun 02, 2026 Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: Perelman School of Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania - Perelman School of Medicine Faculty Mentor: Joost Wagenaar Department: Informatics Number of Positions: 2 Open to applications from US Citizens and foreign nationals. The Wagenaar Lab is seeking a highly motivated Postdoctoral Researcher to conduct research at the intersection of artificial intelligence, common data elements (CDEs), and large-scale biomedical datasets. The Wagenaar Lab is jointly based in the Institute for Biomedical Informatics and the Department of Biostatistics, Epidemiology, and Informatics at the University of Pennsylvania, and leads the academic development of the Pennsieve scientific data platform. The lab’s mission is to create scalable, sustainable infrastructure that enables data integration, reuse, and discovery across clinical and scientific research domains. This postdoctoral position will focus on developing AI-enabled methods to automate and augment data curation, with an emphasis on leveraging CDEs to improve the usability, interoperability, and scientific value of public datasets. The successful candidate will work across disease areas—including Epilepsy, Immune Health, and programs within the NIH HEAL Initiative—to design approaches that harmonize heterogeneous datasets, enrich metadata, and support scalable data exploration. The Postdoctoral Researcher will work closely with the Pennsieve development team and a broad network of scientific collaborators to translate industry best practices in data engineering and AI into the academic research ecosystem. A central goal of this role is to move beyond manual, project-specific curation toward reproducible, automated, and extensible curation workflows that can be applied across datasets, programs, and institutions. In addition to platform and method development, the Postdoctoral Researcher is expected to contribute to peer-reviewed publications, open-source software, and community-facing resources that advance AI-enabled data stewardship and reuse. Responsibilities Develop AI-based methods and deploy them at scale to automate and augment data curation using Common Data Elements Design workflows to harmonize, validate, and enrich public datasets across Epilepsy, Immune Health, and NIH HEAL programs Develop novel mechanisms to interrogate, visualize and interact with complex scientific datasets and increase the value of these datasets for the scientific community. Integrate curation methods into scalable, cloud-based scientific data platforms Collaborate with the Pennsieve development team and scientific partners to align methods with real research workflows Evaluate and validate curation approaches using large, heterogeneous public datasets Prepare manuscripts, technical documentation, and presentations describing methods and outcomes. Qualifications Ph.D. (preferred) or Master’s degree in Biomedical Informatics, Computer Science, Data Science, Bioinformatics, or a related field Experience with machine learning, natural language processing, or AI applied to structured and unstructured data Familiarity with Common Data Elements, data standards, or ontology-based data representation (preferred) Strong programming skills in Docker, Python, Go, Java, or related languages Experience working with large-scale biomedical or clinical datasets Experience with cloud-based data processing and scalable analytics environments (AWS preferred) Strong written and verbal communication skills and an interest in interdisciplinary collaboration Please include a cover letter and a CV for consideration."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/174189": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher in Bio/Statistics and Machine Learning",
      "description": "Posted: Sep 18, 2025 Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: Perelman School of Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania - Perelman School of Medicine Faculty Mentor: Qi Long, Ph.D. Department: Biostatistics, Epidemiology and Informatics Funding Sources: Grant Number of Positions : 1 Open to applications from U.S. citizens and foreign nationals. The University of Pennsylvania, the largest private employer in Philadelphia, is a world-renowned leader in education, research, and innovation. This historic, Ivy League school consistently ranks among the top 10 universities in the annual U.S. News & World Report survey. Penn has 12 highly-regarded schools that provide opportunities for undergraduate, graduate and continuing education, all influenced by Penn’s distinctive interdisciplinary approach to scholarship and learning. Penn offers a unique working environment within the city of Philadelphia. The University is situated on a beautiful urban campus, with easy access to a range of educational, cultural, and recreational activities. With its historical significance and landmarks, lively cultural offerings, and wide variety of atmospheres, Philadelphia is the perfect place to call home for work and play. Duties & Responsibilities: We invite applications for a post-doctoral researcher position in Dr. Qi Long’s lab ( https://www.med.upenn.edu/long-lab/ ). The position is open until filled and the initial appointment is for two years with a possible extension for a third year. The position will offer opportunities to conduct research on 1) statistical foundations of large language models (LLMS) under joint supervision of Dr. Weijie Su ( https://www.weijie-su.com/ ), or 2)  development and assessment of foundation models and agentic AI for biomedical research and healthcare using multimodal data such as electronic health records (EHRs) data, multi-omics data and imaging data in collaboration with researchers in radiology and computer science. Successful post-doctoral researcher is expected to develop independence in methodological research through activities including, but not limited to, writing peer-reviewed publications for research conferences and journals, giving presentations at research conferences, and assisting in grant proposal development. The University of Pennsylvania is an EOE. Minorities/Women/Individuals with disabilities/Protected Veterans are encouraged to apply. Salary: Competitive. Ph.D. degree in statistics, computer science, or related quantitative fields required. Additionally, the following skills are desirable: excellent training in statistics and/or machine learning; excellent programming skills in Python and possibly other programming languages such as C++ and Java; excellent writing and oral communication skills. Review of applications will begin shortly and will continue until the position is filled. Applications should be sent through Interforlio using the link below and include: Cover Letter Curriculum vitae Research Statement Contact Information for three references"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189763": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher in Cardiovascular Research",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: Perelman School of Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania, Perelman School of Medicine The laboratory of Dr. Yanqing (Anna) Gong and Yi Fan in the Cardiovascular Institute at the University of Pennsylvania is seeking one or two highly motivated Postdoctoral Fellows to join a research project investigating vascular function and immunity in cardiovascular disease. We aim at understanding the molecular mechanisms underlying cardiac repair after myocardial infarction with a focus on vessel tube formation, endothelial cell plasticity and immune modulation. The goal of this study is to develop new regenerative therapeutics by targeting hostile ischemic microenvironment to promote cardiac repair after myocardial infarction. We are also interested in immunotherapy for cardiovascular disease using CAR-T or engineered myeloid cells, promising strategies that our laboratories have developed for treating cancer. More information about the laboratories can be found at https://www.med.upenn.edu/apps/faculty/index.php/g275/p8688889 https://www.med.upenn.edu/apps/faculty/index.php/g20002840/p8679288 We encourage applications from highly motivated individuals with a background in cardiology, vascular biology, immunology or related fields. Practical experience in molecular and cellular biology techniques and animal models is required. Preference will be given to candidates with prior experience in endothelial biology and cardiovascular research. Applications should be sent through Interfolio and should include: 1. Curriculum vitae (with names and contact information for three references) 2. Brief statement of prior research experience and current interests 3. Contact information for three (3) references Questions can be directed to: gongy@pennmedicine.upenn.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191385": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher in Chromatin Biology and Neuroscience",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: Perelman School of Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania - Perelman School of Medicine Faculty Mentor: Elizabeth A Heller, Ph.D Department: Pharmacology (SPATT) Funding Source: PI Grant Number of Positions: 2 Open to U.S. citizens, p ermanent residents and foreign nationals The Heller Lab studies the mechanisms by which epigenetic remodeling regulates gene function, in the context of neuropsychiatric diseases such as addiction and depression.  To approach this problem, we directly manipulate histone and DNA modifications at specific genes in vivo, using viral delivery of novel epigenetic editing tools, such as CRISPR/dCas9-fusion proteins. The Perelman School of Medicine is a world leader in biomedical research, boasting over $800M in annual research funding and ranked third nationwide among the best medical schools in research. The Heller Lab is a member of the Penn Epigenetics Institute and Institute for Translational Medicine and Therapeutics, and is housed with the Department of Systems Pharmacology and Translational Therapeutics (SPATT). For further information see http://www.med.upenn.edu/hellerlab/ . Current projects include: 1. Histone methyltransferase, Setd2, in substance use disorders, R01DA064336 2. Epigenetic mechanisms of sustained transcription across cocaine abstinence, R01DA052465-06 3. Sex- and hormone-dependent differences in memory vulnerability to acute traumatic stress, R01MH139931-01 4. Epigenetic regulation of Cdk5 in cognition and emotion, R01MH126027-04 PhD, MD or MD/PhD required.  Expertise in chromatin biology, neurobiology and/or molecular biology is required. Knowledge of protein biochemistry, chromatin biology, and/or bioinformatics (including mathematics and computational biology), behavior, are encouraged.  Competitive salary > NIH minimum. Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the positions are filled. Applications should be sent through interfolio using the link below and include: 1. Cover Letter that includes (1) a brief summary of your present and future research interests <200 words, (2) desired start date. 2. CV, including list of publications. 3. Contact information of three or more references."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/183243": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral researcher in neurodegenerative disease and cancer",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: Perelman School of Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania - Perelman School of Medicine The laboratory of Professor Xiaolu Yang at the Perelman School of Medicine, University of Pennsylvania, is seeking a highly motivated postdoctoral researcher to join our dynamic and interdisciplinary team. Our work investigates the fundamental mechanisms that drive neurodegenerative disease and cancer, with current projects centered around two major research areas: 1. Protein quality control, aging, and neurodegenerative diseases Our laboratory has discovered two previously unrecognized protein quality control (PQC) systems involving tripartite motif (TRIM) proteins and poly-Asp/Glu (polyD/E) proteins. These pathways function independently of ATP and exhibit distinct evolutionary conservation—TRIM in animals and polyD/E across eukaryotes—setting them apart from established PQC mechanisms. Ongoing work aims to define their molecular mechanisms, elucidate their contributions to aging and neurodegenerative diseases (Alzheimer’s, Parkinson’s, ALS), and explore their potential for therapeutic intervention. 2. Tumor suppression, metabolism, and autophagy We examine the regulatory mechanisms and functional roles of the tumor suppressor p53, with emphasis on its control of metabolic pathways essential for biosynthesis and redox balance. Our studies explore how p53 integrates metabolic cues by acting as both a sensor and regulator of cellular metabolism. In parallel, we are identifying metabolic changes that promote tumor initiation and progression. This work has recently expanded to include investigations into how metabolism and autophagy influence the biology of embryonic and cancer stem cells. For more information about our research, please see the following publications and website: Science 381 : eadd6696 (2023) PMID: 37499037 Science Advances 8 : eabo0404 (2022) PMID: 35857842 Nature 597 : 132-137 (2021) PMID: 34408321 Nature Cell Biology 23 : 978–991 (2021) PMID: 34497368 Cell Metabolism 33 : 94-109 (2021) PMID: 33159852 Science 369 : 397-403 (2020) PMID: 32703873 Cell Reports 33 : 108418 (2020) PMID: 33264628 Nature Communications 11 : 348 (2020) PMID: 31953436 Nature Communications 10 : 1495 (2019) PMID: 30940803 Nature Communications 9 : 4683 (2018) PMID: 30409970 Nature Communications 9 : 1223 (2018) PMID: 29581427 Cell Reports 18 : 3143-3154 (2017) PMID: 28355566 Molecular Cell 55 : 15-30 (2014) (Cover Article) PMID: 24882209 Nature 493 : 689-93 (2013) PMID: 23334421 Nature Cell Biology 15 : 991-1000 (2013) (Cover Article) PMID: 23811687 Nature Cell Biology 13 : 310-316 (2011) (Cover Article) PMID: 21336310 Molecular Cell 37 : 668-678 (2010) (Cover Article) PMID: 20227371 Lab website: https://www.med.upenn.edu/apps/faculty/index.php/g275/p20138 Applicants should hold, or be nearing completion of, a PhD or MD degree and demonstrate a strong publication record, rigorous experimental skills, and relevant experience in areas such as biochemistry, cell biology, molecular biology, metabolism, stem cell biology, neuroscience, cancer research, or animal models. Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Applications should be sent through Interfolio using the link below and include: 1. Cover Letter; 2. Curriculum vitae; 3. Contact intormation tor three references."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/178347": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher in Neuroimaging",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: Perelman School of Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania, Perelman School of Medicine Faculty Mentor: Sudipto Dolui, PhD and John Detre, MD Department: Radiology and Neurology Funding Sources: Grant Number of Positions : 1 Candidates requiring visa sponsorship are welcome to apply The Detre Laboratory at the University of Pennsylvania, under the mentorship of Dr. John Detre and Dr. Sudipto Dolui, is seeking a highly motivated Postdoctoral Researcher to join a multidisciplinary team investigating brain aging and Alzheimer’s disease and related dementias through advanced neuroimaging methods. This position will support multiple NIH funded projects aimed at improving understanding of the underlying pathophysiology of aging and neurodegeneration and at developing advanced biomarkers of vascular brain health. Our lab conducts clinical and translational research utilizing structural, functional, and physiological magnetic resonance imaging methods, with a particular emphasis on cerebral blood flow imaging derived from arterial spin labeling to assess cerebrovascular integrity and neural activity. We analyze both locally acquired data and data aggregated from large national and international neuroimaging datasets. The ideal candidate will have an interest in biomedical research, be enthusiastic and highly motivated to participate in team science, and possess strong computing skills, including proficiency in coding languages such as Matlab, Python, or R. Experience in machine learning or deep learning, or a willingness to learn and apply these methods, is strongly desired. This position offers the opportunity to participate in an innovative research environment, gain hands-on experience with a variety of image analysis techniques, learn about the neuroscience of aging and dementia, and make critical contributions to primary research publications and grant proposals. The successful applicant will develop specialized expertise in cerebral blood flow imaging, arterial spin labeling, multimodal MRI, data harmonization, and quantitative neuroimaging approaches that support research in normal aging and Alzheimer’s disease and related dementias and contribute to the development of sensitive and specific neuroimaging biomarkers. Salary: Competitive, commensurate with NIH postdoctoral guidelines. Doctoral degree in Biomedical Engineering, Neuroscience, Computer Science, Electrical Engineering, Physics, or a related field Demonstrated experience in neuroimaging, machine learning, or deep learning Strong scientific writing skills and interest in leading manuscripts and contributing to grant applications Experience with perfusion MRI or arterial spin labeling is preferred Applications should be sent through Interfolio and should include: 1. Curriculum vitae (with names and contact information for three references) 2. Brief statement of prior research experience and current interests 3. Contact information for three (3) references Please contact Dr. Sudipto Dolui (sudiptod@pennmedicine.upenn.edu) with any questions about the position or the application process."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191979": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher, Translational Human Reproductive Biology",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: Perelman School of Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania - Perelman School of Medicine Faculty Mentor: Kathleen O’Neill MD MSTR Department: OBGYN Research Funding Source Type: PI Grant Number of Positions: 1 Candidates requiring visa sponsorship are welcome to apply. The O'Neill Lab in the Division of Reproductive Endocrinology and Infertility, Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology at the University of Pennsylvania Perelman School of Medicine seeks a Postdoctoral Researcher to join a translational research program focused on the female reproductive tract. Our overarching goal is to understand the cellular and molecular basis of implantation, endometrial regeneration, and gynecologic disease, and to translate those discoveries into new therapies for infertility and other disorders of women's health. The lab works almost exclusively in human systems. Ongoing projects include: molecular mapping of the healthy female reproductive tract as a Tissue Mapping Center within the NIH HuBMAP Consortium, using single-cell and spatial approaches (10x Multiome, Visium) and multiplexed imaging (CODEX); development of lipid nanoparticle and mRNA therapeutics for reproductive disorders in collaboration with the Penn Institute for RNA Innovation, including a vaginally administered mRNA-LNP therapeutic to reduce heavy menstrual bleeding as part of the Wellcome Leap “The Missed Vital Sign” program, which seeks precise, non-hormonal treatments that can be self-administered without surgery or suppression of the menstrual cycle; mapping the surface proteome of endometrial cell populations; and studies of endometrial regeneration and pregnancy immunology in recipients of the Penn UNTIL uterus transplant trial. In vitro human models, including patient-derived endometrial organoids, are central to this work. The Postdoctoral Researcher will take primary intellectual and technical ownership of a project within these areas, with the expectation of shaping its direction over time and building toward an independent research program. This is a highly collaborative environment: the lab includes a research assistant, rotating REI fellows, OB/GYN residents, graduate and medical students, and undergraduates, and works closely with clinical teams, engineering and RNA biology groups at Penn, and external scientific collaborators. We place real value on a positive, generous lab culture and on training that is responsive to each person's goals. The successful candidate will be someone who takes initiative, is comfortable troubleshooting and optimizing protocols independently, reaches out across institutions to learn a technique or start a collaboration, and contributes to the scientific and social health of the group. Responsibilities · Design, execute, and independently troubleshoot experiments using primary human reproductive tract tissue and patient-derived organoid models. · Maintain and expand primary and organoid culture systems, including derivation, passaging, differentiation, treatment experiments, and quality control. · Perform core molecular assays, including RNA and DNA extraction, RT-qPCR, protein assays, and sample preparation for bulk, single-cell, and spatial transcriptomics. · Perform and optimize tissue-based imaging, including IHC and immunofluorescence on FFPE and frozen human tissue, and multiplexed or multicyclic imaging platforms such as CODEX. · Analyze, quantify, and interpret imaging and sequencing data; work with computational collaborators to move analyses forward and to interrogate published and consortium datasets. · Maintain rigorous, organized, and reproducible documentation of protocols, experiments, and data; contribute to lab-wide standard operating procedures. · Coordinate with clinical teams and study coordinators on human tissue acquisition and processing, in strict adherence to IRB-approved protocols and specimen handling requirements. · Prepare figures, manuscripts, abstracts, and presentations; present work at lab meetings, institutional forums, and national scientific meetings. · Contribute to grant applications, progress reports, and consortium deliverables, and pursue individual fellowship or career development funding (for example F32, K99/R00, or foundation awards). · Establish and maintain scientific collaborations within Penn and at outside institutions, including directly contacting other laboratories to learn techniques, source reagents, and resolve technical problems. · Mentor and provide day-to-day technical guidance to REI fellows, OB/GYN residents, medical students, and undergraduate trainees. · Participate in shared lab operations and citizenship, including safety compliance, equipment upkeep, reagent ordering, journal club, and onboarding of new lab members. Required Qualifications · PhD, MD, or equivalent advanced degree in reproductive biology, cell or molecular biology, immunology, physiology, bioengineering, or a closely related field. Candidates who have completed all degree requirements but have not yet been conferred the degree may apply, provided completion can be verified by the University. Candidates from non-U.S. universities must hold a degree equivalent to those awarded by U.S. institutions. · Demonstrated record of research productivity appropriate to career stage, including first-author publications or manuscripts under review or in preparation. · Hands-on experience with mammalian tissue culture, including sterile technique and maintenance of primary cells or organoid cultures. · Proficiency in core molecular biology assays, such as nucleic acid extraction, RT-qPCR, protein quantification and immunoblotting, cloning, or ELISA. · Demonstrated ability to work independently: to design experiments, troubleshoot failed protocols, optimize new assays, and drive a project forward with limited day-to-day direction. · Evidence of exceptional initiative, such as independently initiated projects, collaborations, methods, funding applications, or programs developed during graduate training. · Strong organizational skills and conscientious, detailed record keeping across concurrent experiments and timelines. · Excellent written and verbal scientific communication skills, including experience presenting data to both specialist and non-specialist audiences. · Commitment to women's health and reproductive science, and to translational research that is relevant to patients. · A collaborative working style, with a track record of contributing to a positive team environment, supporting colleagues, and reflecting thoughtfully on feedback and on one's own performance. · Willingness to mentor and support junior trainees at multiple levels of experience. Preferred Qualifications · Experience with multiplexed or multicyclic imaging platforms (CODEX/PhenoCycler, Xenium, imaging mass cytometry, Vectra, or similar), including panel design, antibody conjugation, and validation. · Experience with IHC and immunofluorescence on human FFPE and frozen tissue sections, including antigen retrieval optimization and antibody validation. · Quantitative image analysis experience using QuPath, Fiji/ImageJ, CellProfiler, HALO, or comparable tools. · Able to run basic bioinformatic pipelines. · Familiarity with single-cell and spatial transcriptomics (10x Genomics Multiome, Visium, or similar), including sample preparation and interpretation of processed data. · Working proficiency in R or Python for analysis and visualization of imaging or sequencing data. · Experience with flow cytometry and immunophenotyping of tissue-derived cell populations. · Prior experience contributing to grant applications, or a clear plan to apply for individual fellowship or career development funding. · Prior mentoring or teaching experience with students, residents, or junior laboratory staff. · Background or strong interest in endometrial biology, implantation, endometriosis, or gynecologic disease. Please provide all of the following, as outlined, for your application to be considered complete: CV including list of publications Cover Letter or Letter of Interest Applicants are asked to use the cover letter to describe their research experience and technical skills, why they are interested in this laboratory and reproductive biology specifically, and their career goals Name and Contact Information for Three (3) References Must include Doctoral or Primary Research Mentor Letters of Recommendation will be requested for Finalists.  They are NOT required at the time of application."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/184733": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researchers - bench and dry lab scientists in the field of lipoprotein metabolism and the role of lipids in cardiometabolic traits and diseases (including atherosclerosis and metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease or MASLD)",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: Perelman School of Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania - Perelman School of Medicine  Faculty Mentor: Daniel J. Rader MD Hiring Department: Medicine - Division of Translational Medicine and Human Genetics Funding Sources: NIH and AHA grants Number of Positions: 2 U.S. citizens and foreign nationals may apply. Rader lab website: https://www.med.upenn.edu/raderlab/ Applications are invited for postdoctoral positions in the laboratory of Dr. Daniel J. Rader , Departments of Medicine and Genetics at the Perelman School of Medicine (PSOM) of the University of Pennsylvania. The Rader lab is a multidisciplinary research environment focused on leveraging human gene discovery to elucidate the biology and molecular physiology of genes and pathways that influence plasma lipoprotein metabolism as well as the role of lipids in cardiometabolic traits and diseases (including atherosclerosis and metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease or MASLD) and neurodegeneration/Alzheimer’s disease. The lab integrates computational genomics, fundamental molecular investigation in cell and animal model systems, and targeted deep phenotyping in human subjects to establish molecular mechanisms, clinical relevance and accelerate translation of research insights to potential treatment of human cardiometabolic disorders.  Approaches to this work incorporate 1) computational multi-omics to identify genes and pathways of interest; 2) human induced pluripotent stem cell (iPSC)-derived cell types to examine impact on hallmarks or biomarkers of phenotype and/or disease; 3) mouse model systems including germline and somatic gene manipulation; 4) use of natural genetic variants to elucidate protein structure-function relationships, and 5) recall-by-genotype studies in humans for deep phenotyping to inform genotype-phenotype relationships. We particularly encourage inquiries from 1) bench scientists with some background in lipid metabolism and 2) dry lab scientists with experience in bioinformatics and high-throughput genomics, metabolomics and/or proteomics technologies. Bench scientists: PhD in a biological science and a strong record of academic productivity are required; excellent written and verbal communication skills are also required. Individuals should be able to demonstrate an ability to successfully complete projects independently. Applicants with experience in genetics/genomics, biochemistry, cell biology, or physiology, and/or cell and mouse models, are encouraged to apply. We are particularly interested in bench scientists with some background in lipid metabolism. Dry lab scientists: Ph.D. degree in Biostatistics, Computer Science, Bioinformatics, or other biomedical domain required. A strong record of academic productivity, excellent written and verbal communication skills are also required. Individuals should be able to demonstrate an ability to successfully complete projects independently. Additionally, the following skills are desirable: fluency in statistical and data management computer languages such as R, Python, Bash, and SQL. We  are particularly interested in individuals with experience in bioinformatics and high-throughput genomics, metabolomics and/or proteomics technologies. To apply, please submit the following through Interfolio: 1. Brief statement of your research experience and interests detailing your qualifications for this position 2. Curriculum Vitae 3. Name and contact information of three references."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191248": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Scientist",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: Perelman School of Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania - Perelman School of Medicine The Department of Ophthalmology is seeking a highly motivated, full-time postdoctoral scientist to join a dynamic and collaborative research laboratory under the direction of the Principal Investigator (Dr. Lucie Guo, MD PhD). The successful candidate will drive to innovative research at the intersection of molecular biology, epigenetics, gene therapy, and ophthalmology while receiving mentorship in experimental design, scientific communication, grant writing, and career development. Primary responsibilities include: Conduct independent laboratory research using techniques such as molecular cloning, cell and tissue culture, transfection, CRISPR-based genome engineering, quantitative flow cytometry, and molecular and biochemical assays Perform animal studies, including work with mouse models, tissue collection, ocular imaging, and histologic and molecular analyses Design rigorous experiments, develop and optimize research protocols, and troubleshoot technical challenges independently Maintain accurate and organized laboratory records and ensure the quality, reproducibility, and appropriate documentation of experimental data Analyze and interpret research findings using appropriate quantitative and statistical methods Review relevant scientific literature and integrate emerging concepts and technologies into ongoing research projects Present research progress and findings regularly at laboratory meetings, departmental seminars, and collaborative research discussions Prepare figures, manuscripts, abstracts, and other scientific materials for publication in peer-reviewed journals Write fellowship, grant, and other funding applications Present research findings at local, national, and international scientific conferences Collaborate effectively with laboratory members, clinicians, and research partners across disciplines Assist with the training and mentorship of junior researchers, including research staff, graduate students, medical students, and undergraduate students Contribute to maintaining an organized, collegial, and productive laboratory environment Participate in professional-development activities and work with the Principal Investigator to establish and pursue individualized research and career goals Qualified applicants should hold a PhD in biology, biochemistry, biophysics, pharmacology, molecular biology, genetics, neuroscience, vision science, biomedical engineering, or a related field. Candidates should have a strong foundation in experimental design, data analysis, scientific communication, and the interpretation of research findings. Prior experience in one or more of the following areas is highly desirable: Molecular biology techniques, including molecular cloning, PCR, quantitative PCR, Western blotting, and immunofluorescence Mammalian cell and tissue culture, transfection, and the development or use of cell-based assays CRISPR-based genome editing or gene regulation, including guide RNA design and validation Viral vector design, production, or application, particularly adeno-associated viral vectors Animal research, including experience with mouse models, tissue collection, genotyping, injections, imaging, or surgical procedures Microscopy, histology, image analysis, and quantitative data analysis The ideal candidate will be highly motivated, organized, intellectually curious, and capable of working both independently and collaboratively. Strong written and oral communication skills, careful attention to experimental rigor and reproducibility, and a commitment to maintaining accurate research records are essential. Applicants should also demonstrate the ability to manage multiple projects, troubleshoot technical challenges, contribute constructively to a collaborative laboratory environment, and mentor junior researchers. A strong publication record and demonstrated interest in pursuing an independent scientific or academic career are preferred. Applications should be submitted through Interfolio using the link below and must include a cover letter that includes research interest and proposed projects, a curriculum vitae (NIH biosketch acceptable too), and contact information of 2-3 professional references."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/188949": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Scientist - Ophthalmology",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: Perelman School of Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania - Perelman School of Medicine Faculty Mentor: Peter M.J. Quinn Department: Ophthalmology Funding Source: PI Fund/Philantrophic Fund Number of Positions: 1 Open to applications from U.S. citizens and foreign nationals. Job Summary The Department of Ophthalmology is seeking a full-time postdoctoral scientist to work under the direction of the Principle Investigator, and perform duties associated with projects aiming to use gene therapy to tackle inherited retinal disorders (IRDs). Research approaches include therapeutic editing and induced pluripotent stem cell (iPSC) retinal organoid disease modeling. Further, the position requires assisting the principal investigator with grant and manuscript writing. Job Responsibilities · Conduct laboratory research, troubleshoot problems, record and analyze data, independently design new research experiments, and prepare publications, presentations, and grant materials as needed. · Participate in the training and supervision of new and junior lab members as needed, provide scientific and technical expertise and guidance for other ongoing projects as needed. · Maintaining induced pluripotent stem cell lines, differentiation and maintenance of retinal organoids from iPSCs. · Cell and molecular biology experiments related to DNA, RNA and protein from cell lines and organoids. · Design and execute DNA and/or RNA editing experiments in cell lines and retinal organoids. Qualifications · PhD degree in Biology, chemistry, or related science. · Experience with induced pluripotent stem cells and differentiation to retinal organoids or other organoid models is highly desirable. · Experience with DNA and/or RNA editing design and implementation is highly desirable Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue untill the position is filled. Applications should be sent through Interfollo using the link below and include: 1. Cover letter; 2. Curriculum vitae; 3. Contact information for three references."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/183230": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Scientist – Mitochondrial Biology",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: Perelman School of Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania - Perelman School of Medicine Postdoctoral Scientist – Mitochondrial Biology About the Lab: The Bomba-Warczak Laboratory at the University of Pennsylvania is recruiting creative, collaborative, and highly independent Postdoctoral Scientists to lead new research projects in mitochondrial biology. Our lab investigates how mitochondria are maintained and renewed in long-lived mammalian cells, with a particular interest in how organelle function is sustained over extended timescales in complex tissues. We integrate mouse models, quantitative biochemistry, stable-isotope tracing, and mass-spectrometry–based omics to understand the molecular mechanisms that govern mitochondrial maintenance, bioenergetics, and cellular resilience. Learn more about the lab at bombawarczaklab.com Projects will be tailored to the interests of the successful candidate within broadly defined research themes including: Mechanisms of mitochondrial maintenance and renewal in long-lived cells Molecular coordination of mitochondrial gene expression and protein homeostasis Systems-level analysis of mitochondrial metabolism and proteome dynamics Mitochondrial function in complex mammalian tissues, including the nervous system The Bomba-Warczak Lab is based at the Perelman School of Medicine's Department of Physiology at the University of Pennsylvania, within a highly collaborative scientific environment that provides access to world-class facilities for proteomics, metabolomics, imaging, and mouse genetics. Our lab emphasizes rigorous science, intellectual independence, and strong mentorship supporting diverse career trajectories. Required Qualifications PhD in Biology, Biochemistry, Cell Biology, Physiology, Neuroscience, or a related field Demonstrated research experience in mitochondrial biology At least one first-author primary research publication in a peer-reviewed journal Strong experimental and analytical skills in molecular or cellular biology Ability to independently design and execute experiments Preferred Qualifications Experience working with mammalian systems and/or mouse models Exposure to mass spectrometry, proteomics, metabolomics or stable-isotope tracing or metabolic analysis Background in neuroscience or complex tissue physiology Computational/data analysis skills (R, Python, or similar) Applicants should submit a CV, cover letter, a PDF with relevant publication(s), and provide 3 contacts for references. In the cover letter, applicants should describe their research experience and indicate the experimental direction they would like to pursue in the lab. Candidates requiring visa sponsorship are welcome to apply"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/175355": {
      "title": "Postoctoral Researcher",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: Perelman School of Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania - School of Medicine Faculty Mentor: Samantha Piekos, Ph.D. Department: Biostatistics, Epidemiology and Informatics Funding Sources: Grant Number of Positions : 1 Open to applications from U.S. citizens and foreign nationals. The University of Pennsylvania, the largest private employer in Philadelphia, is a world-renowned leader in education, research, and innovation. This historic, Ivy League school consistently ranks among the top 10 universities in the annual U.S. News & World Report survey. Penn has 12 highly-regarded schools that provide opportunities for undergraduate, graduate and continuing education, all influenced by Penn’s distinctive interdisciplinary approach to scholarship and learning. Penn offers a unique working environment within the city of Philadelphia. The University is situated on a beautiful urban campus, with easy access to a range of educational, cultural, and recreational activities. With its historical significance and landmarks, lively cultural offerings, and wide variety of atmospheres, Philadelphia is the perfect place to call home for work and play. Duties and Responsibilities Integrate and analyze longitudinal multi-omics datasets (lipidomics, metabolomics, proteomics, transcriptomics) from blood, urine, and placenta with vaginal and gut microbiome, wearable, clinical and environmental data. Perform network analyses, including community structure modeling, to characterize physiological changes across pregnancy. Identify molecular disruptions in obstetric syndromes (e.g., preeclampsia, gestational hypertension, preterm birth). Apply trajectory modeling and outlier detection to identify earliest deviations from healthy pregnancy pathways. Collaborate with clinical and experimental teams to generate biologically meaningful insights. Develop independence through activities including, but not limited to, writing peer-reviewed publications for research conferences and journals, giving presentations at research conferences, and assisting in grant proposal development. The position is open until filled. The initial appointment is for two years, with the possibility of extension for a third year based on performance and mutual agreement. The University of Pennsylvania is an EOE. Minorities/Women/Individuals with disabilities/Protected Veterans are encouraged to apply. Required Qualifications Ph.D. in Bioinformatics, Computational Biology, Computer Science, Systems Biology, Statistics, or a related field. Demonstrated expertise in data integration, machine learning, or network biology. Experience with large-scale omics data analysis. Proficiency in R and/or Python programming. Strong statistical and modeling skills. Excellent communication and writing abilities. Preferred Qualifications Experience in multi-omics integration frameworks and dimensionality reduction. Familiarity with longitudinal or epidemiological study design. Background in systems biology of complex physiological processes. Knowledge of AI/ML methods for trajectory prediction and anomaly detection. Interest in maternal-fetal health, developmental biology, or reproductive biology. Review of applications will begin shortly and will continue until the position is filled. Applications should be sent through Interforlio using the link below and include: Cover Letter Curriculum vitae Research Statement (2-pages) Contact Information for three references"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194199": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellowship in Philosophy, Politics and Economics University of Pennsylvania",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Arts and Sciences: School of Arts and Sciences Centers and Institutes: Philosophy, Politics and Economics Location: PPE Program, University of Pennylvania (Philadelphia, PA) The Philosophy, Politics and Economics (PPE) Program at the University of Pennsylvania invites applications for a postdoctoral fellowship beginning Fall 2027 (appointment start date July 1, 2027). The fellowship lasts one year, with the possibility of annual renewal for up to four additional years, contingent upon a satisfactory performance review and funding. This position is designed to support a talented economist working in behavioral or experimental economics, or a related PPE area. Duties include pursuing an independent research agenda and teaching three (3) courses each academic year, consisting of two (2) advanced interdisciplinary seminars and one (1) research seminar focused on training a small cohort of students in field-specific research methods. Seminar topics will be developed in consultation with the program and should reflect the fellow’s expertise while engaging substantively with PPE. Applicants must have completed a PhD in Economics or a closely related field by the start of the appointment, along with a demonstrated record of interdisciplinary engagement, a strong research trajectory, and prior teaching experience. Application Materials Cover letter describing research interests, current projects, and fit with the PPE Program Curriculum vitae Teaching dossier Two course descriptions for upper level undergraduate seminars (approx 150 words each) Two letters of recommendation Interviews are anticipated to take place in December and January. For questions, please contact ppe-program@sas.upenn.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189518": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow in Individualized Care for At-Risk Older Adults",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: School of Nursing Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania School of Nursing The NewCourtland Center for Transitions and Health (NCTH) at the University of Pennsylvania School of Nursing invites applications for a postdoctoral fellowship through the National Institute of Nursing Research (NINR)-funded Ruth L. Kirschstein National Research Service Award (NRSA) Training Program in Individualized Care for At-Risk Older Adults (T32NR009356). The program prepares the next generation of nurse scientists to conduct innovative research that improves the health, healthcare experiences, and quality of life of older adults and their family caregivers during complex health and healthcare transitions. Fellows receive individualized mentorship from nationally recognized interdisciplinary faculty and advanced training in research methods, implementation science, dissemination and translation, grant writing, leadership, project management, bioethics, and team science. The program is designed to support the development of independent nurse scientists committed to advancing evidence-based care for older adults and their caregivers. Dates (Deadlines and Anticipating Hiring Date) : August 2, 2026 (earliest start date) and Applications are considered on a rolling basis. Qualifications/Skills: PhD or equivalent doctoral degree in Nursing (completed prior to appointment start date) Active professional nursing license Commitment to full-time research training Strong interest in aging, chronic illness, family caregiving, health transitions, or nursing science Excellent written and verbal communication skills Interest in pursuing an independent program of research Eligible candidates must be a U.S. citizen or noncitizen national or have been lawfully admitted for permanent residence at the time of appointment. Postdoctoral applicants should hold a professional nursing license and have completed a doctoral degree in nursing, by the beginning date of their appointment. All fellows are required to pursue their research training full‐time. Application Instructions: Completed application cover page Personal statement Professional development plan Brief description of research project Three letters of reference Other supporting documents Application details and submission materials can be found at: https://www.nursing.upenn.edu/ncth/about-us/mentorships-fellowships/ Any questions can be directed to Amber Vas (ambvas@nursing.upenn.edu)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193922": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow in Senescence, Aging and Inflammation",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: School of Dental Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania - Penn Dental Medicine The Opportunity A postdoctoral position is available in the laboratory of Dr. Dana T. Graves at the University of Pennsylvania School of Dental Medicine, Department of Periodontics. The fellow will investigate mechanisms by which cellular senescence and inflammation interact to promote age-associated pathology. Prior research experience in cellular senescence is strongly preferred, particularly investigation of senescence-associated secretory phenotype (SASP), senolytics, p16/p21 pathways, or experimental identification and manipulation of senescent cells. The position integrates experimental biology with single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq), 10x Genomics Xenium spatial transcriptomics, quantitative imaging, and molecular and cellular validation to define disease-associated cell states, signaling networks, and potential therapeutic targets. Research Focus The research program will examine how senescent cell states and inflammatory signaling contribute to age-associated periodontal pathology using a periodontal mouse model and human tissue validation. The fellow will participate in in vivo studies and analyses of scRNA-seq and 10x Genomics Xenium datasets to identify senescence- and inflammation-associated cell states, transcriptional programs, cell-cell communication networks, spatially organized cellular responses, and mechanisms that lead to greater bone resorption. A major emphasis will be the experimental characterization and manipulation of senescent cells and their signaling programs, SASP and p16/p21-associated pathways. The analyses will include pathway analysis, trajectory and pseudotime analysis, regulatory-network inference, and ligand-receptor analysis. Experimental studies will include genetically engineered mouse models, primary mouse and human cell cultures, molecular perturbation studies, histology, immunofluorescence, flow cytometry, quantitative image analysis, and other approaches used to validate computational findings and define mechanisms linking aging, senescence, inflammation, and altered tissue function. Key Responsibilities · Investigate molecular and cellular mechanisms linking senescence and inflammation to tissue dysfunction, repair, regeneration, and bone resorption using in vivo mouse models, including experimental approaches to identify, characterize, and manipulate senescent cells. Contribute to bioinformatic analyses of scRNA-seq and 10x Genomics Xenium spatial transcriptomic datasets. · Develop and execute rigorous experimental approaches using in vivo, ex vivo, and/or in vitro models as appropriate to the project. · Perform quality control, data integration, cell annotation, differential expression, pathway, trajectory, state-transition, and ligand-receptor analyses. · Integrate transcriptomic findings with imaging, histologic, cellular, and phenotypic measurements. · Use immunofluorescence and quantitative image analysis to characterize cellular localization, spatial distribution, and tissue organization. · Develop rigorous, reproducible experimental and computational workflows. · Interpret findings in biological and disease contexts and contribute to analytical and experimental strategy. · Generate clear figures, present findings, and communicate effectively with computational and experimental collaborators. · Prepare first-author manuscripts and contribute to grant development and collaborative studies. Computational and Experimental Environment For single-cell and spatial genomics studies, the primary computational environment uses R, Seurat, and related tools, with other validated methods used when they improve analytical rigor or biological interpretation. Quantitative imaging and spatial-analysis tools will be selected based on the specimens, imaging modalities, and scientific questions. Candidates with strong experimental backgrounds will have opportunities to develop additional expertise in computational analysis, while candidates with strong computational backgrounds will have opportunities to gain experience with biological validation and experimental systems. Research Environment and Career Development The Graves laboratory integrates computational discovery with in vivo models, human specimens, histology, flow cytometry, immunofluorescence, primary cell culture, molecular perturbation studies, and quantitative analysis. The fellow will have substantial intellectual ownership of their project, including development of experimental directions, selection of analytical approaches, interpretation and presentation of findings, preparation of first-author manuscripts, and participation in grant writing. Development of independent fellowship applications is encouraged. Dr. Graves will provide direct scientific mentoring and regular project guidance. The fellow will collaborate with investigators and shared-resource specialists across the University of Pennsylvania. Penn core facilities support single-cell and spatial genomics, biostatistics, imaging, histology, and quantitative analysis. The position is designed to support scientific independence, strong publications, grant development, and preparation for an academic or industry career. Required Qualifications · A PhD, MD, DMD, DVM, or equivalent doctoral degree in a relevant field. · A strong background in cell biology, molecular biology, immunology, skeletal biology, bioengineering, genomics, computational biology, bioinformatics, systems biology, or a related discipline. · Demonstrated scientific rigor and the ability to interpret experimental or computational findings in a biological context. · Ability to work independently and collaborate effectively across disciplines. · Strong scientific writing, presentation, and communication skills. Preferred Qualifications · Prior research experience in cellular senescence is strongly preferred. · Experience with aging and inflammation or related areas. · Experience with mouse genetics and disease models, molecular and cellular assays, histology, immunofluorescence, flow cytometry, or quantitative image analysis. · Experience with scRNA-seq or spatial transcriptomics. · Experience integrating molecular, imaging, histologic, and phenotypic data. · Candidates with a strong experimental background who wish to develop expertise in single-cell and spatial genomics, or computational candidates who wish to expand their experimental expertise, are encouraged to apply. Selected Publications · Diabetes exacerbates destructive inflammation by activating the CD137L-CD137 axis. Journal of Clinical Investigation. PMID: 41379565. · Single Cell Sequencing Identifies Distinct Cellular Alterations in Impaired Aged and Diabetic Wounds. Aging Cell. PMID: 41189300. · Ko KI et al. NF-kappaB perturbation reveals unique immunomodulatory functions in Prx1-positive fibroblasts that promote development of atopic dermatitis. Science Translational Medicine. 2022. PMID: 35108061. Appointment and Application ·   Funding: The principal investigator has multiple RO1 grant support through 2031. ·   Start date: Available immediately following interviews and reference review. ·   Application materials: Submit a curriculum vitae, a brief statement describing research experience and future interests, and the names and contact information of three references. ·   Contact: Jen East, jeneast@upenn.edu"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189181": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow in Skeletal Biology, Bioinformatics, and Regenerative Medicine",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: School of Dental Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania - School of Dental Medicine The Embree Lab is recruiting a talented and highly motivated Postdoctoral Scientist to contribute to our NIH-funded research program in skeletal biology, osteoarthritis, pain biology, joint function, and regenerative medicine. Our group integrates mouse genetics, large-animal models, biomaterials, single-cell and spatial sequencing, and bioinformatics to uncover the cellular and molecular mechanisms governing joint development, degeneration, pain behaviors, and functional outcomes. PhD, MD/PhD, DDS/PhD, DMD/PhD, or equivalent doctoral degree. Preferred expertise in bioengineering, developmental/stem cell biology, regenerative medicine,  bioinformatics, or a related field. Demonstrated record of scientific productivity, including peer-reviewed publications. Proficiency in statistical analysis and/or programming languages such as R or Python is desirable. Excellent written and oral communication skills, strong organizational skills, and the ability to work independently as well as collaboratively in a multidisciplinary research environment. Demonstrated commitment to scientific rigor, innovation, and mentorship. Applicants should submit a cover letter describing their research interests and career goals, a current curriculum vitae, and the names and contact information for 2–3 professional references . Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189976": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher – Immunology / Oral Biology",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: School of Dental Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania - School of Dental Medicine The Ko Laboratory at the University of Pennsylvania School of Dental Medicine is seeking a highly motivated postdoctoral fellow to join an NIH-funded research program investigating host–microbe interactions at the oral epithelial barrier. Our research uses periodontitis as a model to understand how excess iron resulting from chronic inflammation alters epithelial and stromal cell function, promotes inflammatory cell death, and drives tissue destruction. We integrate genetically engineered mouse models, clinically derived human tissues, immunology, spatial and single-cell molecular profiling, and computational biology to uncover mechanisms linking iron-dependent cell death with chronic inflammatory disease. The successful candidate will have the opportunity to work in a collaborative, multidisciplinary environment at Penn Dental Medicine's Levy Building and interact with investigators across Penn's vibrant biomedical research community. What We Offer Training in cutting-edge approaches including human tissue biology, immunology, and spatial/single-cell technologies Opportunities to develop interdisciplinary collaborations across the University of Pennsylvania Mentorship focused on scientific independence, grant writing, career development, and publication A collaborative and supportive research environment Required Qualifications Ph.D., D.M.D./Ph.D., M.D./Ph.D., or equivalent degree in Immunology, Oral Biology, Cell Biology, Molecular Biology, or a related field Demonstrated ability to independently design, execute, and interpret scientific experiments Strong written and verbal communication skills Ability to work both independently and collaboratively in a multidisciplinary research environment Preferred Qualifications Experience with murine models of disease Experience with cell culture and in vitro immunological assays Surgical or microdissection experience Experience with computational biology or bioinformatics (R, Python, or related platforms) Track record of peer-reviewed publications What We Offer Training in cutting-edge approaches including human tissue biology, immunology, and spatial/single-cell technologies Opportunities to develop interdisciplinary collaborations across the University of Pennsylvania Mentorship focused on scientific independence, grant writing, career development, and publication A collaborative and supportive research environment Interested applicants should submit: A curriculum vitae (CV) A cover letter describing previous research experience, scientific interests, and long-term career goals Contact information for three professional references Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194436": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researchers,  Baslan Lab",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: School of Veterinary Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania - Penn Vet Faculty Mentor: Timour Baslan, Ph.D. Department: Biomedical Sciences Positions are funded by various grants: federal as well as foundation. Five positions The positions are open to US citizens and foreign nationals. The Baslan Lab at the Department of Biomedical Sciences, the University of Pennsylvania (Penn) is recruiting Postdoctoral Fellows (computational & experimental), 5 positions in total, to join our multidisciplinary team of investigators.  Our work focuses on studying cancer genomes, with a specific attention to the genetics and biology of a cryptic class of cancer associated mutations termed Copy Number Alterations (CNAs). These somatic events change the number and composition of chromosomes in cancer cells to yield cancer and immune biology that is almost entirely unknown. To dissect these events, we employ a systems biology, top-down approach where advanced analytics of large datasets are coupled to focused perturbations (ex: shRNA, CRISPR, cDNA screens) in experimental model systems (e.g. organoid & cell line models). We also apply a bottom-up approach where we employ cutting edge methods to dissect novel model systems we develop (ex: mouse models). Some of the techniques our group develops and applies are: - advanced sequencing technologies, at single cell and bulk levels, to generate large multi-modal datasets. - novel algorithms & computational approaches, based on maths, statistics, & machine learning, to nominate novel cancer therapeutic targets. - innovative, molecularly engineered model systems in in-vitro and in-vivo settings (e.g. mouse models) - highly parallel perturbation approaches; CRISPR, shRNA, PROTACs, small molecules We aim to advance: - novel therapeutics; targeted as well as immunotherapies - early diagnostic as well as prognostic tools - novel technologies that can be leveraged to address outstanding biological questions Disease areas of focus include, but are not limited to, acute leukemias, sarcomas, as well as breast and pancreatic cancers. The training/mentorship opportunities are in the following areas (among others): - Developing computational and chemical biology approaches to advance copy number informed precision therapies. - Studying cancer-immune cell interactions during cancer initiation and progression using functional biology approaches & advanced in-situ sequencing/data analytics. - Developing machine learning approaches to advance novel prognostic and diagnostic methods in cancer. - Dissecting cancer genome evolution using the first of their kind, somatic lineage tracing mouse models, along with human data analysis. - Developing single-cell as well as circulating tumor free DNA sequencing technologies. - Designing and applying novel comparative oncogenomic algorithms to advance unifying principles of chromosomal biology in cancer. Prospective trainees can expect the followings: Mentorship, Freedom, Resources, Exciting Science, & Fun. Further details can be found here: http://baslanlab.com (1) A proven record of publication (this can include bioRxiv and/or arXiv papers, or about to publish), (2) For experimentalists, skills in one, or all of the following areas are required: molecular biology, cell culture, and animal work, for computational positions, skills in one or all of the following areas are required: next generation sequencing data analysis, statistics, and machine learning. Start date and term are negotiable. Candidates should have a Ph.D. Applicants are required to submit the following materials through Interfolio: CV Cover letter A list of 3 referees Questions or follow up inquiries can be emailed to Dr. Baslan at tbaslan@upenn.edu"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/184735": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Scholar in Viral Immunology",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: School of Veterinary Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania - School of Veterinary Medicine Faculty Mentor: Oyebola Oyesola, DVM, PhD Department: Pathobiology Funding Source: Institutional funds. Shortlisted scholars will be encouraged to apply for external fellowship and funds. Number of Positions: 1 Open to applications from U.S. citizens and foreign nationals. We are seeking a highly motivated Postdoctoral Scholar to join an interdisciplinary research program focused on viral and parasite immunology. The successful candidate will investigate immune responses to viral and parasitic infection (with an opportunity to learn this on the job), with an emphasis on mechanisms of host defense and the effect of an individual’s infection history on subsequent exposure. The position offers a positive research environment with access to state-of-the-art facilities and opportunities for collaborations but within and outside University of Pennsylvania. PhD in Immunology, Virology, Microbiology, or related field.  Evidence of scientific productivity evidenced by is at least one first-author publication either in preprint or already published in a peer-reviewed journal.  Experience with viral infection models is a requirement for this position. An open mind and willingness to learn new skills are preferred. Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Applications should be sent through Interfolio using the link below and include: Cover letter; Curriculum vitae; Letters of recommendation from 3 individuals, including the contact information for the referee"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192503": {
      "title": "Anthropology Tenured/Tenure-Track Position in Indigenous Studies Department of Anthropology, University of Pennsylvania",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Arts and Sciences: School of Arts and Sciences - Anthropology Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Anthropology at the University of Pennsylvania (Penn) invites applications for an open rank (Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor) position in Indigenous Studies, beginning in the 2027/28 academic year.  We are particularly interested in candidates who explore the relation between coloniality, indigeneity, contemporary politics, and deep time in a global perspective. Scholars working on research areas that include sovereignty and governance, dispossession and extraction, climate and environment justice, law, health and healing, media and communication, and/or science and technology studies are encouraged to apply. We also welcome applications from scholars who work with collaborative, feminist and decolonial methodologies and projects that focus on Indigenous cultural heritage, material culture, and community collaboration in ways that may build relations with the Penn Museum ’s collections and research areas. The successful candidate will have an active research and publication record advancing the field, that both builds on and expands the Department’s strengths, while also enhancing departmental goals of innovative and collaborative research that crosses sub-disciplinary lines.  Teaching responsibilities will include both undergraduate courses and graduate classes, and a teaching load of four courses per year. The candidate will also participate in the mentorship of undergraduate and graduate students working in these areas. The candidate’s research and teaching should also contribute to Penn’s Native American and Indigenous Studies (NAIS) Initiative.  Candidates must have a PhD in Anthropology or a related discipline at the time of beginning the appointment. About the Department The Department of Anthropology at the University of Pennsylvania is a collaborative and interactive department. Our research engages with a range of issues that cut across anthropology’s subdisciplines, including media and communication, migration and demographics, science and technology, health and environment, heritage and identity, race and gender, violence and social control, poverty and rights, and political and economic development. This research informs the courses we teach, and the students we advise. Our department is housed within the School of Arts & Sciences , which is comprised of 28 academic departments, the College of Arts and Sciences , the Graduate Division , and the College of Liberal and Professional Studies .  Interdisciplinary research and collaboration are supported as much by the breadth and depth of academic pursuits at Penn as well as the proximity of resources on our contiguous green urban campus in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania.  We promote and encourage curiosity, intellectual engagement, and discovery among all our students and faculty. Interested candidates should submit the following materials at apply.interfolio.com/192503 a. Cover letter (2-3 pages) b. CV c. Research Statement (3-5 pages) d. Contact information for three individuals who have agreed to provide letters of recommendation e. Longlisted applicants will be invited to submit a writing sample later in the search process (a chapter or article of no more than 40 pages) The review of applications will begin on October 15, 2026, and will continue until filled. Please contact Colleen Lawson (215.898.7443/ lawsonco@sas.upenn.edu) with questions regarding the application process."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192265": {
      "title": "Associate Professor/Professor of Anthropology and Associate Curator/Curator and Director of the Penn Cultural Heritage Center at the Penn Museum",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Arts and Sciences: School of Arts and Sciences - Anthropology Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Anthropology and the Penn Museum at the University of Pennsylvania (Penn) invite applications for a tenured faculty position and Curator/Director of the Penn Cultural Heritage Center (PennCHC) beginning in the 2027–28 academic year. We seek to appoint an associate or full Professor. The successful candidate should have expertise in archaeology and cultural heritage. Candidates should have a record of bringing together academics with non-academic stakeholders as well as organizing and managing cultural heritage initiatives. While the area of specialization is open, we are particularly interested in candidates with active research in the Americas and/or Africa who situate their research within global heritage scholarship. The Department of Anthropology is housed within the School of Arts & Sciences , which is comprised of 28 academic departments, the College of Arts and Sciences , the Graduate Division , and the College of Liberal and Professional Studies . We promote and encourage curiosity, intellectual engagement, and discovery among all our students and faculty. The Department offers an undergraduate major that provides students with an understanding of the modern discipline of anthropology and a Ph.D. program that trains students across the subdisciplines of anthropology. Within the Department, the candidate’s research should articulate with existing departmental faculty expertise in archaeology, while also contributing to departmental goals of innovative and collaborative research that crosses sub-disciplinary lines. Teaching responsibilities will include both undergraduate and graduate courses in the area of research specialization. Faculty-curators have their teaching load reduced from four to two courses per year. The candidate will also participate in the mentorship of undergraduate and graduate students working in these areas. In the Penn Museum, the candidate will serve as associate curator/curator in the Curatorial Section closest to their research specialization, and in the position of Director of the PennCHC. As curator, the candidate will participate in exhibition, research, education, and outreach activities related to the collections within the Section. As head of the PennCHC, the Director will lead or oversee a growing portfolio of global cultural heritage projects with PennCHC principal investigators, including a national survey of museum collecting practices, convening initiatives, and cultural rights programming funded through an IMLS National Leadership Grant through 2028. Additionally, the Director will seek opportunities to collaborate closely with colleagues who run other cultural heritage research endeavors across the Penn Museum and Penn, and to work with them to position the PennCHC as a research and convening center for discussion of heritage-related activities. The Director will create and implement a new, ambitious strategic plan for the Center, in consultation with Penn CHC’s Director of Research and Programs and the Museum’s Williams Director and Executive Team , that will centralize and build greater capacity for all of these cultural heritage endeavors at the Penn Museum and the University of Pennsylvania. Qualifications include: ·       Expertise in anthropological archaeology and cultural heritage. ·       Experience organizing and managing cultural heritage initiatives. ·       Successful track record of significant scholarship as evidenced by: o   Research/publication record o   External funding record o   Teaching at both the graduate and undergraduate levels and mentoring and supporting a diverse student body o   The direction of graduate/undergraduate research for students from a wide range of backgrounds ·       Candidates are expected to have PhD in anthropology or a related discipline ·       Please provide the following application materials to apply.interfolio.com/192265 : o   CV o   Letter of Application ·       Review of applications will begin on November 1, 2026. ·       Please contact Colleen Lawson ( lawsonco@sas.upenn.edu , (215) 898-7443) with questions regarding the application process"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192648": {
      "title": "Chemistry Department, Assistant Professor in Organic Chemistry",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Arts and Sciences: School of Arts and Sciences - Chemistry Location: Philadelphia, Pennsylvania Chemistry Department, Assistant Professor in Organic Chemistry University of Pennsylvania: School of Arts and Sciences The Department of Chemistry at the University of Pennsylvania (Penn) invites applications for a tenure-track position at the Assistant Professor level. The appointment will be in the broadly defined area of Organic Chemistry. The candidate is expected to establish an externally funded research program and participate in the Department’s undergraduate and graduate teaching mission. Penn Chemistry, a department in the School of Arts & Sciences (SAS), is collaborative and interactive. The traditional sub-fields of biological, inorganic, organic, and physical chemistry serve as a framework from which experimental and theoretical research emerge. Interdisciplinary research and collaboration are supported across the campus, as chemistry faculty actively collaborate with others in SAS as well with researchers in the Perelman School of Medicine (PSOM) and the School of Engineering and Applied Science (SEAS), all part of our contiguous green urban campus in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania. We promote and encourage curiosity, intellectual engagement, and discovery among all our students and faculty . Qualifications include: ● Broad training in areas of chemistry relevant to organic chemistry ● Strong research potential ● Capacity for teaching at both the graduate and undergraduate levels and mentoring and supporting a diverse student body ● The ability to direct graduate/undergraduate research ● Candidates are expected to have completed a PhD and postdoctoral experience by the time of appointment, which is July 1, 2027 ● The successful candidate will be expected to establish an externally funded research program Application instructions/Required documents: ● Please provide the following application materials: o   A curriculum vitae, including a list of publications o   A brief research summary o   A description of proposed research o   One statement describing your past and planned teaching and mentoring, including the general area(s) of chemistry (e.g. organic, biological, inorganic, and/or physical chemistry) that you would feel comfortable teaching. o   Contact information for three recommenders. Review of applications will begin November 1, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Please contact chem-info@sas.upenn.edu with questions regarding the application process. Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Equal Employment Opportunity and Nondiscrimination Statement The University of Pennsylvania seeks talented students, faculty and staff with a wide variety of backgrounds, experiences, and perspectives. The University of Pennsylvania does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, sex, sexual orientation, religion, creed, national origin (including shared ancestry or ethnic characteristics), citizenship status, age, disability, veteran status or any other class protected under applicable federal, state or local law in the administration of its admissions, financial aid, educational or athletic programs, or other University-administered programs or in its employment practices. Questions or complaints regarding this policy should be directed to the Executive Director of the Office of Equal Opportunity Programs; Franklin Building, 3451 Walnut Street, Suite 421, Philadelphia, PA 19104-6106; or (215) 898-6993 (Voice)."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192647": {
      "title": "Chemistry Department, Assistant Professor of Chemistry",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Arts and Sciences: School of Arts and Sciences - Chemistry Location: Philadelphia, Pennsylvania The Department of Chemistry at the University of Pennsylvania (Penn) invites applications for a tenure-track position in the broadly defined area of energy research. This field encompasses the traditional areas of Chemistry with an emphasis on fundamental and applied topics that relate to energy conversion, energy utilization, energy-related materials, and sustainability. We are particularly interested in candidates who develop and/or utilize advanced experimental methodologies to address pressing challenges in energy science . Penn Chemistry, a department in the School of Arts & Sciences (SAS), is collaborative and interactive. The traditional sub-fields of physical, biological, inorganic, and organic chemistry serve as a framework from which experimental and theoretical research emerge. Interdisciplinary research and collaboration are supported across the campus, as chemistry faculty actively collaborate with others in SAS as well with researchers in the School of Engineering and Applied Science (SEAS) and the Perelman School of Medicine, all part of our contiguous green urban campus in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania.  Chemistry research at Penn greatly benefits from a dynamic energy research environment, led by the Vagelos Institute for Energy Sciences and Technology , a collaboration between SAS and SEAS, as well as through close interactions with our energy-relevant dual-degree program, the Vagelos Integrated Program in Energy Research ( VIPER ) and with our NSF-funded Materials Research Science and Engineering Center, the Laboratory for Research on the Structure of Matter . We promote and encourage curiosity, intellectual engagement, and discovery among all our students and faculty . Qualifications include: ● Broad training in areas of chemistry relevant to energy, environment, and/or sustainability ● Strong research potential ● Capacity for teaching at both the graduate and undergraduate levels and mentoring and supporting a diverse student body ● The ability to direct graduate/undergraduate research ● Candidates are expected to have completed a PhD and postdoctoral experience by the time of appointment, which is July 1, 2027 ● The successful candidate will be expected to establish an externally funded research program Application instructions/Required documents: ● Please provide the following application materials: o   A curriculum vitae, including a list of publications o   A brief research summary o   A description of proposed research o   One statement describing your past and planned teaching and mentoring, including the general area(s) of chemistry (e.g. organic, biological, inorganic, and/or physical chemistry) that you would feel comfortable teaching. o   Contact information for three recommenders. Review of applications will begin November 1, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Please contact chem-info@sas.upenn.edu with questions regarding the application process. Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Equal Employment Opportunity and Nondiscrimination Statement The University of Pennsylvania seeks talented students, faculty and staff with a wide variety of backgrounds, experiences, and perspectives. The University of Pennsylvania does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, sex, sexual orientation, religion, creed, national origin (including shared ancestry or ethnic characteristics), citizenship status, age, disability, veteran status or any other class protected under applicable federal, state or local law in the administration of its admissions, financial aid, educational or athletic programs, or other University-administered programs or in its employment practices. Questions or complaints regarding this policy should be directed to the Executive Director of the Office of Equal Opportunity Programs; Franklin Building, 3451 Walnut Street, Suite 421, Philadelphia, PA 19104-6106; or (215) 898-6993 (Voice)."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194622": {
      "title": "Part-time Lecturer in the Japanese Language Program",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Arts and Sciences: School of Arts and Sciences - East Asian Languages and Civilizations Location: Philadelphia, Pennsylvania The Department of East Asian Languages and Civilizations at the University of Pennsylvania invites applications for a part-time Lecturer in the Japanese Language Program for the Spring 2027 semester. Applicants for the position should demonstrate a primary focus on language education and have received at least a master’s degree in education, Japanese Language, Literature, Linguistics, or a related field.  Native or near-native competency in the Japanese language and fluency in English are required. Preference will be given to applicants with significant teaching experience at all levels of Japanese language at post-secondary institutions in the United States. Knowledge of and experience in current trends in second/foreign language teaching methodologies and meaningful application of technologies in language instruction are highly desirable. Duties include teaching Japanese language classes (three classes (3CU) per semester) at any assigned levels, holding regular office hours, attending meetings of the Japanese Language Program, and working with the Director of the Language Program and East Asia faculty on curriculum development and program articulation. Please submit: a cover letter, CV, teaching statement, and contact information for a minimum of two individuals who have agreed to provide a recommendation letter; a link to a video recording of a class, together with a description of the video and relevant materials.  We also encourage the applicants to upload recent teaching evaluations including written comments, if available. The review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. The School of Arts and Sciences at the University of Pennsylvania is committed to cultivating a vibrant community of students, scholars, researchers, and staff and to upholding the highest standards of academic excellence. The School and the Department of East Asian Languages and Civilizations strive to create working and learning environments that foster intellectual growth and encourage meaningful connections within our Penn academic community and beyond."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193194": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow in Adversity and Health at the University of Pennsylvania's Population Studies Center (PSC)",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Arts and Sciences: School of Arts and Sciences - Sociology Location: University of Pennsylvania The Malawi Longitudinal Study of Families and Health (MLSFH; https://www.MLSFHresearch.org) at the University of Pennsylvania seeks applicants for a postdoctoral researcher to support a NICHD funded 5-year project on Adversities, Health and Resilience in Early Adulthood. This novel project builds on a quarter-century of social and contextual MLSFH data. Starting in 2017, the project captured prospective data from a new cohort of over 2,000 MLSFH children as they moved through adolescence. They are now entering young adulthood, and the next phase of the project will examine how earlier childhood adversity influences sexual, reproductive and mental health , and how it is transmitted across generations to their children. The new study will provide unparalleled insights into how adversities exert lasting impacts across the life course and across generations, and identify key resilience processes that can inform policies The postdoc will work closely with Hans-Peter Kohler ( hpkohler@sas.upenn.edu ) at the University of Pennsylvania, Rachel Kidman ( Rachel.Kidman@stonybrookmedicine.edu ) at Stony Brook University, and other MLSFH collaborating partners in the USA and Malawi. The postdoctoral scholar will have the opportunity to engage in this innovative MLSFH project from in its analyses stage. Through this project, the postdoctoral fellow will gain access to newly collected 2026 data that provide exceptional research opportunities and facilitate long-term career building. To apply, please submit cover letter, resume/CV, writing sample, and two letters of recommendation via apply.interfolio.com/193194 Responsibilities include: Analyze longitudinal MLSFH data on the adolescent/young adult cohort to estimate the impacts of adverse childhood experience (ACEs) on health both across the life course and across generations Collaborate with a multidisciplinary and international research team and with community stakeholders Disseminate research findings at scientific conferences and stakeholder meetings Draft manuscripts for scientific publication Provide technical and written assistance on grants for NIH and other external funding The University of Pennsylvania is committed to creating a community of students, scholars, and staff that reflects the diversity of the world we live in. We create working and learning environments that are affirming, equitable, and inclusive. As a community, we value dynamic interactions and thoughtful discussions, and strive for an environment where everyone is supported and valued. For information on the School of Arts and Sciences’ inclusion and anti-racism initiatives, please see: https://www.sas.upenn.edu/2020-inclusion-and-anti-racism-initiatives Qualifications Ph.D. in epidemiology, economics, demography, sociology, psychology and/or related fields Interest in global health, childhood adversity, sexual and reproductive health and/or life course analyses Skills in quantitative data and econometric/statistical analysis Excellent English verbal and written skills Strong organizational skills and ability to establish priorities to meet deadlines Ability to work in a multidisciplinary setting with an international research team To apply, please submit cover letter, resume/CV, writing sample, and two letters of recommendation via apply.interfolio.com/193194"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/176322": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow in Trans Studies",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Arts and Sciences: School of Arts and Sciences Centers and Institutes: The Center for Research in Feminist, Queer, and Transgender Studies Location: Philadelphia, PA The Gender, Sexuality, and Women’s Studies Program (GSWS) along with the Center for Research in Feminist, Queer, and Transgender Studies (FQT) invite applicants for a Postdoctoral Fellowship in Trans Studies, beginning August 1, 2026. This is a one-year position, with the possibility of a one-year extension, contingent upon funding and performance. We seek an interdisciplinary scholar and teacher with a demonstrated commitment to inclusive pedagogy and feminist, queer, and trans studies. We welcome expertise disability studies, black studies, trans of color critique or transnational/global approaches. The successful candidate’s scholarly work and teaching must engage with some portion of the broad academic mission of FQT/GSWS. Our intellectual community includes visiting scholars and artists in residence, graduate student associates, and faculty affiliates from across the university. The successful applicant will be expected to contribute to this community through attendance at/participation in our events, including invited lectures, pedagogy seminars, teach-ins, and works-in-progress. The postdoctoral fellow will have significant opportunities to develop skills as a teacher, scholar, and collaborator in FQT/GSWS public programming and to be mentored by staff and faculty: ●      Teaching One to two courses will be required during the fellowship year, determined in part through program needs and the fellow’s expertise. ●      Scholarly engagement through a Work-in-Progress presentation and possible support (contingent on budget constraints) to plan their own symposium event Application materials: Applications should include a cover letter, CV, research statement, writing sample of up to 40 pages, description of one proposed undergraduate course (approx. 300 words), and the names and contact information for 3 referees (letters to be requested for candidates who progress to a later stage of consideration). Consideration of applications will begin on January 15th, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. The postdoc will receive an annual salary of $66,300 plus benefits, and up to $3,000 for research expenses. Applications are due on December 15, 2025. The School of Arts and Sciences at the University of Pennsylvania is committed to cultivating a vibrant community of students, scholars, researchers, and staff and to upholding the highest standards of academic excellence. The School and The Center for Research in Feminist, Queer, and Transgender Studies and Program in Gender, Sexuality, and Women’s Studies strive to create working and learning environments that foster intellectual growth and encourage meaningful connections within our Penn academic community and beyond."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191793": {
      "title": "Tenure-track Assistant Professor of Cinema and Media Studies",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Arts and Sciences: School of Arts and Sciences - Cinema and Media Studies Location: Philadelphia, Pennsylvania The Department of Cinema & Media Studies at the University of Pennsylvania in Philadelphia invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor in the field of Global South media studies with expertise in Latin American, African, and/or African diasporic media cultures. Welcome subfields include but are not limited to media industries, South-South relations, sound studies, and reception studies. The successful candidate will be expected to teach two courses per semester, including undergraduate and graduate courses that expand the geographic coverage of the department’s course offerings. Teaching responsibilities also include participating in rotations to teach a large introductory lecture in media studies. The Department of Cinema and Media Studies is housed within the School of Arts & Sciences , which is comprised of the College of Arts and Sciences , the Graduate Division , and the College of Liberal and Professional Studies .  Interdisciplinary research and collaboration are supported by the breadth and depth of academic pursuits at Penn as well as the proximity of resources on our contiguous green urban campus in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania.  We promote and encourage curiosity, intellectual engagement, and discovery among all our students and faculty. University, School, and departmental resources that support our efforts include but are not limited to: the Offices of the Vice Provost for Faculty and Vice Provost for Research , PennGlobal , Wellness at Penn , the Center for Teaching and Learning , the Penn Center for Undergraduate Research and Fellowships , and Penn Human Resources . Qualifications: The successful candidate will: provide evidence of strong research potential (and media production experience, if relevant) show a demonstrated commitment to teaching, advising, and mentoring a diverse student body at the graduate and undergraduate levels hold a doctorate in a relevant field by the time of appointment, July 1, 2027 Application instructions / Required Documents: Please provide the following application materials: a cover letter (750 words or less) that addresses research and teaching a curriculum vitae a writing sample (8,000 words or less) 3 confidential letters of recommendation Review of applications will begin on October 1, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Please contact Karen Redrobe (redkaren@sas.upenn.edu ) with questions regarding the application process."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192291": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Assistant Professor of Classical Studies",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Arts and Sciences: School of Arts and Sciences - Classical Studies Location: Philadelphia, Pennsylvania The Department of Classical Studies at the University of Pennsylvania invites applications for a tenure-track position in Latin literature and Roman culture at the rank of Assistant Professor to begin in the 2027–28 academic year. We seek a colleague who will contribute to our ongoing efforts to reimagine the field of Classical Studies through scholarship, teaching, and public engagement. The successful candidate will have an original and generative set of research projects, will be ready to teach Latin language and literature at all levels and provide Classical Studies courses that appeal to a wide range of students, and will have the ability to collaborate productively with colleagues both in and beyond the department. The teaching load is four courses per year, one of which is typically at the graduate level. The PhD must be completed by July 1, 2027. The candidate must be an excellent teacher with a commitment to pedagogy and to the mentorship of students in all our programs. The department offers undergraduate majors and minors in three different tracks of Classical Studies as well as Ancient History, all of which provide students with the opportunity to engage deeply with the diverse ancient Greek and Roman worlds and their receptions, and our undergraduate courses introduce non-majors to a wide range of subject areas in Classical Studies. Our new colleague will work with students in the PhD program in Greek and Latin Languages and Literatures and potentially with graduate students in cognate programs such as Ancient History, Art and Archaeology of the Mediterranean World, and Comparative Literature. Many of our faculty also teach and advise students in Penn’s Postbaccalaureate Program in Classical Studies. More information about all these programs can be found on our website. Our department is committed to responsible and creative research and teaching, an interdisciplinary approach to the ancient world, and inclusive excellence that fosters intellectual engagement inside and outside the academy and across departmental boundaries here at Penn. University resources that support our department and our community include but are not limited to: the Penn Museum ( https://www.penn.museum ), the Center for Ancient Studies ( https://www.sas.upenn.edu/ancient/ ), and the Wolf Humanities Center ( https://wolfhumanities.upenn.edu ). We are proud of our long tradition of contributing to shared governance in the department, the School of Arts and Sciences, and the University, as well as to leadership in professional organizations such as the Society for Classical Studies and the Archaeological Institute of America. Our department is housed within the School of Arts and Sciences, which is comprised of the College of Arts and Sciences, the Graduate Division, and the College of Liberal and Professional Studies. Interdisciplinary research and collaboration are supported by the breadth and depth of academic pursuits at Penn as well as the proximity of resources on our contiguous green urban campus in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania. We promote and encourage curiosity, intellectual engagement, and discovery among all our students and faculty, and campus resources that support this mission include the offices of the Vice Provost for Faculty and Vice Provost for Research, Penn Global, Wellness at Penn, the Center for Teaching and Learning, the Penn Center for Undergraduate Research and Fellowships, and Penn Human Resources. Application Materials Applicants should submit a cover letter; a CV; a writing sample of no more than 30 pages; the names of three referees who will then be prompted to upload letters of recommendation; a teaching statement (1–2 pages) addressing pedagogical priorities, past and potential courses, and any initiatives beyond the classroom; and a research statement (1–2 pages) describing the candidate’s current and future scholarly projects, including any public engagement. Review of applications will begin on October 15, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. We may request further materials from short-listed candidates. We expect to conduct first-round interviews over Zoom, followed by a small number of in-person campus visits. Questions about the position and the search may be addressed to our department coordinator, Sarah Gish-Kraus, gishkrau@sas.upenn.edu , or the chair of the search committee, Prof. James Ker, jker@sas.upenn.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192175": {
      "title": "Tenure-track Assistant Professor of Eastern European and/or Central European Literature and Culture",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Arts and Sciences: School of Arts and Sciences - Russian and East European Studies Location: Philadelphia, PA  The Department of Russian and East European Studies (REES) at the University of Pennsylvania invites applications for a tenure-track position in Eastern European and/or Central European Literature and Culture. We are a collaborative and interactive area studies department, combining social scientists and humanists among its faculty members, that seeks to advance understanding of the societies and cultures of a complex region of the world. REES is housed within the School of Arts & Sciences , which is comprised of the College of Arts and Sciences , the Graduate Division , and the College of Liberal and Professional Studies . Interdisciplinary research and collaboration are supported by the breadth and depth of academic pursuits at Penn as well as the proximity of resources on our contiguous green urban campus in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania. We promote and encourage curiosity, intellectual engagement, and discovery among all our students and faculty. University, School, and departmental resources that support our efforts include but are not limited to: the Offices of the Vice Provost for Faculty and Vice Provost for Research , PennGlobal , the Wolf Humanities Center , Wellness at Penn , the Center for Excellence in Teaching, Learning and Innovation , the Penn Center for Undergraduate Research and Fellowships , and Penn Human Resources . The REES department offers an undergraduate major that provides students a flexible, multidisciplinary understanding of the geographies, histories, and cultures within our purview.  Through departmental curricula as well as those of the Penn Language Center , students have access to education in Russian, Czech, Hungarian, Polish, Ukrainian, Bosnian-Croatian-Serbian, and other languages of the region. We contribute to the education of graduate students in several neighboring disciplines, including Anthropology, Comparative Literature, History, Political Science, and others. Our graduate students often go on to become assistant professors at leading universities. Our faculty also participate in cross-disciplinary undergraduate programs, such as Comparative Literature and Literary Theory , Gender, Sexuality and Women’s Studies , and International Relations . We are especially interested in applicants whose research and teaching reach across national and linguistic borders to engage in the literatures and cultures of more than one Eastern European or Central European society, taking these terms in the broadest possible sense to include locations in the Baltic and Balkan regions in addition to those in between. We seek a dynamic and innovative researcher, educator, and colleague and welcome applicants with any historical focus and topic of research. Qualifications: ● Deep training in one or more literatures and cultures of Eastern and/or Central Europe, broadly conceived, other than Russian literature and culture ● Native or near-native capability in the relevant languages ● Familiarity with Russian language, literature and culture welcome, but not required ● Clear evidence of establishing a high-quality, productive, and original research agenda, appropriate to career stage ● Capacity for teaching at both the graduate and undergraduate levels and mentoring and supporting a diverse student body ● The ability to direct graduate/undergraduate research ● Candidates are expected to have a PhD by the time of appointment, which is July 1, 2027 Application instructions/Required documents: o Cover Letter o CV o A statement on research (2 pp.) o A statement on teaching (2 pp.) o Contact information for three recommenders ● Review of applications will begin on October 15 and continue until the position is filled. ● Please contact Alina Yakubova ( yakubova@sas.upenn.edu ) with questions regarding the application process."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191965": {
      "title": "Tenure-track Assistant Professor of Modern Chinese History",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Arts and Sciences: School of Arts and Sciences - History Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of History at the University of Pennsylvania seeks a tenure-track Assistant Professor of Modern Chinese History to begin July 1, 2027. “Modern” is defined as 1895 to the present. Candidates must have completed a doctoral degree in history (or a closely related discipline) by the time of appointment. The successful candidate will teach a survey on modern China, among other courses related to their specialization. We particularly welcome applicants with rich archival experience in multiple locations who examine China within broad transnational context. We seek candidates who share our commitment to research, teaching, and mentoring to the highest standards of academic excellence. A complete application includes 1) a cover letter that explains the candidate’s interest, scholarly trajectory, and plans for both undergraduate and graduate teaching; 2) a curriculum vitae; 3) an article-length writing sample; and 4) three letters of recommendation, to be submitted separately by their authors. If the writing sample is part of a dissertation or book manuscript, include an abstract explaining its relation to the larger project. Review of applications will begin on November 5, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. The Department of History is housed within the School of Arts & Sciences, which is comprised of the College of Arts and Sciences, the Graduate Division, and the College of Liberal and Professional Studies. The University of Pennsylvania promotes curiosity, intellectual engagement, and discovery among students, faculty, and staff. We strive to create working and learning environments that foster meaningful connections within the Penn community and beyond."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190940": {
      "title": "Tenure-track Assistant Professor of Sociology",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Arts and Sciences: School of Arts and Sciences - Sociology Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Sociology at the University of Pennsylvania invites applications for a tenure-track assistant professor position in sociology.  The position is open to all areas of sociology. The department is a national leader in the discipline, continuing to push the boundaries of social science, and we seek candidates with strong research experience and potential..  Our department is housed within the School of Arts & Sciences , which is comprised of the College of Arts and Sciences , the Graduate Division , and the College of Liberal and Professional Studies .  Interdisciplinary research and collaboration are supported by the breadth and depth of academic pursuits at Penn as well as the proximity of resources on our contiguous green urban campus in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania.  We promote and encourage curiosity, intellectual engagement, and discovery among all our students and faculty.  University, School, and departmental resources that support our efforts include but are not limited to: the Population Studies Center , the Population Aging Research Center , the Offices of the Vice Provost for Faculty and Vice Provost for Research , the Center for Excellence in Teaching, Learning and Innovation , and the Penn Center for Undergraduate Research and Fellowships . The department offers a variety of undergraduate majors and minors, including a traditional sociology degree, but also specialized degree programs in medical sociology and law and society.  We also offer two graduate training programs, in sociology and demography.  Assistant professors are expected to teach and mentor graduate and undergraduate students in our programs. Qualifications include: Strong research potential Capacity for teaching at both graduate and undergraduate levels The ability to direct graduate and undergraduate research Candidates are expected to have a Ph.D. by the time of appointment, which is July 1, 2027 Please submit the following application materials to apply.interfolio.com/190940 : Cover letter CV Research statement Teaching statement A writing sample, preferably 30 pages or less Three confidential letters of reference Review of applications will begin on September 21, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Please contact Aiasha Saalim Graham (abgraham@upenn.edu, 215-898-7665) with questions regarding the application process."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193623": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Position in Mathematics",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Arts and Sciences: School of Arts and Sciences - Mathematics Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Mathematics at the University of Pennsylvania invites applications for one tenure-track Assistant Professor position in Algebra or Analysis. Responsibilities include teaching undergraduate and graduate courses in Mathematics and conducting research in their field. We are a collaborative and interactive department. We pursue original investigations in Differential Geometry, Partial Differential Equations, Functional Analysis, Mathematical Physics, Algebraic Topology, Algebraic Geometry and Algebraic Number Theory, Combinatorics and Algorithms, Category Theory, Logic and its applications to Computer Science, and several other areas of current mathematical development. The Mathematics Department is housed within the School of Arts & Sciences , which is comprised of the College of Arts and Sciences , the Graduate Division , and the College of Liberal and Professional Studies . Interdisciplinary research and collaboration are supported by the breadth and depth of academic pursuits at Penn as well as the proximity of resources on our contiguous green urban campus in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania.  We promote and encourage curiosity, intellectual engagement, and discovery among all our students and faculty. University, School, and departmental resources that support our efforts include but are not limited to: the Offices of the Vice Provost for Faculty and Vice Provost for Research , PennGlobal , Wellness at Penn , the Center for Excellence in Teaching, Learning and Innovation , the Penn Center for Undergraduate Research and Fellowships , and Penn Human Resources . The department offers an undergraduate major that provides students an understanding of the modern discipline of mathematics.  More generally, we teach hundreds of undergraduate students and sixty grad students each year.  Our graduate students often go on to become postdocs and faculty in academic institutions, and to work as applied research mathematicians for government agencies, as industry scientists working in data analysis, and as researchers in biotech companies.  Our department also participates in cross-disciplinary efforts, many faculty interacting with adjacent undergraduate programs such as computer science, physics, systems and mechanical engineering, and operations research and statistics in the business school. ● Strong research potential ● Capacity for teaching at both the graduate and undergraduate levels and mentoring and supporting a diverse student body ● The ability to direct graduate/undergraduate research ● Candidates are required to have a PhD and expected to have some postdoctoral experience by July 1, 2027, which is the starting date for this appointment. ● The successful candidate will be expected to o attract external funding o contribute actively to the mission of the department ● Please provide the following application materials to URL: o CV o Cover letter o Personal statement(s): § Research § Teaching o Publication list o 3 reference letters from mathematicians familiar with your work o 1 additional letter discussing your teaching experience and ability. ● Review of applications will begin on October 23, 2026 and continue until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193126": {
      "title": "Tenured Associate or Full Professor in Jewish Studies",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Arts and Sciences: School of Arts and Sciences - Religious Studies Location: Philadelphia, PA The School of Arts and Sciences at the University of Pennsylvania invites applications for the Julie Beren Platt and Marc E. Platt Professorship in Jewish Studies, at the rank of tenured Associate or Full Professor. We seek a scholar who specializes in early modern or modern Jewish Studies. The primary appointment will be in one of the following three departments: Francophone, Italian & Germanic Studies ; Middle Eastern Languages & Cultures ; or Religious Studies . The candidate will also be expected to be an active member of the Jewish Studies Program. Applicants should demonstrate expertise and training in both Jewish Studies and in the field of departmental appointment, including the ability to work in relevant languages. The successful candidate must be an accomplished scholar as well as a dedicated and dynamic teacher. Applicants should demonstrate a substantial record of scholarly achievement, including sustained publication and research activity. We are especially interested in candidates with a proven commitment to academic citizenship, service, and leadership. The holder of the Platt Professorship will join the School of Arts & Sciences , which is comprised of 28 academic departments, the College of Arts and Sciences , the Graduate Division , and the College of Liberal and Professional Studies . The University is home to the Herbert D. Katz Center for Advanced Judaic Studies , which supports a residential postdoctoral fellowship on an annual theme and maintains an extraordinary rare book and Judaica collection. Applicants should submit (1) a cover letter; (2) a curriculum vitae; (3) a statement addressing research, teaching, and service (not to exceed 2,500 words); and (4) the names and contact information of three references. Review of applications will begin on November 2, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192741": {
      "title": "Tenured/Tenure-track Position in Africana Studies",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Arts and Sciences: School of Arts and Sciences - Africana Studies Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Africana Studies at the University of Pennsylvania seeks a historian of sub-Saharan Africa at the rank of Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor. The successful candidate will be selected based on superb scholarship and outstanding teaching and mentoring and will join a department distinguished by a clear commitment to Global Black Studies as an interdisciplinary endeavor. Overview of the Department & the School of Arts & Sciences The Department of Africana Studies is an interdisciplinary department devoted to the critical and systematic examination of the cultural, political, social, economic, and historical experiences of peoples of African descent around the world, including North America, Africa, Latin America, the Caribbean, Europe, and the Middle East. The Department is committed to a rigorous, cooperative, and cross-regional approach that brings fresh perspectives to the study and teaching of a new Global Black Studies. The Department works closely with the Center for Africana Studies , a research hub for scholars across the campus doing intellectual work that highlights the impact of Africa and the African diaspora on humanity. We are housed within the School of Arts & Sciences , which is comprised of 28 academic departments, the College of Arts and Sciences , the Graduate Division , and the College of Liberal and Professional Studies .  Interdisciplinary research and collaboration are supported as much by the breadth and depth of academic pursuits at Penn as well as the proximity of resources on our contiguous green urban campus in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania. The Department offers a major and minor for undergraduate students, a graduate certificate program, and a Ph.D. Our curriculum represents a broad range of disciplines and thematic interests, including: Political Theories and Movements; Cultural and Literary Studies; Arts and Performance; Gender and Sexuality; Social Justice; and History. Ours is a unique approach to the study and teaching of Global Black Studies, with a dedication to training the next generation of scholars in this field. We are especially interested in historians whose research and teaching focus on religious and/or sociocultural practices in sub-Saharan Africa. Qualifications include All candidates should have: Publications appropriate to rank Demonstrable commitment to teaching undergraduate and graduate students, and mentoring and supporting a diverse student body in Africana Studies Expertise/training in the history of sub-Saharan Africa and its relationship with the African diaspora Active research agenda, preferably in religion and/or socio-cultural practices in Africa Ability to direct graduate/undergraduate research for a diverse student body Willingness to provide service in the department and school, and to assume leadership positions in the Department and Center as appropriate Candidates are expected to have the PhD in hand by July 1, 2027. Application instructions/Required documents: Please provide the following application materials: CV Cover letter that includes a description of Research, Teaching, and Mentoring A writing sample of no more than 30 pages—could be a published article, book chapter, chapter of a manuscript, or chapter of a dissertation. Contact information for no more than three referees. Additional supporting materials may be requested at a later date. We will begin reviewing applications by October 16, 2026, and continue until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193646": {
      "title": "Tenured/Tenure-track Position in the Department of Middle Eastern Languages and Cultures",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Arts and Sciences: School of Arts and Sciences - Middle Eastern Languages and Cultures Location: Philadelphia, Pennsylvania The Department of Middle Eastern Languages and Cultures at the University of Pennsylvania and the Penn Museum invite applications for a faculty-curator position in Mesopotamian Archaeology to be appointed jointly by the Museum as a curator in the Near East Section and by the School as a tenured or tenure-track faculty member, with time, duties and responsibilities evenly split between the two roles. Faculty-curators have their teaching load reduced from four to two courses per year. Applications are welcome from archaeologists actively engaged in fieldwork in Mesopotamia (modern Eastern Syria and Iraq) with a research focus on Mesopotamia’s historic periods.  The successful candidate will carry out independent research and publication; complement current course offerings on ancient Mesopotamian cultures, history, and material remains at the undergraduate and graduate levels; supervise B.A. and M.A. theses and Ph.D. dissertations; participate in the mentorship of undergraduate and graduate students working in these areas; and engage actively in all other faculty duties. As curator, the candidate will participate in exhibition, research, education, and outreach activities related to the collections within the Section.  Experience in collections stewardship is highly desirable. The Penn Museum Home to over a million extraordinary artifacts and archaeological finds, the Penn Museum has been uncovering our shared humanity across continents and millennia since 1887. In bridging archaeology (the study of objects made by humans), with anthropology (the science of humanity), we chart a course for finding one’s own place in the arc of human history. We are dedicated to telling powerful stories that emerge from our own excavations and research across the world. The curatorial appointment will be in the Museum’s Near East Section, home to over 100,000 artefacts most of which come from over a century of excavations carried out by the Museum in almost every country in the Middle East and spanning a broad range of time periods. The Department of Middle Eastern Languages and Cultures (MELC) MELC engages in the interdisciplinary study and teaching of the cultures and peoples of the Middle East as they have expressed themselves in languages, texts and literatures, including written and oral sources, as well as art, architecture, archaeology, and material objects. Our studies encompass the geographic region that stretches from North Africa and the Iberian Peninsula through the eastern Mediterranean to Arabia and Iran. The areas we study primarily include Egypt, Mesopotamia, Israel/Palestine, greater Syria, Arabia, Iran and the Persianate world, and Anatolia and Turkey. We teach ancient and modern languages including Sumerian, Akkadian, Egyptian, Hebrew, Arabic, Persian, Turkish, and Kurdish. We endeavor in teaching and research to explore past and present social contacts across communities and places. In this way, we push boundaries and frontiers within and beyond the Middle East. Our department upholds a conscious commitment to scholarship within the University of Pennsylvania and the world. The intellectual life of the Department is collaborative and flourishes from its culture of inclusion, which aspires to make everyone feel welcome. We aim to build connections to scholars across the University of Pennsylvania campus and elsewhere at local, national, and international levels as we exchange ideas and spread knowledge. This commitment to collegiality, inquiry, and exploration makes our department a vibrant center for the humanistic study of the Middle East from ancient times to the present. The department offers an undergraduate major that provides students with an understanding of the ancient, medieval and modern Middle East.  Our faculty also participate in cross-disciplinary graduate programs, such as the Art and Archaeology of the Mediterranean World. Interdisciplinary Connections The successful candidate will join a highly interdisciplinary collective for research and teaching, including the PhD programs in the Art and Archaeology of the Ancient World and Ancient History, as well as faculty and students in Anthropology, Art History, MELC and the Museum’s Center for the Analysis of Archaeological Materials. In addition to Penn’s groups, a strong regional collective for faculty and graduate students engages in course exchange with Bryn Mawr College, Princeton University, and Temple University. The School of Arts and Sciences The Department of Middle Eastern Languages and Cultures is housed within the School of Arts & Sciences , at Penn. The School is comprised of 28 academic departments, the College of Arts and Sciences , the Graduate Division , and the College of Liberal and Professional Studies .  Interdisciplinary research and collaboration are supported as much by the breadth and depth of academic pursuits at Penn as well as the proximity of resources on our contiguous green urban campus in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania.  We promote and encourage curiosity, intellectual engagement, and discovery among all our students and faculty. University, School, and departmental resources that support our efforts/ include but are not limited to: the Offices of the Vice Provost for Faculty and Vice Provost for Research , PennGlobal , Wellness at Penn , the Center for Teaching and Learning , the Penn Center for Undergraduate Research and Fellowships , and Penn Human Resources . Qualifications include: ●      All candidates should have: o   publications appropriate to rank o   a deep commitment to teaching undergraduate and graduate students, and mentoring and supporting a diverse student body o   expertise in the archaeology of ancient Mesopotamia o   active research and fieldwork in the area of ancient Mesopotamia o   the ability to direct graduate/undergraduate research for a diverse student body ●      Candidates are expected to have a PhD/postdoctoral experience/other terminal degree by the time of appointment, which is July 1, 2027. Application instructions/Required documents: Cover letter (2-3 pages) CV A research statement (3-5 pages) covering recent, present, and planned research programs Contact information for three individuals who have agreed to provide letters of recommendation A writing sample of no more than 40 pages will be requested from longlisted applicants Review of applications will begin October 19th and continue until the position is filled. Please contact Dr. Steve Tinney ( stinney@sas.upenn.edu ) with questions regarding the application process"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192132": {
      "title": "Tenured/Tenure-Track Position in the History of Computing, Data Science, or Artificial Intelligence in The Department of the History and Sociology of Science",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Arts and Sciences: School of Arts and Sciences - History and Sociology of Science Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of the History and Sociology of Science at the University of Pennsylvania (Penn) invites applications for a tenured/tenure-track position in the history of technology and science with a focus on the history of computing, data and information science, or artificial intelligence. We are interested in applications from colleagues who hold a Ph.D. in the history of science and related disciplines and whose research is grounded in historical and/or ethnographic methods. Tenure-track applicants are expected to have completed all requirements for the Ph.D. in the history of science or another related field by June 2027. The Department of the History and Sociology of Science is a collaborative and interdisciplinary department committed to critical studies of science and knowledge production, from premodernity to the recent past, and with a wide geographical range. The faculty is expected to carry a 2-2 teaching load; maintain a robust research profile; and take an active role in departmental, institutional, and professional service. The department teaches and graduates approximately 200 undergraduates per year who earn degrees in one of our two majors: Health and Societies (HSOC) and Science, Technology, and Society (STSC). Our faculty aims to equip undergraduates with sophisticated critical faculties, multidisciplinary skills, and an extensive knowledge of how science and technology have been defined, practiced, and contested over time and place. Our majors not only prepare undergraduates for careers in medicine, business, law, government, journalism, research, and education, but also provide a foundation for citizenship in a globalizing, diversifying world characterized by rapid technological and scientific change. We also have a thriving Ph.D. program , with an established track record of excellent placement in careers in teaching, research, curatorship, and institutional governance in both the education and healthcare sectors. While the Department is housed within the School of Arts & Sciences , the faculty and students regularly engage in interdisciplinary research and collaboration with the Schools of Engineering , Medicine , and Nursing , efforts that are supported by the University and made possible by our contiguous green urban campus in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania. We are especially interested in applicants whose research and teaching interests focus on artificial intelligence from a global perspective, data and power, computing and infrastructure, digital sovereignty, and technological labor. The successful candidate will be expected to actively contribute to various data and/or AI initiatives across campus, as applicable, such as Penn Data Driven Discovery , Penn AI , and SAS Horizons , offering critical perspectives on data, AI and/or computing. ●        All candidates should have: o   publications appropriate to rank o   a demonstrated commitment to teaching and advising undergraduate and Ph.D. students with evidence of mentoring and supporting students from a wide range of backgrounds o   expertise and training in historical and/or ethnographic methods o   an active research agenda, with evidence of international reputation appropriate to rank o   leadership experience commensurate with rank ●        Candidates for tenure-track appointment are expected to have a Ph.D. in hand by the time of appointment, which is July 1, 2027 ●        The successful candidate will be expected to: o   Teach a 2-2 course load across undergraduate and graduate curriculum as well as advise Ph.D. students and undergraduate majors o   Contribute to the vibrant intellectual life of the department as well as faculty administrative operations o   Maintain a consistent record of publishing peer-reviewed research Please provide the following application materials to https://apply.interfolio.com/192132: CV Cover letter Research statement Teaching statement Writing sample of no more than 10,000 words Contact information for three recommenders Review of applications will begin October 5, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Please contact Ms. Kristen Ramsey ( kramsey1@sas.upenn.edu / 215-898-8400) with questions regarding the application process."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194927": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Oral Medicine, Clinician Educator Track - Orofacial Pain Specialist",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Dental Medicine: School of Dental Medicine - Oral Medicine Location: Philadelphia, Pennsylvania The Department of Oral Medicine at Penn Dental Medicine invites applications for a full-time Assistant Professor in the Clinician Educator (CE) Track. The successful candidate will serve as an Assistant Professor in the Department of Oral Medicine at Penn Dental Medicine and serve as an Orofacial Pain specialist who is dedicated to improving comprehensive care for individuals with complex orofacial pain conditions. The faculty member will provide advanced clinical care, participate in the education of predoctoral and postgraduate learners, contribute to interdisciplinary patient management, and support scholarly and programmatic initiatives that advance orofacial pain management. The successful candidate will work collaboratively with faculty from Penn Dental Medicine, the Perelman School of Medicine and affiliated health systems to improve access for orofacial pain, patient-centered care. Rank: Assistant Professor (Clinician Educator Track). Rank and salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. Required DMD, DDS from a United States, CODA accredited dental educational program. Completion of a 2-year US CODA Approved Residency in Orofacial Pain Current Unrestricted Pennsylvania Dental license or current eligibility for licensure. Board Certified by the American Board of Orofacial Pain Demonstrated 5-year experience caring for orofacial pain patients. Excellent interpersonal, organizational, and communication skills. Experience working within interdisciplinary healthcare teams. Preferred Experience with the development of an Orofacial Pain residency or fellowship program. Experience in academic health centers as teaching environments. Experience with Epic, Axium and other comparable electronic health record systems. Advanced Training beyond Orofacial Pain residency in Pain Management Demonstrated interest in quality improvement, clinical scholarship, or educational innovation. Applicants should apply through the University of Pennsylvania's faculty application system (Interfolio). Questions regarding the position may be directed to: Eric T. Stoopler, DMD, FDSRCS, FDSRCPS Professor of Oral Medicine Department of Oral Medicine Penn Dental Medicine Phone: 215-898-6672 Email: ets@upenn.edu Top of Form"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189265": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Oral Medicine, Clinician Educator Track / Nurse Practitioner, Personalized Care Suite",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Dental Medicine: School of Dental Medicine - Oral Medicine Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Oral Medicine at Penn Dental Medicine invites applications for a full-time Assistant Professor in the Clinician Educator Track to serve as the Nurse Practitioner within the Personalized Care Suite (PCare). PCare is an innovative interdisciplinary program dedicated to improving access to comprehensive oral health care for individuals with intellectual and developmental disabilities, medically complex conditions, physical disabilities, behavioral health needs, and other vulnerable populations. The successful candidate will provide advanced clinical care, educate predoctoral and postgraduate learners, collaborate with interdisciplinary teams across Penn Medicine, and contribute to scholarship and program development that advance integrated medical-dental care. This faculty member will play a central role in supporting Penn Dental Medicine's missions of clinical excellence, education, research, and community engagement. The successful candidate will work collaboratively with faculty from Penn Dental Medicine, the Perelman School of Medicine, Penn Nursing, and affiliated health systems to develop innovative models of patient-centered care. Rank: Assistant Professor (Clinician Educator Track). Rank and salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. Doctor of Nursing Practice (DNP) from an accredited program. Certification as a Family Nurse Practitioner (FNP) or Adult-Gerontology Primary Care Nurse Practitioner (AGPCNP). Current Pennsylvania Registered Nurse and Nurse Practitioner licenses or eligibility for licensure. National certification as a Nurse Practitioner. Demonstrated experience caring for medically complex adult patients. Excellent interpersonal, organizational, and communication skills. Experience working within interdisciplinary healthcare teams. Commitment to diversity, equity, inclusion, and improving access to care for underserved populations. Applicants should apply through the University of Pennsylvania's faculty application system (Interfolio). Questions regarding the position may be directed to: Eric T. Stoopler, DMD, FDSRCS, FDSRCPS Professor of Oral Medicine Department of Oral Medicine Penn Dental Medicine Phone: 215-898-6672 Email: ets@upenn.edu Top of Form"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190601": {
      "title": "Assistant, Associate or Full Professor of Anesthesiology (AC Track)",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Dental Medicine Location: Philadelphia The University of Pennsylvania School of Dental Medicine invites applications for a full-time faculty position in Anesthesiology at the Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor level in the Academic Clinician track. We are seeking an enthusiastic and forward-thinking anesthesiologist to join our collaborative academic community. Responsibilities include: Providing high-quality clinical and didactic instruction to postgraduate specialty students, including but not limited to Periodontics and Pediatric Dentistry residents as well as dental students, in all aspects of anesthesiology and sedation. Delivering expert anesthetic and sedation management for patients with special health care needs and for individuals who require advanced sedative or anesthetic interventions. Contributing to innovative educational initiatives, interdisciplinary collaboration, and the continuous advancement of our sedation and anesthesia programs. This is a rare opportunity to influence the training of future dental specialists and drive innovation in patient-centered anesthesia care at a top-tier institution. Salary and academic rank will be commensurate with experience and qualifications. The posting is open until filled. Start date is negotiable. Qualifications for this position include: (1) DDS/DMD degree with certificate of advanced training in Dental Anesthesiology preferred; (2) MD or DO with dental anesthesiology training acceptable (3) board eligibility or board certification from the American Dental Board of Anesthesiology or American Board of Anesthesiology (4) eligibility for dental or medical licensure with anesthesia permit in the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania; (5) experience in the anesthetic management of patients with special health care needs. Preference will be given to candidates who currently hold active PA licensure. All applicants must apply via Interfolio. For further information regarding the position, contact the search committee chair: Dr. Neeraj Panchal ( npanchal@upenn.edu ) Assistant Professor of Oral and Maxillofacial Surgery and Pharmacology University of Pennsylvania School of Dental Medicine Evans Building 240 South 40th Street Philadelphia, PA 19104"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/181288": {
      "title": "Assistant, Associate or Full Professor of Anesthesiology (CE Track)",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Dental Medicine Location: Philadelphia, PA The University of Pennsylvania School of Dental Medicine invites applications for a full-time faculty position in Anesthesiology at the Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor level in the Clinician Educator track. We are seeking an enthusiastic and forward-thinking anesthesiologist to join our collaborative academic community. Responsibilities include: Providing high-quality clinical and didactic instruction to postgraduate specialty students, including but not limited to Periodontics and Pediatric Dentistry residents as well as dental students, in all aspects of anesthesiology and sedation. Delivering expert anesthetic and sedation management for patients with special health care needs and for individuals who require advanced sedative or anesthetic interventions. Contributing to innovative educational initiatives, interdisciplinary collaboration, and the continuous advancement of our sedation and anesthesia programs. This is a rare opportunity to influence the training of future dental specialists and drive innovation in patient-centered anesthesia care at a top-tier institution. Salary and academic rank will be commensurate with experience and qualifications. The posting is open until filled. Start date is negotiable. Qualifications for this position include: (1) DDS/DMD degree with certificate of advanced training in Dental Anesthesiology preferred; 2) MD or DO with dental anesthesiology training acceptable; (3) board eligibility or board certification from the American Dental Board of Anesthesiology or American Board of Anesthesiology (4) eligibility for dental or medical licensure with anesthesia permit in the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania; (5) experience in the anesthetic management of patients with special health care needs. Preference will be given to candidates who currently hold active PA licensure. All applicants must apply via Interfolio. For further information regarding the position, contact the search committee chair: Dr. Neeraj Panchal ( npanchal@upenn.edu ) Assistant Professor of Oral and Maxillofacial Surgery and Pharmacology University of Pennsylvania School of Dental Medicine Evans Building 240 South 40th Street Philadelphia, PA 19104"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/183477": {
      "title": "Assistant, Associate Professor or Full Professor of Oral Medicine and Radiology (Academic Clinician)",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Dental Medicine: School of Dental Medicine - Oral Medicine Location: Philadelphia Job Description Expected to be responsible for the entire undergraduate DMD radiology curriculum.  This curriculum will include didactic lectures, seminars and clinical radiology teaching including 3D imaging. Expected to improve digital radiography capture, interpretation throughout the school by improving infrastructure and workflow. Expected to assist both members in the Department of Oral Medicine, as well as the school, in general, to assist in their radiologic and clinical  needs Expected to establish relationships with the international oral maxillofacial radiology community and to participate in interprofessional activities within the broader University of Pennsylvania community. Expected to lead school in preparation for its next accreditation related to radiology education, training and regulatory outcomes. Potentially expected to participate in the development of a fellowship in Oral Radiology, possibly leading to a residency program in the same discipline. Summary of your major responsibilities includes the following: ·        Assume the role of Director of the Division of Radiology ·        Lead the Division of Radiology in education, and clinical service, supporting all departments ·        Assume responsibility for the radiology curriculum including both didactic and clinical teaching DMD/DDS from USA preferred Two year CODA certified OMR residency program from US/Canada American Board of OMR Eligible/Certified Five years of experience  teaching Oral and Maxillofacial Radiology preferred Interested candidates must apply via the Interfolio application process. Questions can be directed to the search committee chair, Dr. Sunday Akintoye Sunday O. Akintoye, BDS, DDS, MS Professor Director, Oral Medicine Research Program Director, Master of Oral Health Sciences Program Department of Oral Medicine Phone: 215-898-9932 Email: akintoye@dental.upenn.edu"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/157710": {
      "title": "Assistant, Associate Professor or Full Professor of Oral Medicine and Radiology (Clinician Educator)",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Dental Medicine: School of Dental Medicine - Oral Medicine Location: Philadelphia Expected to be responsible for the entire undergraduate DMD radiology curriculum.  This curriculum will include didactic lectures, seminars and clinical radiology teaching including 3D imaging. Expected to improve digital radiography capture, interpretation throughout the school by improving infrastructure and workflow. Expected to assist both members in the Department of Oral Medicine, as well as the school, in general, to assist in their radiologic clinical and research needs Expected to participate in scholarly activity, seeking and securing clinical research funding and contributing to scholarly publications. Expected to establish relationships with the international oral maxillofacial radiology community and to participate in interprofessional activities within the broader University of Pennsylvania community. Expected to lead school in preparation for its next accreditation related to radiology education, training and regulatory outcomes. Potentially expected to develop a fellowship in Oral Radiology possibly leading to a residency program in the same discipline. Summary of your major responsibilities includes the following: Assume the role of Director of the Division of Radiology Lead the Division of Radiology in education, clinical service and research, supporting all departments Assume responsibility for the radiology curriculum including both didactic and clinical teaching Contribute to the scientific knowledge of dental radiology through research DMD/DDS from USA preferred Two year CODA certified OMR residency program from US/Canada American Board of OMR Eligible/Certified Five year experience with teaching and research preferred Interested candidates must apply via the Interfolio application process. Questions can be directed to the search committee chair, Dr. Sunday Akintoye Sunday O. Akintoye, BDS, DDS, MS Professor Director, Oral Medicine Research Program Director, Master of Oral Health Sciences Program Department of Oral Medicine Phone: 215-898-9932 Email: akintoye@dental.upenn.edu"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/170522": {
      "title": "Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor of Clinical Restorative Dentistry (AC Track)",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Dental Medicine: School of Dental Medicine - Preventive and Restorative Sciences Location: University of Pennsylvania, School of Dental Medicine  240 S. 40th street  Philadelphia, PA The University of Pennsylvania, School of Dental Medicine, Department of Preventive and Restorative Sciences, invites applications from qualified individuals for a position in the Department with a focus on Fixed and Removable Prosthodontics. This full-time faculty position in the Academic Clinician track at the assistant, associate, professor level calls for a person who, through excellence in teaching and clinical practice in prosthodontics, is able to build and maintain the Department's reputation nationally and internationally through leadership and direction of Department activities. These activities include teaching in predoctoral, both clinical and pre-clinical, and postdoctoral education programs as well as research and service to the community. Responsibilities can also include projects as assigned by the Division Director of PG Prosthodontics, Associate Chair/Chair of the department. Preference will be given to candidates who have completed an accredited Advanced Education Program in Prosthodontics and are eligible/board certified by the American Board of Prosthodontics. The successful candidate should have (recommended) a DDS or DMD degree and be eligible for a Pennsylvania dental license. Salary and academic rank will be commensurate with experience and qualifications. The posting is open until filled. Qualifications for this position include a DMD/DDS degree or equivalent; experience with teaching and mentoring students and experience in research and scholarship. Preference will be given to candidates with who have completed an accredited Advanced Education Program in Prosthodontics and are board certified or eligible by the American Board of Prosthodontics. Interested candidates must apply via Interfolio: Additional questions can be directed to the search committee chair, Dr. Nupur Patel. Nupur Patel, DMD Nupur5@dental.upenn.edu"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/185301": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor, Clinician Educator Track Division of Community Oral Health University of Pennsylvania School of Dental Medicine",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Dental Medicine: School of Dental Medicine - Preventive and Restorative Sciences Location: The University of Pennsylvania, School of Dental Medicine The Division of Community Oral Health at the University of Pennsylvania School of Dental Medicine invites applications for a full-time, 12-month faculty position at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor in the Clinician Educator track. This position is designed for an individual committed to excellence in teaching, clinical care, and community-engaged oral health research, consistent with the expectations of the Clinician Educator faculty track at the University of Pennsylvania. The successful candidate will contribute to the Division’s educational mission through didactic instruction, clinical care, mentoring, and curriculum development within the School of Dental Medicine. The position also emphasizes the development of an independent program of scholarly activity related to community oral health, public health dentistry, health disparities, or related fields. Primary Responsibilities · Deliver didactic and seminar-based instruction to predoctoral and graduate dental students in topics related to community oral health, public health dentistry, health promotion, prevention, and other areas. · Provide clinical care to patients at community sites managed by the division and at Penn Dental Medicine. · Contribute to curriculum development within the Division of Community Oral Health. · Mentor students and trainees in scholarly projects and community-based initiatives. · Develop and maintain a program of scholarship, including peer-reviewed publications. · Collaborate with faculty across the School of Dental Medicine and the University of Pennsylvania on interdisciplinary research and educational initiatives. · Participate in academic service activities within the Division, School, and University. About the Division Committed to improving oral health for our patients and our local and global communities, Penn Dental Medicine’s Division of Community Oral Health is responsible for the required predoctoral didactic and community based experiential and clinical education in community and public health, health promotion, cultural competency, ethics, and behavioral and practice management. The Division’s academically based service learning program provides the framework for faculty and students to expand community engagement in the Philadelphia region with patients traditionally underserved by dentistry, such as low-income adults and children, including preschool and school age children, elderly, and those patients with medical complex conditions. The Division also maintains a robust profile of community engaged scholarship and research projects, and is currently engaged in a large research development initiative focused on community oral health. Qualifications Applicants must possess the following: · PhD/DrPH/ScD in dental public health, health services research, epidemiology, or similar field and clinical training such as RDH, DMD, DDS, etc. or · DMD/DDS or equivalent dental degree with demonstrated research training or experience (e.g., MPH or equivalent research-focused experience) Individuals holding a doctoral degree and PHDHP credential are especially encouraged to apply. Candidates should demonstrate: · Commitment to teaching and educational improvement · History of excellence in clinical care · Potential for independent research and extramural funding · Interest in community-engaged scholarship and population oral health Applicants should upload the following documents with their application: CV Cover Letter Research or Personal Statement 3 Contact References For further information regarding the above position, please contact: Alonso Carrasco Labra DDS, MsD, PhD Associate Professor, Center for Intergrative Global Oral Health Director, Cochrane Oral Health Callaborating Center carrascl@upenn.edu The posting for this position will remain open until filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/145760": {
      "title": "Full Time Instructor, Department of Preventive and Restorative Sciences",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Dental Medicine: School of Dental Medicine - Preventive and Restorative Sciences Location: The University of Pennsylvania, School of Dental Medicine 240 S. 40th street Philadelphia, PA The University of Pennsylvania, School of Dental Medicine, Department of Preventive and Restorative Sciences, invites applications from qualified individuals for full-time Instructor  position in the Department with a focus on Fixed and Removable Prosthodontics. The position calls for a person who, through excellence in teaching and research, is able to build and maintain the department's reputation nationally and internationally. This specialty program educates predoctoral and postgraduate dental students in Prosthodontics. Candidates must be eligible for a Pennsylvania state dental license or a Restricted Pennsylvania state dental license. Preference will be given to those holding a DScD , DMD/DDS or equivalent. This position will report to the Department Division Chief of Preventive and Restorative Sciences. Responsibilities will include predoctoral and postdoctoral clinical and didactic teaching and research in the areas specified above.  Women and minority candidates are encouraged to apply. This position is a 1- year contract, spanning July 1 through June 30 of a given year. The is  renewable for reappointment at the end of the academic year. For further information regarding the above position, please contact: David Hershkowitz, DDS, AAS Associate Professor of Clinical Restorative Dentistry Division Chief, Department of Preventive and Restorative Sciences dhh1@upenn.edu The posting for this position will remain open until filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191978": {
      "title": "Full-time Research Associate, Department of Oral Medicine",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Dental Medicine: School of Dental Medicine - Oral Medicine Location: Philadelphia, Pennsylvania The Department of Oral Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania School of Dental Medicine invites applications for a Research Associate position within the Academic Support Staff. The Research Associate will report to Dr. Cagla Akay-Espinoza. The successful candidate will participate in a research program that uses state-of-the-art technologies and experimental models to identify and characterize discrete cell subtypes within complex multicellular environments. The Research Associate will conduct laboratory studies involving molecular biology, next-generation sequencing, mammalian cell culture, and mouse models. Responsibilities may also include experimental design, data interpretation, manuscript preparation, and contributions to grant applications. The successful candidate will have the opportunity to develop an independent research project that is scientifically aligned with the laboratory’s research program and to pursue external research funding. Applicants must have a Ph.D., M.D., or equivalent doctoral degree and approximately five years of relevant postdoctoral research experience. The successful candidate should demonstrate: Extensive expertise in molecular and cellular biology Experience with next-generation sequencing technologies Proficiency in mammalian cell culture and mouse models A strong publication record Strong scientific writing and communication skills Ability to contribute to manuscript preparation and grant writing Ability to work both independently and collaboratively in a multidisciplinary research environment Experience with electrophysiological recording, multielectrode array recording, computational analysis, or next-generation sequencing data analysis is desirable but not required. Applicants must apply via the posting in Interfolio, but may reach out to Dr. Cagla Akay-Espinoza (akayc@upenn.edu) with additional questions."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192966": {
      "title": "Bioengineering Tenured or Tenure Track Positions FY27",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Engineering and Applied Science: School of Engineering and Applied Science - Bioengineering Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Bioengineering at the University of Pennsylvania invites applications for tenure-track and tenured faculty positions at the Assistant, Associate, and Full Professor levels as part of a multi-year hiring initiative. For nearly three centuries, Penn has been a leader in shaping and responding to the world’s changing needs through innovation in education, research, and service. Guided by In Principle and Practice ( https://in-principle-and-practice.upenn.edu/ ), the University is investing boldly in interdisciplinary research and education that address humanity’s most pressing challenges. With twelve schools situated on a single compact campus, Penn offers its faculty unparalleled opportunities for research and education in Bioengineering through collaboration across various engineering, sciences, and health programs. The faculty in the Department of Bioengineering are engaged in integrative research and education across the continuum from fundamental discovery to clinical translation. Bioengineering plays a prominent role in the strategic vision of The School of Engineering and Applied Science, Penn Engineering 2030 ( https://www.engineering.upenn.edu/about/strategic-plan/ ). We are seeking to recruit outstanding faculty who will further enhance our integrative research and educational programs. Broad thematic areas for growth include: (1) Precision Engineering for Health, (2) Intelligent and Autonomous Systems for Engineered Life, and (3) Engineering and AI Models Driving Digital Health. While these themes already encompass a wide range of subdisciplines, technologies, and applications in bioengineering, we also welcome applications from exceptional candidates in any emerging field of bioengineering whose vision complements and expands the Department’s interests and strengths. Candidates should hold a doctoral degree in Bioengineering/ Biomedical Engineering or a related field. At the assistant professor level, individuals should demonstrate stellar academic credentials and promise for a visionary career in bioengineering research. At higher professorial levels, a proven and evident track record of research excellence, including successful competitive research funding and academic standing, is expected. A commitment to teaching and pedagogical excellence should also be apparent, consistent with experience and rank, including development of undergraduate and graduate courses, supervision of doctoral students, and academic and professional mentoring and advising of students at all levels. We seek individuals who are committed to nurturing and building our scholarly community in the broadest sense, participating in departmental, school-level, and university-level efforts towards this goal, and to work closely with students to identify and address challenges. Penn Engineering strongly supports dual career hires, and we welcome and encourage inquiries about dual career assistance (for academic and non-academic opportunities) at an early stage of the recruitment process. The deadline for the application is November 23, 2026. Review of applications will be conducted on a rolling basis, and candidates are encouraged to apply early to be given full consideration. Applications must be submitted online via Interfolio .  Applications must include: Curriculum vitae Research Statement (please include initiatives for building a scholarly community) Teaching Statement (please include initiatives for building a scholarly community) Contact information for three references who could provide letters of recommendation. For technical assistance with your application, contact Interfolio Scholar Services by email at help@interfolio.com or by phone at (877) 997-8807 from 9:00 – 6:00 EST on Monday – Friday."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192537": {
      "title": "CBE Tenured or Tenure-track Positions 2026 - 2027",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Engineering and Applied Science: School of Engineering and Applied Science - Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering seeks outstanding applicants for tenure-track or tenured positions at the Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor levels. Individuals with exceptional promise for, or a proven record of research achievement who will excel in teaching undergraduate and graduate students are encouraged to apply. The Department’s research programs span a broad range of topics within chemical and biomolecular engineering, including computation, theory, and experimental research. We welcome applicants from all areas of Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering related to computational or theoretical research, including, but not limited to process systems engineering; artificial intelligence and machine learning; molecular simulation and thermodynamics; multiscale and data-driven modeling; optimization and control; soft matter and complex fluids; computational protein design and engineering, modeling of biomolecular systems; systems biology and synthetic biology. More information about the department’s research areas is available at https://cbe.engineering.upenn.edu/research/research-areas/ The Department of Chemical Engineering is located within Penn Engineering. Information about Penn Engineering is available at https://www.engineering.upenn.edu/ . The school’s strategic plan is available at https://www.engineering.upenn.edu/about/strategic-plan/ . Penn provides tremendous opportunities for research both within and across academic units and schools, including the School of Arts and Science, and the School of Medicine. The close proximity of all of Penn's schools and research centers enables seamless collaboration. Penn Engineering strongly supports dual career couples, and we welcome and encourage inquiries about dual career assistance (for academic and non-academic opportunities) at an early stage of the recruitment process. Applicants must have a Ph.D. in Chemical Engineering or a related field. At the Assistant Professor level, individuals should demonstrate excellent academic credentials and promise for impact in research in Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering. At the Associate and Full Professor ranks, a strong demonstrated track record of research excellence including successful competitive research funding and academic standing is expected. A commitment to teaching should also be apparent including development of undergraduate and graduate courses, supervision of doctoral students, and academic advising of students at all levels. To ensure full consideration, applicants are encouraged to apply by November 30, 2026. However, applications will be accepted until the position is filled or the search is closed. Applications must be submitted online via Interfolio and should include: Curriculum vitae Cover Letter Research statement Teaching statement Names and contact information for at least three referees For technical assistance with the application process, please contact Interfolio Scholar Services at help@interfolio.com or (877) 997-8807, Monday through Friday, 9:00 a.m. to 6:00 p.m. EST."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194856": {
      "title": "CIS Lecturer Positions 2027",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Engineering and Applied Science: School of Engineering and Applied Science - Computer and Information Science Location: Philadelphia, PA The University of Pennsylvania’s Department of Computer and Information Science is pleased to welcome applications for teaching positions as a Lecturer or Senior Lecturer.  It is a very exciting time to be engaged in computer science education at Penn, as we are developing new curricular programming to embrace artificial intelligence’s increased role in the field. We are also developing multiple new on-campus and online learning programs. We are seeking dedicated, accomplished, full-time instructors to teach undergraduate electives and Master’s-level courses, particularly in areas such as software engineering, artificial intelligence and data science, theoretical computer science, computer graphics, and cybersecurity, starting in July 2027. We seek individuals who promote and welcome wide-ranging viewpoints and experiences, and who will work closely with students of all backgrounds. Additionally (see separate job posting), as part of our revamped teaching-track faculty career path, we also have an open Practice Assistant Professor position. This is a great career choice for teaching-focused faculty members who have a track record of published research in engineering education and are interested in developing and publishing on innovative pedagogical techniques. Applicants are encouraged to apply for any/all positions for which they think they may be qualified. Master’s or PhD in Computer Science or relevant discipline, or equivalent experience. Application Instructions: To ensure full consideration, applicants are encouraged to apply by November 15th, 2026. However, applications will be accepted until positions are filled. Please submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, teaching statement, and three confidential letters of recommendation. Other supporting documents are optional. Applications must be submitted online through Interfolio."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194863": {
      "title": "CIS Practice Assistant Professor 2027",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Engineering and Applied Science: School of Engineering and Applied Science - Computer and Information Science Location: Philadelphia, PA The University of Pennsylvania’s Department of Computer and Information Science is pleased to welcome applications for experienced teaching faculty under the Practice Professor track. It is a very exciting time to be engaged in computer science education at Penn, as we are developing new curricular programming to embrace artificial intelligence’s increased role in the field. We are also developing multiple new on-campus and online learning programs. We are seeking dedicated, accomplished, full-time faculty to teach undergraduate electives and Master’s-level courses, particularly in areas such as software engineering, artificial intelligence and data science, theoretical computer science, computer graphics, and cybersecurity, starting in July 2027. We seek individuals who promote and welcome wide-ranging viewpoints and experiences, and who will work closely with students of all backgrounds. As part of teaching-track career path, the Practice Assistant Professor position seeks teaching-focused faculty members, who have a track strong record of research in engineering education and developing and publishing on innovative pedagogical techniques. Applicants who desire to pursue this track but do not yet have an extensive publication history are encouraged to apply also to our posting for a Lecturer or Senior Lecturer position PhD in Computer Science or relevant discipline. To ensure full consideration, applicants are encouraged to apply by November 15th, 2026. However, applications will be accepted until positions are filled. Please submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, research statement, teaching statement, and names of 3 references. Other supporting documents are optional. Applications must be submitted online through Interfolio."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194852": {
      "title": "CIS Tenured or Tenure-track Positions 2027",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Engineering and Applied Science: School of Engineering and Applied Science - Computer and Information Science Location: Philadelphia, PA Penn Engineering and the Department of Computer and Information Science are engaged in an ambitious, multi-year hiring effort for tenure-track positions at the Assistant, Associate, and Full Professor levels. We have hired over 30 new tenure-track and tenured faculty, spanning all ranks, over the past 9 years. We seek individuals with stellar research achievement and potential, who will excel in teaching undergraduate and graduate courses and take a position of international leadership in defining their field of study. These individuals will promote and welcome wide-ranging viewpoints and experiences and will work closely with students of all backgrounds. Leadership in cross-disciplinary collaborations is of particular interest. Successful applicants will find Penn to be a stimulating environment conducive to professional growth. This position will start July 1, 2027. Applicants must have a PhD in Computer Science or a relevant discipline. We seek exceptional candidates in all areas of computer science, with a special interest this year in candidates in theoretical computer science, computer security, and artificial intelligence (including AI systems). We especially invite applications from candidates whose interests are aligned with the school’s strategic plan: https://www.engineering.upenn.edu/about/strategic-plan/ . To ensure full consideration, applicants are encouraged to apply by November 30th, 2026. However, applications will be accepted until positions are filled. Please submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, research statement, teaching statement and four confidential letters of recommendation. Other supporting documents are optional. Applications must be submitted online through Interfolio ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191969": {
      "title": "ESE Lecturer 2026-2027",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Engineering and Applied Science: School of Engineering and Applied Science - Electrical and Systems Engineering Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Electrical and Systems Engineering (ESE) within the School of Engineering and Applied Science (SEAS) at the University of Pennsylvania seeks outstanding applicants for a full-time lecturer position to teach courses in artificial intelligence, machine learning, and data science, with particular focus on applications of Artificial Intelligence (AI) and on computer systems and architectures for AI. The department seeks individuals with exceptional promise for, or a proven record of, excellence in undergraduate and graduate education. Appointments may be at Senior Lecturer or Lecturer rank commensurate with the candidate's experience and accomplishments. This position is non-tenure track. Candidates must have a commitment to excellence in undergraduate and graduate education in electrical and systems engineering or related areas, and a strong commitment to fostering a positive educational environment. The Department maintains close collaborations with all other engineering departments in SEAS and other Schools at Penn including the School of Arts and Sciences, the Perelman School of Medicine, the Wharton School of Business, and the Stuart Weitzman School of Design. The Department has strong interactions with leading centers including the General Robotics, Automation, Sensing, and Perception (GRASP) Laboratory, the Penn Institute for Computational Science (PICS), and the Penn Center for Health, Devices & Technology. Applicants should hold an advanced degree in an engineering discipline related to Artificial Intelligence including Electrical Engineering, Computer Engineering, Computer Science, Mechanical Engineering, Machine Learning, Data Science, or related areas. Applications must be submitted online via Interfolio . Applicants must submit a CV, teaching statement, and contact information for three references for consideration."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193098": {
      "title": "ESE Tenured or Tenure-track Faculty Positions, 2026 - 2027",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Engineering and Applied Science: School of Engineering and Applied Science - Electrical and Systems Engineering Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Electrical and Systems Engineering is soliciting applications for multiple tenured or tenure-track positions at all levels. Candidates must hold a Ph.D. in Electrical Engineering, Computer Engineering, Systems Engineering, or a related area. The department seeks individuals with exceptional promise for, or proven record of, research achievement, who will take a position of international leadership in defining their field of study and who will excel in undergraduate and graduate education. Leadership in cross-disciplinary and multi-disciplinary collaborations is of particular interest. We are seeking exceptional candidates in all areas of electrical and systems engineering, with emphasis on Decision Systems, Artificial Intelligence, and Computer Engineering. We are particularly interested in AI methods that strongly connect to real-world application domains (e.g., energy systems, transportation, healthcare) or directly inform operational or strategic decisions in complex systems. In computer engineering, we are particularly interested in digital design, computer architecture, and programming systems that permit the joint optimization of hardware and software. We are especially interested in candidates with a diversity of experiences and perspectives.  Penn Engineering strongly supports dual career couples, and we welcome and encourage inquiries about dual career assistance (for academic and non-academic opportunities) at an early stage of the recruitment process. Interested persons should submit an online application and include curriculum vitae, research, and teaching statements, and at least three confidential letters of recommendation. Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2026. Must have a Ph.D. in Electrical Engineering, Computer Engineering, Systems Engineering, or related area. Applications must be submitted online via Interfolio .  Applications include: Curriculum vitae Research statement Teaching statement 3-5 Confidential Letters of Recommendation For technical assistance with your application, contact Interfolio Scholar Services by email at help@interfolio.com or by phone at (877) 997-8807 from 9:00 – 6:00 EST on Monday – Friday."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195553": {
      "title": "MSE Tenure-Track Positions 2026–2027",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Engineering and Applied Science: School of Engineering and Applied Science - Materials Science and Engineering Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Materials Science and Engineering at the University of Pennsylvania is conducting a multi-year search for tenure-track faculty whose research aligns with the strategic plan of the School of Engineering and Applied Science (Penn Engineering). We invite applications from all experimental and computational areas of materials research. We have particular interest in candidates who develop or apply emerging methods in artificial intelligence (AI), machine learning (ML), data science, and materials computation. We are interested in these methods as tools to advance the discovery, synthesis, characterization, and production of electronic materials, structural materials, and materials that address challenges in energy and environmental sustainability. Penn has signature initiatives in data science, energy and sustainability , and quantum devices and engineering ( QUIEST ). Penn also has shared facilities at the Singh Center for Nanotechnology , the Vagelos Laboratory for Energy Science and Technology (VLEST), and the Laboratory for Research on the Structure of Matter (LRSM). We seek colleagues who will help build a collegial scholarly community within the department, across Penn Engineering, and throughout the University. We also seek colleagues who will work closely with students to identify and address research challenges. Penn Engineering strongly supports dual-career couples. We encourage early inquiries about dual-career assistance for both academic and non-academic opportunities. Materials Discovery, Synthesis, and Characterization for Advanced Applications Progress in materials science and advanced manufacturing depends on the discovery, development, and characterization of new electronic, structural, sustainable, and energy-related materials systems. The next generation of highly engineered materials is expected to be synthesized with control over several features at once. These features are local atomic bonding and structure, anisotropy, the ordering of defects, charges, and spins, and dimensionality and topology. We seek candidates with experimental or computational expertise who develop modern tools that integrate AI, ML, data science, and materials computation to accelerate materials discovery, synthesis, characterization, and processing. Of particular interest are AI/ML and computational approaches to materials prediction and design, autonomous experimentation, synthesis and processing, and characterization. This includes methods that combine physical models, simulation, data, and experiment. Areas of interest include, but are not limited to: Autonomous and AI-driven materials discovery, characterization, optimization, synthesis, and processing Data-driven design and synthesis of solid-state materials Scalable methods for producing and integrating materials across all length scales Prediction and control of material composition, structure, and properties at multiple length scales Translation of fundamental materials science into electronics, photonics, sensing, and energy applications The ideal candidate will demonstrate the ability to predict, control, and interrogate material composition, structure, and properties at multiple length scales to enable next-generation devices and systems. Candidates should have a strong background in materials theory, computation, synthesis, or characterization. They should also have a clear vision for how their approach will improve device performance and functionality and accelerate materials innovation. We particularly encourage applications from candidates who connect fundamental materials science to device and system performance and who are committed to fostering interdisciplinary collaboration. Prof. Christopher B. Murray chairs the search committee. Review of applications will proceed on a rolling basis and candidates are encouraged to submit materials early for full consideration. All application materials must be received by December 1, 2026 . Must have a Ph.D. in Materials Science and Engineering or related disciplines. Applications must be submitted online via Interfolio . Applications include: Cover Letter Curriculum vitae Research statement (5-page limit) Teaching statement (2-page limit) Three confidential letters of recommendation"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193932": {
      "title": "Tenure-track Position in Mechanical Engineering and Applied Mechanics (MEAM) 2026-2027",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Engineering and Applied Science: School of Engineering and Applied Science - Mechanical Engineering and Applied Mechanics Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Mechanical Engineering and Applied Mechanics within the School of Engineering and Applied Science (SEAS) at the University of Pennsylvania seeks outstanding applicants for a tenure-track or tenured position at the Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor level. The Department seeks individuals with a record of, or exceptional promise for, research achievement, a dedication to excellence in undergraduate and graduate education, and a commitment to building our scholarly community. A candidate's research must be centered in mechanical engineering, and their teaching interests should align with the Department's core educational programs. We are particularly interested in applicants working in the broad areas of fluid mechanics and thermal sciences, including research with applications in aerospace, human health and physiological flows, manufacturing, thermal management for electronics and computing, energy, and the environment. We welcome candidates advancing computational and experimental methods, including multiscale simulation, AI-enabled modeling of physical systems, transformative flow and thermal measurement approaches, and autonomous experimentation. The Department has grown substantially in recent years and is positioned to expand its impact as part of the Penn Engineering 2030 Strategic Plan . Our faculty collaborate with researchers across the University and benefit from engagements with leading research centers . We encourage applications from candidates who will contribute to, and benefit from, this vibrant interdisciplinary environment. Penn Engineering strongly supports dual-career couples, and we welcome and encourage inquiries about dual career assistance (for academic and non-academic opportunities) at an early stage of the recruitment process. The anticipated start date is July 1, 2027, or a later mutually agreed to date. Applicants must have a doctoral degree in mechanical engineering or a related discipline by the start date. Applications are required to be submitted online through Interfolio , and include a cover letter, curriculum vitae, teaching statement (1-2 pages), research statement (2-3 pages), and names and contact information of 3 references. Candidates will be contacted before references are requested. For full consideration, please apply by December 4, 2026 ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193944": {
      "title": "Clinical Instructor –  Care of Women & Infants (NURS 2150)",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Nursing: School of Nursing - Family and Community Health Location: Philadelphia, PA Are you passionate about shaping future generations of nursing? Do you find purpose in mentorship, clinical excellence, and teaching? If you are currently pursuing your MSN, or hold an MSN, DNP, or PhD and have expertise in inpatient OB clinical care , consider joining Penn Nursing as a Clinical Instructor for the entry-to-practice course, NURS 2150: Care of Women & Infants. About the Role Clinical Instructors play a vital role in guiding nursing students through hands-on clinical experiences in alignment with respective course objectives. Instructors supervise and mentor students in providing safe, equitable, and high-quality person-centered care, while assisting development of: •                    Clinical judgment and decision-making skills •                    Competency in nursing knowledge, skills, and attitudes •                    Effective communication with patients, families, and healthcare teams •                    Evidence-based practice Clinical Instructors also evaluate student performance, provide performance feedback, and support student growth as future professionals in nursing. Course Overview NURS 2150 prepares students to care for second‑year undergraduate nursing course focused on the health care of women and infants. The course integrates: Women’s health across the lifespan (prenatal, postpartum, reproductive health) Infant care (newborn assessment, feeding, growth and development, family support) Interdisciplinary collaboration in maternal and infant health settings Clinical Dates : February 13, 2027 – April 25, 2027. 8-hour shifts x 8 weeks.  No clinical over spring break (3/6-3/14/27) or Easter weekend 3/27-2/28 Clinical Sections : Weekend & Weekdays available; day shift (0645-1515) & evening shift (1445-2315) available Location : Penn Medicine inpatient OB units – Hospital of the University of Pennsylvania & Pennsylvania Hospital available MSN in progress, or completed MSN, DNP or PhD in Nursing Active PA RN license Inpatient OB RN experience Passion for teaching and mentoring Commitment to equity, safety, and excellence in nursing practice Interested candidates should submit a cover letter and CV. We look forward to hearing from you! To apply or learn more, please contact: Jennifer Smolenski jngreen@upenn.edu"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/177759": {
      "title": "Clinical Instructor – Entry-to-Practice Nursing Program (NURS 5040)",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Nursing: School of Nursing - Biobehavioral Health Sciences Location: Philadelphia, PA Are you passionate about shaping future generations of nursing? Do you find purpose in mentorship, clinical excellence, and teaching? If you are currently pursuing your MSN, or hold an MSN, DNP, or PhD and have expertise in adult clinical care, consider joining Penn Nursing as a Clinical Instructor for the entry-to-practice course, NURS 5040: Nurses Caring for the Individual Adult and Across Populations. About the Role Clinical Instructors play a vital role in guiding nursing students through hands-on clinical experiences in alignment with respective course objectives. Instructors supervise and mentor students in providing safe, equitable, and high-quality person-centered care, while assisting development of: • Clinical judgment and decision-making skills • Competency in nursing knowledge, skills, and attitudes • Effective communication with patients, families, and healthcare teams • Evidence-based practice Clinical Instructors also evaluate student performance, provide performance feedback, and support student growth as future professionals in nursing. Course Overview NURS 5040 prepares students to care for adults across the continuum of health and illness/injury. The course integrates: • Symptom and disease management • Health promotion and disease/injury prevention • Transitions in care • Cultural, ethical, and policy considerations • Interprofessional collaboration Course Dates : January 14 – March 4, 2025 Clinical Section : Thursdays, 6:30 AM – 7:00 PM Location : Penn Medicine inpatient units • MSN in progress, or completed MSN, DNP, PhD in Nursing • Minimum of 5 years of adult, inpatient RN experience • Passion for teaching and mentoring • Commitment to equity, safety, and excellence in nursing practice Interested candidates should submit a cover letter and CV. We look forward to hearing from you! To apply or learn more, please contact: Maria White namowicz@nursing.upenn.edu"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/125149": {
      "title": "Clinical Simulation Instructor",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Nursing Location: Philadelphia, PA Ranked as the best nursing school in the world the University of Pennsylvania School of Nursing advances the entire profession, from research & education to practice & policy.  The University of Pennsylvania School of Nursing has immediate openings for part-time Clinical Simulation Instructors. Facilitates formative and summative simulation sessions at the undergraduate level. Provides feedback on pre-simulation assignments to prepare the learner for an active and engaging educational experience. Fosters clinical judgement by posing reflective questions during debriefing. Provides a safe learning environment to ensure an optimal learning experience. Courses: All undergraduate clinical courses (Health Assessment, Medical-Surgical, Pediatric, Obstetrical, Community, Psychiatric Mental Health, and Leadership Nursing). The candidate will teach in the Department of Biobehavioral Health Sciences or Department of Family and Community Health in the School of Nursing. Master of Science in Nursing (MSN) from an accredited institution and a current PA RN license required. To note: BSN-prepared nurses, currently enrolled in an MSN program are eligible but MSN must be completed within 5 years. Interested candidates should submit a cover letter and CV."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194355": {
      "title": "Practice Professor of Nursing-Sexual Reproductive Health & Nurse Midwifery",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Nursing: School of Nursing - Family and Community Health Location: Philadelphia The University of Pennsylvania School of Nursing , one of 12 Schools in a highly ranked institution, is dedicated to the integration of interdisciplinary research, education, and practice. The Family and Community Health (FCH) Department in the School of Nursing seeks candidates for an associated faculty position on the non-tenure practice track. We invite individuals prepared at the doctoral level (PhD or DNP) to apply to the Associated faculty (Practice Track at rank of Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor). Applicants must have content expertise in sexual reproductive health, women’s health, and/or nurse-midwifery. Teaching responsibilities may include courses in the Women’s Health Gender Related (WHGRNP) Nurse Practitioner program, Nurse-Midwifery program, and our BSN and MPN programs. The Women’s Health/Gender-Related Nurse Practitioner program focuses on the primary, sexual, and reproductive health care needs of women from adolescence through the advanced years, concentrating on promoting and maintaining wellness. The Nurse-Midwifery program prepares graduates for midwifery practice focused on providing family-centered, comprehensive care for fundamentally healthy people and their families. The BSN and MPN programs both include courses focused on sexual reproductive health and nursing of women and infants. The Practice Professor may have teaching responsibilities in both the entry-to-practice (BSN & MPN) and graduate programs. Experience in academic program leadership, curriculum development/evaluation, academic teaching using multiple modalities (didactic, online) and working with an interprofessional audience of physicians and nurses is preferred.  Strong analytical skills with the ability to assimilate numerous sources of complex information is required. Excellent writing skills are essential. Strong interpersonal and organizational skills needed. The candidate must be licensed as a Registered Nurse (RN) in the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania or be eligible for licensure, with acquisition of the RN license within the first semester of employment. For those who will teach in the Women’s Health/Gender-Related Nurse Practitioner (WHGRNP) Program, the candidate must also be licensed as a Certified Registered Nurse Practitioner (CRNP) in Pennsylvania or be eligible for licensure, with acquisition of the CRNP license within the first semester of employment. Current national board certification is required. For those who will teach in the Nurse-Midwifery Program, the candidate must be licensed as a Certified Nurse-Midwife (CNM) in Pennsylvania or be eligible for licensure, with acquisition of the CNM license within the first semester of employment. Current certification by the American Midwifery Certification Board (AMCB) is required. Qualifications: Academic teaching experience is required and should meet minimum qualifications for the Practice Professor track: § Achieves consistently high-level quality teaching via peer review and student evaluations. § Identifies and applies creative teaching and evaluation methods within the curricular framework identified by the SON. § Demonstrates leadership in curriculum development. § Demonstrates excellence in depth and breadth of knowledge in the content being taught. § Contributes in significant ways to the design, implementation, and evaluation of programmatic educational offerings within the SON. § Demonstrates impact in his/her area of expertise through a consistent record of publications, consultations, presentations, and panels at local and regional forums. § Demonstrates excellence in student advising and mentoring. § Mentors faculty in other tracks (e.g. lecturer positions) and/or mentors teaching assistants Interested Applicants please submit Cover Letter and CV"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/149150": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Small Animal Surgical Oncology, Clinician Educator",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Veterinary Medicine: School of Veterinary Medicine - Clinical Sciences and Advanced Medicine Location: Philadelphia, PA, United States The University of Pennsylvania School of Veterinary Medicine, Department of Clinical Sciences and Advanced Medicine, Section of Comprehensive Cancer Care invites qualified individuals to apply for an open faculty position in the Clinician Educator (CE) track in Small Animal Surgical Oncology at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor. Applications are currently being accepted, and the start date is negotiable. The successful candidate is expected to have history of or potential as an exemplary clinician, educator and researcher and excel as an academic leader in their field. Members of faculty in the CE track perform 29 weeks of clinical patient care ( up to a 60% clinical commitment ) which includes clinical service in surgical oncology and sharing backup on-call for surgery in the Matthew J. Ryan Veterinary Hospital. Teaching duties include both clinical and didactic teaching of veterinary students, interns, and residents in oncologic and soft tissue surgery . The CE faculty member is expected to contribute to their field through scholarship that brings new knowledge and advances their field. The remaining commitment for CE faculty members is to establish a productive clinical research program that will lead to advancements in their field. Support for clinical research within the Department and the School is excellent. The University of Pennsylvania offers an outstanding environment for surgical research, as collaborative research opportunities abound within the School of Veterinary Medicine and the wider Penn research community. The successful candidate will enjoy a wealth of opportunities that will enable the fulfillment of these responsibilities in a highly collaborative, outstanding clinical environment within a busy academic teaching hospital. The Section of Comprehensive Cancer Care employs multiple members of faculty in direct support of the program, including four medical oncologists and two radiation oncologists. House officers include six medical oncology residents, two radiation oncology interns and a clinical trials intern. Seven surgery residents spend a minimum of 6 weeks each year in surgical oncology and 14 rotating interns spend a minimum of 2 weeks in the oncology service. Sixty-eight boarded specialists support the section of Comprehensive Cancer Care, including minimally-invasive surgery, interventional radiolog y , 2 clinical pathologists, and 4 anatomic pathologists. Oncology clinicians enjoy the benefits of the Rosenthal Imaging and Treatment Center, including multi-slice CT, MRI, a Varian linear accelerator with IMRT capability, as well as the recently renovated Richard Lichter emergency room and s tate-of-the-art Advanced Dentistry and Oral Surgery Suite. Adjacent to the veterinary hospital are three world-class biomedical hospitals and research centers, which provide excellent opportunities for collaboration: The Perelman School of Medicine, the Children’s Hospital of Philadelphia, and the Wistar Institute. The PennVet Cancer Center, launched in 2017, connects our clinicians to researchers at these surrounding centers for a collaborative approach to advance global cancer research ( www.vet.upenn.edu/research/centers-laboratories/center/penn-vet-cancer-center ). The University provides a comprehensive and competitive benefits package, which includes multiple options for retirement savings and tuition benefits for the candidate’s spouse or dependent children. Philadelphia is a vibrant, historic, and welcoming city that is home to world-class museums, award-winning restaurants, diverse neighborhoods, and a thriving arts scene. The city offers endless opportunities for exploration and enrichment with easy access to New York and Washington DC, as well as the mountains and the shore ( www.upenn.edu/life-at-penn/philadelphia ). With extensive parks and green spaces, including the nation’s largest urban park system, Philadelphia offers a high quality of life for outdoor enthusiasts, families, and young professionals alike. Candidates must have a veterinary degree and be board-certified by the American College of Veterinary Surgeons or successfully completed an accredited surgical residency and are preparing for board-certification. The candidate must demonstrate expertise in surgical oncology. Fellowship training is highly desirable, with the goal of establishing a Surgical Oncology Fellowship Program. Salary will be commensurate with professional and academic credentials and experience. Applications will be accepted immediately and reviewed until an appropriate candidate is identified. A signed non-competition agreement is required for all faculty hires at Penn Vet. Interested applicants should apply online at ( http://apply.interfolio.com/149150 ). The application system will prompt you to attach a letter of intent, a statement of research and personal goals, a Curriculum Vitae, and the contact information of three references. These materials should be submitted for the attention of the Search Committee Chair, Dr. Pavlisko, Department of Clinical Sciences and Advanced Medicine. Questions should be sent to Dr. Noah Pavlisko ( pavlisko@vet.upenn.edu ) or Ms. Kelli Cave ( kellijw@upenn.edu ) ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/183019": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Clinical Small Animal Internal Medicine - Academic Clinician (AC)",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Veterinary Medicine: School of Veterinary Medicine - Clinical Sciences and Advanced Medicine Location: Philadelphia The University of Pennsylvania, School of Veterinary Medicine, Department of Clinical Sciences and Advanced Medicine, invites applications for a clinical faculty position at the Assistant or Associate Professor level in Small Animal Internal Medicine in the Academic Clinician (AC) track. The successful candidate is expected to have history of or potential as an exemplary clinician and educator. The successful candidate must also possess demonstrated excellence in clinical service and teaching, consistent with their primary and driving career interests and goals. Members of faculty in the AC track in Internal Medicine are primarily devoted to clinical service; Eighty percent (80%) of the candidate’s time will be in the Matthew J. Ryan Veterinary Hospital. Time outside of clinical service is used for professional development, citizenship to the Department, and didactic teaching. Developing or maintaining an independent scholarship or research program is not a focus of the AC track and is not a requirement for reappointment or promotion, although AC faculty may contribute to or participate in collaborative research projects within and outside of the School of Veterinary Medicine if desired. The position includes clinical service and teaching in a busy university teaching hospital. Clinical service responsibilities include provision of patient care and supervision of day-to-day operations of the Internal Medicine service. Teaching responsibilities include small group and didactic instruction of veterinary students rotating through the service and mentoring of internal medicine residents. In addition, clinical faculty participate in classroom/laboratory teaching. The proximity of the Veterinary School to the Medical School fosters numerous collaborative clinical endeavors. The Department offers junior rank faculty mentoring through a specifically appointed mentoring committee. The Section of Medicine employs eight (7 ACVIM-Small Animal Internal Medicine, 1 ECVIM-Small Animal Internal Medicine) board-certified internists in support of the program. There are currently 12 residents enrolled in the residency-training program. More than 30 Diplomates of various American and European certifying Colleges directly support the Internal Medicine program. Faculty AC positions are long-term, non-tenured Associated Faculty positions with reappointment every three years. Promotion along the ladder of the University Academic ranks is available. Salary will be commensurate with experience. Full University benefits including vacation time, health, dental, vision, retirement, and eligible tuition reimbursement for self, spouse, and or dependents accompany the position. The anticipated start date, although negotiable, is July 2026. The hospital is a dynamic and intellectually stimulating environment with an excellent nursing staff and residents. The School is located amid a vibrant urban neighborhood that offers an abundance of cultural resources including music, art, theatres, and fine dining (www.upenn.edu/life-at-penn/philadelphia). The University of Pennsylvania is a qualifying employer for the federal Public Service Loan Forgiveness (PSLF) Program. Candidates must have a veterinary degree, and either be a Diplomate of the American or European College of Veterinary Internal Medicine, Specialty of Small Animal Internal Medicine, or have successfully completed an internal medicine residency program at an accredited University.  Board-certification would need to be achieved within 24 months of employment. Applications will be reviewed until an appropriate candidate has been identified. Interested applicants must apply electronically to https://apply.interfolio.com/183019 . The system will prompt you to attach a letter of intent including a statement of personal goals, curriculum vitae, and the names, addresses, phone, and contact information for three references to Dr. Christine Cain, Associate Professor of Dermatology and chair of the search committee ( ccain@vet.upenn.edu ). Questions regarding the position can be sent to Kelli Cave, Search coordinator at kellijw@upenn.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/183016": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Small Animal Internal Medicine – Clinician Educator (CE)",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Veterinary Medicine: School of Veterinary Medicine - Clinical Sciences and Advanced Medicine Location: Philadelphia The University of Pennsylvania, School of Veterinary Medicine, Department of Clinical Sciences and Advanced Medicine, invites applications for a faculty position at the Assistant or Associate Professor level in Small Animal Internal Medicine in the Clinician Educator (CE) track. The successful candidate is expected  to have history of or potential as an exemplary clinician educator, researcher and academic leader in their field and  possess demonstrated excellence in clinical service and teaching, as well as a record of or potential to develop a scholarly research program that advances the field. The CE faculty member is expected to contribute to their field through scholarship that brings new knowledge and advances their field. Members of the faculty in the CE trach in Internal Medicine provide patient care; Sixty (60%) of the candidate’s time will be dedicated to clinical service and teaching in the Matthew J. Ryan Veterinary Hospital. Time outside of clinical service is used for scholarly activity, professional development, citizenship to the Department, and didactic teaching. The position includes clinical service and teaching in a busy university teaching hospital; as well as, scholarly activity.  Clinical service responsibilities will include provision of patient care and supervision of day-to-day operations of the Internal Medicine service. Teaching responsibilities will include small group and didactic instruction of veterinary students rotating through the service and mentoring of internal medicine residents. In addition, faculty will participate in classroom/laboratory teaching. A requirement of the Clinician Educator track is development of a scholarly research program that advances the field of internal medicine. Opportunities for collaborative research and clinical endeavors are abundant within the Veterinary School as well as with the nearby School of Medicine. The Department offers junior rank faculty mentoring through a specifically appointed mentoring committee as well as competitive internal funding for pilot research projects. The Section of Medicine employs eight (7 ACVIM-Small Animal Internal Medicine, 1 ECVIM-Small Animal Internal Medicine) board-certified internists in support of the program. There are currently 12 residents enrolled in the residency-training program. More than 30 Diplomates of various American and European certifying Colleges directly support the Internal Medicine program. This position is part of the School’s Standing Faculty. It is a non-tenured position, with reappointment every three years and promotion required by year nine.  Salary will be commensurate with experience. Full University benefits including vacation time, health, dental, vision, retirement, and eligible tuition reimbursement for self, spouse, and or dependents accompany the position. The anticipated start date, although negotiable, is July 2026. The hospital is a dynamic and intellectually stimulating environment with excellent nursing staff, interns, and residents. The School is located amid a vibrant urban neighborhood that offers an abundance of cultural resources including music, art, theatres, and fine dining (www.upenn.edu/life-at-penn/philadelphia). The University of Pennsylvania is a qualifying employer for the federal Public Service Loan Forgiveness (PSLF) Program. Candidates must have a veterinary degree and either be a Diplomate of the American or European College of Veterinary Internal Medicine, Specialty of Small Animal Internal Medicine, or have successfully completed an internal medicine residency program at an accredited university. Board-certification would need to be achieved within 24 months of employment. Applications will be reviewed until an appropriate candidate has been identified.  Interested applicants must apply electronically to https://apply.interfolio.com/183016 . The system will prompt you to attach a letter of intent including a research statement, curriculum vitae, and the names, addresses, phone, and contact information for three references to Dr. Christine Cain, Associate Professor of Dermatology and chair of the search committee ( ccain@vet.upenn.edu ). Questions regarding the position can be sent to Kelli Cave, search coordinator at kellijw@upenn.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/178847": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Small Animal Surgical Oncology, AC track",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Veterinary Medicine: School of Veterinary Medicine - Clinical Sciences and Advanced Medicine Location: Philadelphia The University of Pennsylvania School of Veterinary Medicine, Department of Clinical Sciences and Advanced Medicine, Section of Comprehensive Cancer Care invites qualified individuals to apply for an open faculty position in the Academic Clinician (AC) track in Small Animal Surgical Oncology at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor. Applications are currently being accepted, and the start date is negotiable. The successful candidate is expected to have history of or potential as an exemplary clinician, and educator and excel as an academic leader in their field. Members of faculty in the AC track perform 42 weeks per year of clinical patient care, which includes clinical service in surgical oncology and sharing backup on-call for surgery in the Matthew J. Ryan Veterinary Hospital.  Each clinical week is composed of 4 days of patient care and 1 non-patient care day to allow clinicians to complete records and other follow ups.  Teaching duties include both clinical and didactic teaching of veterinary students, interns, and residents in oncologic and soft tissue surgery. The AC faculty member is expected to contribute to their field through education and service that advances their field. The remaining commitment and time away from clinical service for AC faculty members is for professional development, preparation and creation of educational materials, citizenship to the Department and School, and other academic activities. Promotion along the ladder of the University Academic rank is available. The University of Pennsylvania offers an outstanding environment for clinical and educational excellence, as collaborative opportunities abound within the School of Veterinary Medicine and the wider Penn research community. The successful candidate will enjoy a wealth of opportunities that will enable the fulfillment of these responsibilities in a highly collaborative, outstanding clinical environment within a busy academic teaching hospital. The Section of Comprehensive Cancer Care and Section of Surgery employs multiple members of faculty in direct support of the program, including 4 soft tissue surgeons, 1 interventional radiologist, four medical oncologists, and two radiation oncologists.  The Hospital also has an active and robust minimally invasive soft tissue and orthopedic caseload.  House officers include six medical oncology residents, two radiation oncology interns, and a clinical trials intern. Seven surgery residents spend a minimum of 6 weeks each year in surgical oncology and 14 rotating interns spend a minimum of 2 weeks in the oncology service. Over 70 additional boarded specialists support Comprehensive Cancer Care and Surgery, including 2 clinical pathologists and 4 anatomic pathologists. Oncology clinicians enjoy the benefits of the Rosenthal Imaging and Treatment Center, including multi-slice CT, MRI, a Varian linear accelerator with IMRT capability, as well as the recently renovated Richard Lichter emergency room and state-of-the-art Advanced Dentistry and Oral Surgery Suite. Adjacent to the veterinary hospital are three world-class biomedical hospitals and research centers, which provide excellent opportunities for collaboration: The Perelman School of Medicine, the Children’s Hospital of Philadelphia, and the Wistar Institute. The PennVet Cancer Center, launched in 2017, connects our clinicians to researchers at these surrounding centers for a collaborative approach to advance global cancer research ( www.vet.upenn.edu/research/centers-laboratories/center/penn-vet-cancer-center ). The University provides a comprehensive and competitive benefits package, which includes multiple options for retirement savings and tuition benefits for the candidate’s spouse or dependent children. Philadelphia is a vibrant, historic, and welcoming city that is home to world-class museums, award-winning restaurants, diverse neighborhoods, and a thriving arts scene. The city offers endless opportunities for exploration and enrichment, with easy access to New York and Washington, DC, as well as the mountains and the shore ( www.upenn.edu/life-at-penn/philadelphia ). With extensive parks and green spaces, including the nation’s largest urban park system, Philadelphia offers a high quality of life for outdoor enthusiasts, families, and young professionals alike. Candidates must have a veterinary degree and be board-certified by the American College of Veterinary Surgeons or have successfully completed a registered surgical residency. The candidate must demonstrate expertise in surgical oncology. Fellowship training is highly desirable, with the goal of establishing a Surgical Oncology Fellowship Program. Salary will be commensurate with professional and academic credentials and experience. Applications will be accepted immediately and reviewed until an appropriate candidate is identified. A signed non-competition agreement is required for all faculty hires at Penn Vet. Interested applicants should apply online at ( https://apply.interfolio.com/178847 ). The application system will prompt you to attach a letter of intent, a statement of research and personal goals, a Curriculum Vitae, and the contact information of three references. These materials should be submitted for the attention of the Search Committee Chair, Dr. Pavlisko, Department of Clinical Sciences and Advanced Medicine. Questions should be sent to Dr. Noah Pavlisko ( pavlisko@vet.upenn.edu ) or  Kelli Cave ( kellijw@upenn.edu ), Search Coordinator."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/162287": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor, Soft Tissue Surgery, Academic Clinician (AC) Track",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Veterinary Medicine: School of Veterinary Medicine - Clinical Sciences and Advanced Medicine Location: Philadelphia The University of Pennsylvania School of Veterinary Medicine, Department of Clinical Sciences and Advanced Medicine, Section of Surgery invites qualified individuals to apply for an open faculty position in the Academic Clinician (AC) track in Small Animal Soft Tissue Surgery at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor. Faculty members in the Academic Clinician (AC) track are primarily devoted to clinical service (42 weeks a year) in the Matthew J Ryan Veterinary Hospital and participate in the clinical instruction of veterinary students, interns, and residents. Clinic time will be divided between soft tissue surgery and surgical oncology, but the amount of service in each area is flexible. Time away from clinical service is used for professional development, study for Board preparation (if needed), citizenship to the Department, and didactic teaching. Developing or maintaining an independent scholarship or research is not a focus of the AC track, is not a requirement for reappointment or promotion, and should not detract from excellence in clinical service and teaching.  Faculty AC positions are long-term, non-tenured Associated Faculty positions with reappointment every three years. Promotion along the ladder of the University Academic ranks is available. The successful candidate is expected to have a history of or potential as an exemplary clinician and educator. The successful candidate will join Penn Vet’s Section of Surgery, which employs eight faculty and seven residents. Penn Vet offers an opportunity to practice in a cutting-edge academic service within a busy urban academic teaching hospital staffed with specialists in all areas, including outstanding anesthesiology and critical care teams and a comprehensive canine and feline blood bank with an active donor program for optimal and complete peri-operative care. Mentorship and growth in the fields of renal transplantation, microvascular surgery, minimally invasive surgery, advanced upper and lower urinary tract surgery, and interventional radiology is available depending on an applicant’s interest. Surgeons enjoy the benefits of the Rosenthal Imaging and Treatment Center, including multi-slice CT, MRI, a Varian linear accelerator with IMRT capability, as well as the recently renovated Richard Lichter emergency room and state-of-the-art Advanced Dentistry and Oral Surgery Suite. Adjacent to the veterinary hospital are three world-class biomedical hospitals and research centers, which provide excellent opportunities for collaboration: The Perelman School of Medicine, the Children’s Hospital of Philadelphia, and the Wistar Institute ( https://www.vet.upenn.edu/research/centers-laboratories ). The University provides a comprehensive and competitive benefits package, including multiple retirement savings options and tuition benefits for the candidate’s spouse or dependent children. Philadelphia is a vibrant, historic, and welcoming city that is home to world-class museums, award-winning restaurants, diverse neighborhoods, and a thriving arts scene. The city offers endless opportunities for exploration and enrichment with easy access to New York and Washington DC, as well as the mountains and the shore ( www.upenn.edu/life-at-penn/philadelphia ). With extensive parks and green spaces, including the nation’s largest urban park system, Philadelphia offers a high quality of life for outdoor enthusiasts, families, and young professionals alike. Candidates must have a veterinary degree and be board-certified by the American College of Veterinary Surgeons or successfully completed an accredited surgical residency and are preparing for board-certification. Salary will be commensurate with professional and academic credentials and experience. Applications will be accepted immediately and reviewed until an appropriate candidate is identified. A signed non-competition agreement is required for all faculty hires at Penn Vet. Interested applicants should apply online at Penn’s Faculty HR website ( https://apply.interfolio.com/162287 ). The application system will prompt you to attach a letter of intent including a personal statement, a Curriculum Vitae, and the contact information of three references. These materials should be submitted for the attention of the Search Committee Chairs, Dr. Pavlisko, Department of Clinical Sciences and Advanced Medicine. Questions should be sent to Dr. Noah Pavlisko ( pavlisko@vet.upenn.edu ) or Ms. Kelli Cave ( kellijw@upenn.edu )."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189208": {
      "title": "Faculty Position (Assistant Professor) in Dairy Production Medicine, Academic Clinician Track",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Veterinary Medicine: School of Veterinary Medicine - New Bolton Center Location: Kennett Square, PA The Department of Clinical Studies-New Bolton Center at the University of Pennsylvania School of Veterinary Medicine invites applications for a faculty position in Dairy Production Medicine at the rank of Assistant Professor in the Academic Clinician Track. The Academic Clinician (AC) track emphasizes excellence in clinical service and teaching. Faculty in the AC track typically devote 90% of effort to clinical activities and 10% to academic activities. Didactic and laboratory teaching, as well as administrative or other professional responsibilities, are integrated throughout both clinical and academic efforts. The successful candidate will join the Dairy Field Investigation Team within the Section of Population Medicine and contribute to its missions of clinical service, veterinary education, and stakeholder engagement. Pennsylvania's dairy industry faces evolving challenges and opportunities related to animal health, labor, economics, technology adoption, and environmental sustainability. Clinical responsibilities of the position include working with area farms in troubleshooting these and other production and management issues, while supporting farm profitability and addressing animal welfare and environmental stewardship. For this position, demonstrated interest or experience in whole-farm evaluations encompassing diverse topics including dairy health, nutrition, reproduction, herd and data management, precision technologies, and environmental impact, is highly desirable. The successful candidate will be expected to engage with dairy producers and collaborate with key stakeholder organizations, including the Pennsylvania Center for Dairy Excellence, to develop practical, science-based solutions that support the long-term success of the modern dairy industry. Teaching responsibilities may include clinical rotations (teaching in the field), case discussions, laboratories, classroom lectures, and continuing education programs to train veterinary graduates to address the challenges facing the dairy industry. While this position does not require independent research, AC faculty are encouraged to participate in collaborative and translational scholarship. Opportunities exist to work with other Penn Vet faculty across a broad range of disciplines, including dairy nutrition, digestion and metabolism, population medicine, epidemiology, precision livestock technologies and data applications, animal welfare, early-life interventions, methane mitigation, and environmental sustainability. These collaborations provide opportunities to evaluate and implement emerging technologies and management strategies and to translate research discoveries into practical applications for commercial dairy operations. Agriculture is the cornerstone of Pennsylvania's economy, with dairy being the largest livestock contributor. Pennsylvania ranks 7 th in milk production and 2 nd in the number of dairy farms in the United States, encompassing  480,000 cows across 4,900 farms and generating 53,000 jobs throughout the dairy food supply chain. The location of New Bolton Center in southeast Pennsylvania provides exceptional access to dairy operations spanning the full spectrum of herd sizes and production systems. This diversity of farm types and management philosophies serves as a strength of the region, enabling faculty and students to address the varied needs of Pennsylvania's dairy producers. New Bolton Center is located on 700 rolling acres of beautiful pastoral land in Kennett Square , Chester County . The campus is 35 miles from Philadelphia combing the advantages of a rural setting with those of a nearby major metropolitan center. New Bolton Center provides a vibrant academic community with exceptional opportunities for interaction with a wide range of Large Animal board-certified specialists, researchers, and house officers.  Faculty members have access to the Veterinary School’s 200-cow Marshak Dairy and the 250-sow Penn Swine Teaching & Research Center for clinical and laboratory teaching and are encouraged to interact and collaborate with other departments and initiatives at New Bolton Center, including the Center for Stewardship Agriculture and Food Security , Pathobiology , and Wildlife Futures . Qualifications include a DVM/VMD (or equivalent) degree and the ability to obtain licensure in Pennsylvania. Extensive experience with dairy production systems and management is required.  Large-animal board certification or eligibility for certification (for example, with the American College of Veterinary Internal Medicine (Nutrition) or the American Board of Veterinary Practitioners) with an emphasis on food animals is desirable but not required. Application review will begin on August 13, 2026, and applications will be considered until the position is filled. Salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience.  Questions about the position can be directed to Dr. Michaela Kristula, Search Committee Chair, by email at kristula@vet.upenn.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/186691": {
      "title": "2026-2027 Assistant Professor of Marketing, The Wharton School",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: The Wharton School: The Wharton School - Marketing Location: Philadelphia, PA The Wharton Marketing Department is seeking applicants for a full-time, tenure-track faculty position at the level of Assistant Professor. The department consists of a large group of faculty with varied research interests, and we offer degree programs at the undergraduate, MBA, and Ph.D. levels. Our goal is to attract individuals who have strong potential for excellence in research and teaching, and who will enrich our academic environment. Information about the Marketing Department and the research expertise of its current faculty may be found at: Marketing Department We will be interviewing candidates, virtually, in August to meet with outstanding individuals who are interested in tenure-track faculty positions in Marketing at the Wharton School. Individuals who are interested are encouraged to submit materials for review. Applicants should have interests in areas such as Marketing, Psychology, Economics, Statistics, Neuroscience, Computer and Information Science, and related disciplines. Demonstrated ability or potential for high-quality research and teaching are essential. Applicants must have a Ph.D. (expected completion by June 30, 2028, is acceptable) from an accredited institution. The appointment is expected to begin on July 1, 2027. To be considered for an interview during The Wharton School’s 2026 Recruitment cycle (August 2026), please submit your application to us by July 15, 2026. Applications will be reviewed until the position is filled. You may email us at mktg-recruitment@wharton.upenn.edu with any questions. All applicants must complete and submit all required materials via the online application in order to be considered. All application materials must be submitted in PDF format only. Required: Cover Letter Required: Current CV Required: Recommendations (2 Minimum, up to 3) Required: Job Market Paper Abstract Required: Job Market Paper Required: Personal Statement Optional: Up to 2 additional papers"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/182267": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty Position in Wharton Accounting Department",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: The Wharton School: The Wharton School - Accounting Location: Philadelphia, PA The Wharton School’s Accounting Department has a need for highly qualified adjunct faculty to staff a variety of courses in Financial and Managerial Accounting. All instruction will be conducted in person on the University’s campus in Philadelphia, PA. This is a part-time position and is not eligible for tenure. Since excellence in teaching is the primary criteria for this position, applicants must have outstanding communication skills, along with an interest in teaching undergraduate and/or MBA students. Applicants must have university-level teaching experience and a PhD degree from an accredited institution. To apply you can submit the following materials through our online application system: ·        Cover letter ·        Curriculum vitae ·        Teaching Evaluations & Associated Course Syllabi from Past Teaching Experience ·        Contact information for three references who will automatically receive requests for letters of recommendation. If review of your qualifications result in a decision to pursue candidacy, you will be contacted directly."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193740": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Accounting",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: The Wharton School: The Wharton School - Accounting Location: Philadelphia The Wharton School's Accounting Department is seeking applicants for a full-time, tenure-track faculty position as an Assistant Professor.  Applicants should have interests in areas such as Financial, Managerial, and Tax Accounting.  Demonstrated ability or potential for high quality research and teaching are important.  The appointment is expected to begin July 1, 2027. Applicants must have a Ph.D. (expected completion by June 30, 2028 is acceptable) from an accredited institution an outstanding research record or potential. Applications, including cover letter, curriculum vitae, and recent publications or working papers, must be submitted online (beginning, September 17, 2026): Candidates should also include contact information for three references who will be asked to submit letters of recommendation."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193112": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Business Economics and Public Policy (Tenure Track) 2026-2027",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: The Wharton School: The Wharton School - Business Economics and Public Policy Location: Philadelphia, PA The Business Economics and Public Policy Department of the Wharton School, University of Pennsylvania, is seeking applicants for a full-time, tenure-track faculty position at the Assistant Professor level. The Department encompasses microeconomics fields and participates in teaching undergraduate and MBA business economics. The Department also offers a PhD in Applied Economics. The appointment is expected to begin July 1, 2027. Information about the Business Economics and Public Policy Department at Wharton may be found at: https://bepp.wharton.upenn.edu . Excellence in research and teaching are the primary criteria for selection.Applicants must have a Ph.D. from an accredited institution (expected completion by June 30, 2028 is acceptable). Applications, including cover letter, curriculum vitae, statements, and recent publications or working papers, must be submitted online by November 15th, 2026. Candidates must also include contact information for three references who will be asked to submit letters of recommendation. We encourage early submission of applications as they will be reviewed until our deadline of November 15, 2026. Department representatives will be conducting first round interviews of prospective candidates in mid-December via Zoom. FURTHER INFORMATION: Contact Dhivya Kaushik, dhivya@wharton.upenn.edu Business Economics and Public Policy Department The Wharton School, University of Pennsylvania 3rd floor Dinan Hall, 3733 Spruce Street Philadelphia, PA 19104"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191776": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Finance (Tenure Track) 2026 - 2027",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: The Wharton School: The Wharton School - Finance Location: Philadelphia, PA The Finance Department of the Wharton School is seeking applicants for a full-time, tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor. Applicants should have interests in areas such as Corporate Finance, Asset Pricing, and Macroeconomics. The Wharton School offers Finance courses at the undergraduate, MBA, and doctoral levels. The appointment is expected to begin July 1, 2027. Information about the Finance Department and the research expertise of its current faculty may be found here: https://fnce.wharton.upenn.edu/ If you should need to contact the Finance Department, please email the Recruitment Coordinator at fnce-recruitcoord@wharton.upenn.edu Applicants must have a PhD (expected completion by June 30, 2028 is acceptable) from an accredited institution. Demonstrated ability or potential for high quality research and teaching are important. Applicants should complete and submit the following materials in PDF format only in order to be considered. Required: Cover Letter Required: Curriculum Vitae Required: Job Market Paper Required: Contact information for three references who will be asked to submit letters of recommendation. Optional: 4th Letter of Recommendation Optional: Additional Documents Applicants are strongly encouraged to have their completed application submitted by Friday, November 20, 2026 for full consideration."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192153": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Operations, Information and Decisions (Tenure Track) 2026-2027",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: The Wharton School: The Wharton School - Operations, Information and Decisions Location: 3730 Walnut St, Philadelphia, PA. USA The Operations, Information and Decisions Department of the Wharton School is seeking applicants for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in the areas of decision making, information systems or operations management. Our faculty is comprised of scholars with a diverse set of backgrounds and interests, and we encourage applicants with degrees in a business discipline, social sciences, or engineering, among others. More information about the Department is available at: https://oid.wharton.upenn.edu/ Demonstrated ability or potential for high quality research and teaching are important. Applicants must have a Ph.D. (expected completion by June 30, 2028 is acceptable) from an accredited institution. The appointment is expected to begin July 1, 2027. Interested individuals should complete and submit an online application via our secure website, and must include: •                 A curriculum vitae •                 A job market or other sample paper •                 Names of three recommenders, including email addresses Additional materials or information will be requested as needed. To ensure full consideration, materials should be submitted by October, 23, 2026."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193636": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Real Estate (Tenure Track) 2026-2027",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: The Wharton School: The Wharton School - Real Estate Location: Philadelphia, PA The Wharton School’s Real Estate Department invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor faculty position. We seek candidates in applied economics, finance, or related fields who can make significant contributions to real estate economics/finance or urban economics. While applicants may pursue a range of research topics, part of their research should address issues in real estate or urban economics, broadly defined. A strong commitment to excellent teaching in real estate is required. Information about the Wharton Real Estate Program can be found at: http://real-estate.wharton.upenn.edu/ and at http://realestate.wharton.upenn.edu/ Applicants must have a Ph.D. (expected completion by June 30, 2028, is acceptable) from an accredited institution and have an outstanding research record or potential. Expected appointment start date is July 1, 2027. Please submit applications on-line beginning September 17, 2026. Applicants should include a cover letter, curriculum vitae, recent publications or working papers, and contact information for three references who will be asked to submit recommendation letters. Candidates are strongly encouraged to have their completed applications submitted by November 16, 2026, for full consideration. First-round interviews will be conducted virtually."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193446": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Statistics and Data Science (Tenure-Track) 2026 - 2027",
      "description": "Posted: Sep 14, 2026 Department: University of Pennsylvania: The Wharton School: The Wharton School - Statistics and Data Science Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Statistics and Data Science of the Wharton School, University of Pennsylvania, is seeking applicants for a full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor position. The appointment is expected to begin July 1, 2027. Applicants must show outstanding capacity and achievement in research, as well as excellent teaching and communication skills. Applicants must have a Ph.D. or equivalent (expected completion by June 30, 2028 is acceptable) from an accredited institution. To apply, please submit the following materials through our online application system: Cover letter. Curriculum vitae. Statements on research and teaching. Contact information for three references who will automatically receive requests for letters of recommendation. Up to 3 selected publications or preprints if available (optional). For full consideration, candidates are encouraged to have all required application documents submitted by November 15, 2026. Any questions can be sent to statistics.recruit@wharton.upenn.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193745": {
      "title": "Associate or Full Professor of Accounting",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: The Wharton School: The Wharton School - Accounting Location: Philadelphia The Wharton School's Accounting Department is seeking applicants for a full-time, tenure-track faculty position as an Associate or Full Professor.  Applicants should have interest in areas such as Financial, Managerial, and Tax Accounting.  Demonstrated ability or potential for high quality research and teaching are important.  The appointment is expected to begin July 1, 2027. Applicants must have a Ph.D. from an accredited institution and have an outstanding research record or potential. Applications, including cover letter, curriculum vitae, and recent publications or working papers, must be submitted online (Beginning from September 17, 2026)."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191778": {
      "title": "Associate or Full Professor of Finance (Tenure Track) 2026 - 2027",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: The Wharton School: The Wharton School - Finance Location: Philadelphia, PA The Finance Department of the Wharton School is seeking applicants for a tenure-track faculty position at the level of Full and Associate Professor. Applicants should have interests in areas such as Corporate Finance, Asset Pricing, and Macroeconomics. The Wharton School offers finance courses at the undergraduate, MBA, and doctoral levels. The appointment is expected to begin July 1, 2027.  Information about the Finance Department and the research expertise of its current faculty may be found at: https://fnce.wharton.upenn.edu/ If you should need to contact the Finance Department, please email the Recruitment Coordinator at fnce-recruitcoord@wharton.upenn.edu Excellence in research and teaching are the primary criteria for selection. Applicants must have a Ph.D. from an accredited institution. Applicants should complete and submit the following materials in PDF format only in order to be considered. Required: Curriculum Vitae Optional: Additional Documents"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193459": {
      "title": "Associate Professor of Statistics and Data Science (Tenure-Track) 2026 - 2027",
      "description": "Posted: Sep 16, 2026 Department: University of Pennsylvania: The Wharton School: The Wharton School - Statistics and Data Science Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Statistics and Data Science of the Wharton School, University of Pennsylvania, is seeking applicants for a full-time, tenure-track Associate Professor position. The appointment is expected to begin July 1, 2027. Applicants must show outstanding capacity and achievement in research, as well as excellent teaching and communication skills. Applicants must have a Ph.D. or equivalent from an accredited institution. To apply, please submit the following materials through our online application system: Cover letter. Curriculum vitae. Statements on research and teaching. Up to 3 selected publications or preprints if available (optional). For full consideration, candidates are encouraged to have all required application documents submitted by November 15, 2026. Any questions can be sent to statistics.recruit@wharton.upenn.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191074": {
      "title": "Management Faculty Recruitment (Tenure Track Assistant Professor) 2026-2027",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: The Wharton School: The Wharton School - Management Location: Philadelphia, PA, United States The Management Department of the Wharton School is seeking applicants for a full-time, tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor in the areas of: Entrepreneurial Management Human Resource Management Multinational/Comparative Management Organizational Behavior Strategic Management Demonstrated ability or potential for high quality research and teaching are important. Applicants must have a Ph.D. (expected completion by June 30, 2028 is acceptable) from an accredited institution. The appointment is expected to begin July 1, 2027. Interested individuals should complete and submit the required materials in PDF format. Required: Cover Letter Required: Curriculum Vitae Required: 3 Letters of Recommendation Required: Job Market Paper and Abstract Optional: 4th Letter of Recommendation Optional: Research Statement Optional: Additional Documents Recruitment Committee review of applications will begin on September 15, 2026 . Candidates are strongly encouraged to have their completed application submitted by that date for full consideration. If you have any questions please contact the Management Department Recruitment Coordinator at mgmt-recruitment@wharton.upenn.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191064": {
      "title": "Management Faculty Recruitment (Tenure Track Associate or Full Professor) 2026-2027",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: The Wharton School: The Wharton School - Management Location: University of Pennsylvania, Phila., PA  The Management Department of the Wharton School is seeking applicants for a full-time, tenure track faculty position at the rank of Associate or Full Professor in the areas of: Entrepreneurial Management Human Resource Management Multinational/Comparative Managment Organizational Behavior Strategic Management Demonstrated ability or potential for high quality research and teaching are important. Applicants must have a Ph.D. from an accredited institution. The appointment is expected to begin July 1, 2027. Interested individuals are required to complete and submit the required materials in PDF format: Cover letter (indicating the areas for which you wish to be considered) Curriculum Vitae Research Statement Recruitment Committee review of applications will begin on September 15, 2026. Candidates are strongly encouraged to have their completed applications submitted by that date for full consideration. If you have questions, please contact the Management Department Recruitment Coordinator at mgmt-recruitment@wharton.upenn.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/182270": {
      "title": "Part-time or Full-time Lecturer in the Wharton Accounting Department",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: The Wharton School: The Wharton School - Accounting Location: Philadelphia, PA The Wharton School’s Accounting Department has a need for highly qualified full-time or part-time lecturers to staff a variety of courses in Financial and Managerial Accounting. Lecturers will teach in-person courses on the University’s campus in Philadelphia, PA. These positions are not eligible for tenure. Since excellence in teaching is the primary criteria for this position, applicants must have outstanding communication skills, along with an interest in teaching undergraduate and/or MBA students. Applicants must have a graduate degree from an accredited institution. University-level teaching experience is required. To apply, please submit the following materials through our online application system: · Cover letter · Curriculum vitae · Teaching Evaluations & Associated Course Syllabi from Past Teaching Experience · Contact information for three references who will automatically receive requests for letters of recommendation. If review of your qualifications results in a decision to pursue candidacy, you will be contacted directly."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193500": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor in City and Regional Planning",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Weitzman School of Design: Weitzman School of Design - City and Regional Planning Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of City & Regional Planning at the Stuart Weitzman School of Design at the University of Pennsylvania seeks an outstanding scholar for a full-time, tenure-track position with responsibilities for teaching, research, and service. Candidates at the assistant and associate professor levels will be considered for the position. We seek an exceptional scholar and educator whose research and practice contribute to our department in one or more of the following areas: Urban and Regional Economics; Public Finance; Real Estate and Housing Economics and/or Finance; Housing Planning and/or Policy; and Land Use Planning. The successful candidate will have: 1) a proven track record of scholarly research in one or more of these areas; 2) connections to the broader field of city and regional planning; 3) the ability to teach existing and new courses relevant to the core or concentrations in their area of urban planning specialty; 4) the ability and desire to lead a departmental concentration, certificate program, and/or perform similar administrative duties; 5) leadership experience or potential, including the ability to collaborate and make connections across departments/programs/schools/centers. The Weitzman School of Design includes City and Regional Planning, Architecture, Fine Arts, Historic Preservation, and Landscape Architecture. The Department of City and Regional Planning offers a two-year Master of City Planning degree, a one-year Master of Urban Spatial Analytics, and a PhD. We welcome the new faculty member to participate in the various research centers located at the School and University. Minimum qualifications: a PhD in Urban Planning or a related field by the start of employment. Applicants should submit a statement of interest that describes their relevant experience, a research statement outlining their agenda for research and scholarship, a curriculum vitae, two recent publications or writing samples, a teaching statement, and the names and contact information for three references who will be contacted at a later date. Please keep the statement of interest under 500 words and the research and teaching statements under 1,000 words each. Review of applications will begin on November 1, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. The position will begin on July 1, 2027, or on a mutually agreed date thereafter. Applications should be addressed to the Search Committee, Department of City & Regional Planning. Questions should be directed to Kate Daniel, Department Coordinator, City & Regional Planning, at katf@design.upenn.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189248": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor, Landscape Architecture",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Weitzman School of Design: Weitzman School of Design - Landscape Architecture Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Landscape Architecture at the University of Pennsylvania’s Stuart Weitzman School of Design welcomes highly qualified individuals to apply for an Assistant or Associate Professor position to offer graduate-level instruction in landscape architectural design and to conduct innovative design research that enhances disciplinary knowledge and expands the boundaries of our field. The Department of Landscape Architecture is recognized internationally for its innovative ecological approach to the design of landscapes, public works, public spaces, and infrastructures. This approach addresses the rich and entangled web of everyday environmental relationships between living things—humans, plants, and animals—and the mineral world. We assert that a deep understanding of these ecologies is necessary to address the ongoing climate polycrisis and move toward reparative social, environmental, and multispecies justice. In addition to the Department of Landscape Architecture, the Weitzman School of Design includes the distinguished departments of Historic Preservation, City & Regional Planning, Architecture, and Fine Arts, providing the potential for rich collaboration with other disciplines. The Weitzman School prepares students to address complex sociocultural and environmental issues through thoughtful inquiry, creative expression, and innovation. As a diverse community of scholars and practitioners, we are committed to advancing the public good—both locally and globally—through art, design, planning, and preservation. This position is a full-time tenure-track or tenured appointment at either the Assistant or Associate Professor level, with responsibilities for departmental teaching, research, and service, with opportunities for scholarly engagement across the school’s five departments. The specific rank of this appointment will be determined based on the experience and qualifications of the preferred candidate. The successful candidate will join a supportive departmental community of scholars, practitioners, and students who are actively engaged in advancing both the discipline and profession of landscape architecture. The University of Pennsylvania offers competitive salaries and excellent benefits for its full-time faculty and their eligible dependents. We are committed to caring for each faculty member, their work-life balance, and their family’s overall well-being. The successful candidate will be expected to teach graduate design studios and seminar courses in landscape architecture, including elective courses in areas of specialization that reflect the candidate’s research expertise and interests. Undergraduate teaching and mentorship of students pursuing the Landscape Studies minor of the University of Pennsylvania’s School of Arts and Sciences is also possible. Teaching responsibilities will include core graduate design studios in the Department of Landscape Architecture and elective seminars open to graduate students across the Weitzman School of Design. In addition to demonstrating pedagogical innovation and effectiveness in teaching, the successful applicant must provide evidence of a strong capacity to produce scholarly and creative work through a record of previous publications, ongoing design research, and plans for future research. We also welcome the new faculty member to participate in various centers and institutes at both the Weitzman School of Design and across the broader University, particularly the McHarg Center for Urbanism and Ecology, our department’s faculty research center. Candidates must hold a professionally accredited master’s degree in landscape architecture, or a terminal degree in a closely related field. A PhD in landscape architecture or another related field is an enhancement but is not required. Applicants should submit a cover letter that includes a succinct description of their agenda for design research and scholarship as well as their teaching philosophy; a curriculum vitae; two recent publications or writing samples; two recent course syllabi and an accompanying teaching portfolio of student work, not to exceed 15MB; and a design portfolio of individual creative work, not to exceed 15MB. In addition, applicants should provide the names of three references who will be contacted at a later time. Applications should be addressed to the Chair of the Faculty Search Committee, and all materials should be submitted via the “Apply Now” link on this page.  ( https://apply.interfolio.com/189248) Review of applications will begin on October 1, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. The position will begin on July 1, 2027, and the selected candidate is expected to be in residence in Philadelphia to commence teaching in fall 2027. Questions should be directed to Melissa DiGiacomo, Assistant Director of Administration / Department Coordinator, Landscape Architecture, at: landarch@design.upenn.edu"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/187657": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor, Historic Preservation - Conservation",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Weitzman School of Design: Weitzman School of Design - Historic Preservation Location: Philadelphia, PA The Department of Historic Preservation at the Stuart Weitzman School of Design, University of Pennsylvania, welcomes applications for an Assistant, Associate, or full Professor to conduct graduate-level instruction, advising, and research in architectural conservation. We especially seek scholars with experience and demonstrated leadership in: 1) research, teaching, and practice related to architectural conservation and heritage materials science; 2) lab-based pedagogy; and 3) field-based professional practice. The Department of Historic Preservation provides an integrated approach to training architects, landscape architects, planners, historians, archaeologists, conservators, managers, and other professionals to understand, sustain, and transform the existing built environment. Through two master’s degree programs, we offer concentrated study of architectural conservation, public history of the built environment, preservation planning, and preservation design. The successful candidate will be expected to teach courses at the graduate level, including the introduction to architectural conservation principles and practices (including laboratory-based analysis), specialized seminars focused on particular material types and conservation issues, applied courses (studios or workshops) using field-based learning, and/or other courses that suit the candidate’s research. The position also includes leadership of the architectural conservation lab for teaching and research, advising of master’s students, and the possibility of supporting doctoral students in allied departments. The new faculty member will also participate in and cultivate their own funded research projects, supported through the Department’s research center, the Preservation Research Collaborative , and other centers at the School and University. Future opportunities may exist to take on leadership responsibilities in the Department and/or Preservation Research Collaborative. This position is a tenure-track or tenured appointment at the Assistant, Associate, or full Professor level, with responsibilities for teaching, research, practice, and service. Rank determined based on review of experience. Research-based practice will be suited to the candidate’s own research agenda, but may include such activities as: partnerships with public or private heritage-managing agencies and organizations; documentation, conservation management planning, and other field projects; academic research on materials or treatments; and exhibitions and publishing. In addition to the Department of Historic Preservation, the Weitzman School includes the departments of City & Regional Planning, Architecture, Landscape Architecture, and Fine Arts. The Weitzman School prepares students to address complex sociocultural and environmental issues through thoughtful inquiry, creative expression, and innovation. As a diverse community of scholars and practitioners, we are committed to advancing the public good—both locally and globally—through art, design, planning, and preservation. Minimum qualifications: 1) Experience in the practice of architectural conservation and/or heritage materials science, 2) teaching experience, and 3) a PhD or other terminal degree in historic preservation, architecture, physical sciences, heritage science, archaeology, or another related field. Applicants should submit a cover letter that succinctly describes their agenda for research, scholarship, and practice; a curriculum vitae; two recent publications or writing samples; a teaching statement that addresses the above teaching expectations; and the names and contact information for three references who may be contacted at a later date. Applications should be addressed to Chair, Search Committee. Review of applications will begin on November 1, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. The position will begin on July 1, 2027, and the selected candidate will be expected to begin teaching in fall 2027. Questions should be directed to Micah Dornfeld, Department Coordinator, Historic Preservation, at: micahdor@design.upenn.edu ."
    },
