    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196020": {
      "title": "AC Faculty Position in Hematological Malignancies/Leukemia - Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Department of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Medicine - Hematology/Oncology Location: Philadelphia, PA Posted: Oct 22, 2026 Deadline: Oct 22, 2028 The Division of Hematology-Oncology in the Department of Medicine at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for several Assistant Professor positions in the non-tenure academic clinician track. Expertise is required in the specific area of leukemia. Applicants must have an M.D. or equivalent degree. Board certification or board eligibility in Hematology and/or Medical Oncology is required. Teaching responsibilities may include teaching responsibilities may include teaching hematology/oncology fellows, house staff and medical students. The academic clinician track requires 100 credits per year of active and high-quality teaching. Clinical responsibilities may include patient care in the area of Hematological Malignancies (Acute Leukemia). The Leukemia Program encompasses a busy inpatient service working with APP's, fellows, and residents, as well as an outpatient clinic with an emphasis on clinical care and clinical research."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196021": {
      "title": "AC Faculty Position Thoracic Oncology - Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Department of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Medicine - Hematology/Oncology Location: Philadelphia, PA Posted: Oct 22, 2026 Deadline: Oct 22, 2028 The Division of Hematology-Oncology in the Department of Medicine at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for several Assistant Professor positions in the non-tenure academic clinician track. Expertise is required in the specific area of thoracic oncology. Applicants must have an M.D. or equivalent degree. Board certified or board eligible in Hematology and/or Medical Oncology is required. Teaching responsibilities may include teaching hematology/oncology fellows, house staff and medical students. The academic clinician track requires 100 credits per year of active and high-quality teaching. Clinical responsibilities may include patient care in the area of thoracic cancers in an outpatient setting, and the care of general solid tumor oncology patients as an attending on the inpatient service at the Hospital of the University of Pennsylvania."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196045": {
      "title": "AC Track Faculty Position, Palliative Oncology – Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Department of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Medicine - Hematology/Oncology Location: Philadelphia, PA Posted: Oct 22, 2026 Deadline: Oct 22, 2028 The Division of Hematology-Oncology in the Department of Medicine at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for several Assistant Professor positions in the non-tenure academic clinician track. Expertise is required in the specific area of palliative oncology. Candidates should possess leadership skills to interact with a wide range of stakeholders in the university, health system, and community. Applicants must have an M.D. or equivalent degree. Board certification or eligibility in Medical Oncology is required. Teaching responsibilities may include palliative care fellows who rotate through the Palliative and Supportive Oncology Clinic and have a strong background and interest in education to further the Division’s commitment to equip front-line oncology clinicians and oncology trainees with basic palliative care skills in serious illness communication and symptom management. The academic clinician track requires 100 credits per year of active and high-quality teaching. Clinical responsibilities may include excellent clinical skills in providing ambulatory palliative care consultation for cancer patients in close partnership with each patient’s oncology team. The successful candidate will develop and pilot embedded palliative and supportive oncology clinics at Abramson Cancer Center sites in the greater Philadelphia area. The successful candidate will have opportunities to partner with leadership in the Division of Hematology and Oncology and the Cancer Service Line to lead quality improvement initiatives aimed at improving end-of-life outcomes for cancer patients throughout Penn Medicine."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195871": {
      "title": "Academic Clinician Track - Assistant Professor in Dermatology",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Dermatology Location: Philadelphia, PA Posted: Oct 20, 2026 Deadline: Oct 20, 2028 The Department of Dermatology at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for an Assistant Professor position in the non-tenure academic clinician track. Applicants must have an M.D. or M.D./Ph.D. or equivalent degree. Applicants must be board-certified or board eligible in Dermatology. Teaching responsibilities may include clinical teaching of residents and/or fellows. Clinical responsibilities may include providing health care services at University of Pennsylvania dermatology clinics. Clinicians may see patients at several locations, including but not limited to Perelman Center for Advanced Medicine, Penn Medicine University City, Dermatology at Pennsylvania Hospital, Dermatology at Bucks County, and Dermatology at Radnor."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195878": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Pediatric and Fetal Surgery at CHOP (Tenure)",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Surgery Location: Philadelphia, PA Posted: Oct 23, 2026 Deadline: Oct 23, 2028 Children's Hospital of Philadelphia and the Department of Surgery at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seek candidates for an Assistant Professor position in the tenure track. Expertise is required in the specific area of pediatric surgery and fetal surgery. Applicants must have an M.D. or M.D./Ph.D. or equivalent degree. Applicants must be certified or eligible for certification by the American Board of Surgery and hold special ABS certification (or eligibility) in pediatric surgery. Teaching responsibilities may include instruction of medical students, residents and fellows. Clinical responsibilities may include building a clinical practice in pediatric general thoracic and fetal surgery. Research or scholarship responsibilities may include pursuing research in aspects of fetal biology, including in utero gene editing and in utero hematopoietic stem cell transplantation. Applicants must have training in clinical fetal therapy. Candidates with research fellowship training and showing evidence of scholarship will be preferred."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195830": {
      "error": "RuntimeError: interfolio position has no name (closed?)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194591": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Tenure Track, CHOP Division of Hematology",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Pediatrics: Perelman School of Medicine - Pediatrics - Hematology Location: Philadelphia, PA Posted: Oct 23, 2026 Deadline: Oct 23, 2028 Children's Hospital of Philadelphia and the Division of Hematology in the Department of Pediatrics at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seek candidates for an Assistant Professor position in the tenure track. Applicants must have an M.D and/or Ph.D or equivalent degree. Teaching responsibilities may include the education and mentoring of graduate students and postdoctoral fellows. Clinical responsibilities may include inpatient and outpatient services according to interest for MDs or MD/PhDs. Research or scholarship responsibilities may include independent research pursuing fundamental or translational research directly relevant to benign hematology, hematopoiesis, stem cell biology, hemostasis and thrombosis, red blood cell disorders including hemoglobinopathies, bone marrow failure syndromes, or other hematologic diseases. The successful candidate will be expected to secure independent research grant support, usually from federal organizations, in addition to support from participation in collaborative grants. As a CHOP-based Penn faculty member, the candidate will have access to exceptional institutional resources, including state-of-the-art core facilities, robust computational and genomic infrastructure, extensive opportunities for collaboration across CHOP and Penn, and unique access to clinical investigators, patient cohorts, and biospecimen resources. A recruitment package, dedicated laboratory space, and outstanding mentorship opportunities will be provided."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196088": {
      "title": "Instructor, Hospitalist - Global Health Fellowship, Division of Hospital Medicine",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Department of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Medicine - Hospital Medicine Location: Philadelphia, PA Posted: Oct 21, 2026 Deadline: Oct 21, 2028 The Division of Hospital Medicine in the Department of Medicine at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for several Instructor positions in the Academic Support Staff. This appointment will be initially for one (1) year and continuation during that time period and renewal are based on satisfactory performance and availability of funding (limited to three (3) years). Applicants must have an M.D. or equivalent degree. Candidates must be board certified or board eligible in Internal Medicine or Family Medicine. Responsibilities may include a combination of weeks and weekends on teaching service at the Hospital of the University of Pennsylvania (HUP), HUP Cedar Campus, Penn Presbyterian Medical Center (PPMC) and/or Good Shephard Penn Partners Long-term Acute Care (LTACH). The successful applicant will have an opportunity to participate in a unique non-ACGME fellowship in global health. The incumbent will be provided with structured mentorship in global health and academic hospital medicine as well as generous financial allowances for international travel and housing."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195671": {
      "title": "Medical Director, Kidney Transplant Program - Clinician Educator",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Department of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Medicine - Renal-Electrolyte and Hypertension Location: Philadelphia, PA Posted: Oct 21, 2026 Deadline: Oct 21, 2028 The Division of Renal-Electrolyte and Hypertension in the Department of Medicine at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for an Associate or Full Professor position in the non-tenure clinician educator track. Expertise is required in the specific area of clinical, regulatory and administrative leadership of an adult kidney transplant program. Applicants must have an M.D. or M.D./Ph.D. or equivalent degree. Board certification in Nephrology is required. Candidates must meet OPTN/UNOS criteria to serve as a primary kidney transplant physician. Teaching responsibilities may include instruction and supervision of nephrology and transplant nephrology fellows, internal medicine residents, and medical students in the inpatient transplant service, consult service, and outpatient transplant clinics. Clinical responsibilities may include evaluation and management of kidney and kidney-pancreas transplant candidates and recipients, as well as living kidney donor candidates, in the outpatient setting, and attending on the inpatient kidney transplant service. The selected candidate will have experience working with advanced practice providers, transplant coordinators, and trainees. Research or scholarship responsibilities may include leading in clinical, translational, or health services research and scholarly activities related to kidney transplantation. Scholarship is required for faculty appointed to the Clinician Educator track and may include collaborative research, peer-reviewed publications, and other academic contributions that advance the field of kidney transplantation. The Medical Director will oversee the clinical, operational, and regulatory responsibilities of the Kidney Transplant Program in partnership with the Surgical Director and Penn Transplant Institute leadership, with goals of patient safety, access to transplant, organ utilization, and program growth. Responsibilities include stewardship of waitlist management, selection committee processes, quality and performance improvement, and compliance with OPTN/UNOS, CMS, and SRTR requirements. The Medical Director will serve as an internal and external spokesperson for the program and participate in all transplant-related clinical and administrative meetings. The Renal-Electrolyte and Hypertension Division is a highly collaborative academic and clinical environment with over 50 faculty and ACGME-accredited general nephrology and transplant nephrology fellowships. The Division provides exceptional clinical care across general nephrology, dialysis, and transplant nephrology, as well as specialty areas such as glomerular disease, rare and inherited kidney disease, polycystic kidney disease, onconephrology, neuroendocrine tumors, and hypertension. Penn performed the region's first kidney transplant in 1966, and the Penn Transplant Institute is the largest transplant center in the region. As a member of the standing faculty on the Clinician Educator track, the successful candidate will be expected to demonstrate sustained academic scholarship and contributions to the field of kidney transplantation through scholarly publications, research, and other academic activities consistent with the expectations of the Clinician Educator track. The ideal candidate will be an experienced transplant nephrologist with strong leadership skills who is committed to patient-centered care, equitable access to transplantation, and multidisciplinary collaboration. Academic rank will be commensurate with experience."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195919": {
      "title": "Post-doctoral Fellow - Epidemiology, Traumatic Brain Injury, Brain Aging, and Dementia",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: Perelman School of Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania Posted: Oct 19, 2026 Dr. Andrea Schneider in the Departments of Neurology and Epidemiology at the University of Pennsylvania is seeking a highly motivated Postdoctoral Fellow to join an NIH-funded research program focused on understanding how traumatic brain injury (TBI) contributes to cognitive decline and Alzheimer’s disease and related dementias (AD/ADRD) across the life course. TBI is an important and potentially modifiable risk factor for dementia, yet the mechanisms through which TBI influences long-term cognitive health—and the time course over which these associations emerge—remain poorly understood. This project addresses these critical gaps by leveraging data from more than 40,000 participants from four large, NIH-funded prospective cohort studies: the National Longitudinal Study of Adolescent to Adult Health (Add Health), Atherosclerosis Risk in Communities (ARIC) Study, Coronary Artery Risk Development in Young Adults (CARDIA) Study, and Multi-Ethnic Study of Atherosclerosis (MESA). The project uses an accelerated longitudinal/cohort-sequential design and provides an opportunity to work with exceptionally rich longitudinal data spanning adolescence through older adulthood. The post-doctoral fellow will play a key role in a collaborative research program investigating how cumulative TBI exposure across the life course relates to cognitive trajectories and incident AD/ADRD; how pre- and post-injury vascular risk factors (including hypertension, diabetes, hyperlipidemia, smoking, and obesity) modify these associations; and how TBI relates to biomarkers of neurodegeneration and vascular disease. Open to applications from U.S. citizens and foreign nationals. Research Opportunities Depending on the post-doctoral fellow's interests and experience, projects may include: Harmonizing TBI, cognitive, vascular, biomarker, and neuroimaging measures across large longitudinal cohorts Examining the relationship between cumulative TBI exposure and long-term cognitive trajectories and dementia risk Developing novel life-course approaches to understanding the intersection of TBI, vascular disease, brain aging, and AD/ADRD Developing complementary independent research questions and manuscripts based on the post-doctoral fellow's own scientific interests Training and Mentorship The post-doctoral fellow will receive close mentorship in neuroepidemiology, longitudinal epidemiologic methods, life-course epidemiology, dementia epidemiology, biomarker analyses, and analysis of large population-based cohorts. The position is designed to support the post-doctoral fellow's development toward an independent research career, with opportunities to lead publications, present findings at national and international meetings, develop grant applications, and pursue independent research questions. The fellow will be part of a highly collaborative research environment spanning neurology, epidemiology, biostatistics, neurocritical care, and population health. There will also be opportunities to collaborate with investigators across institutions. Applicants should have: Doctoral degree in epidemiology, biostatistics, or a related field Strong quantitative and analytical skills Experience with statistical programming (e.g., R, Stata, or SAS) Experience analyzing longitudinal epidemiologic data is preferred Strong scientific writing and communication skills An interest in TBI, dementia/ADRD, neuroepidemiology, aging, biomarkers, or related areas Experience with large population-based cohorts, longitudinal data analysis, survival analysis, biomarkers, or neuroimaging would be particularly valuable, but candidates with strong quantitative training and an interest in developing expertise in these areas are encouraged to apply. Applicants should submit a cover letter, CV, research statement (2 pages maximum), and contact information for three references. Materials should be submitted through interfolio. Any questions, or follow up inquiries, can be emailed to Dr. Andrea Schneider (Andrea.Schneider@pennmedicine.upenn.edu). Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196026": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Position in Cancer Metabolism and Molecular Imaging",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: Perelman School of Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania - School of Medicine Posted: Oct 21, 2026 Dr. David Tischfield, Assistant Professor of Radiology at the University of Pennsylvania, is seeking a postdoctoral researcher to join his research program within the Penn Image-Guided Interventions Laboratory, directed by Dr. Terence Gade. Based in the Departments of Radiology and Cancer Biology, the laboratory brings together investigators with complementary expertise in cancer biology, chemistry, engineering, imaging, and clinical medicine. The fellow will work closely with Dr. Tischfield and collaborate with other co-principal investigators and team members on integrated studies that draw on this multidisciplinary expertise. Dr. Tischfield’s research program investigates cancer metabolism and the tumor microenvironment to develop molecular imaging approaches and therapeutic strategies. The position emphasizes preclinical hyperpolarized carbon-13 MRI, PET, and mass spectrometry–based metabolomics, integrating imaging with mechanistic studies in cells and animal models of cancer. The postdoctoral researcher will initially contribute to completing ongoing studies and will then take increasing ownership of projects leading to first-author publications. Working closely with Dr. Tischfield and collaborators, the fellow will design experiments, interpret results, prepare manuscripts, and help shape new research directions. Research activities include: Preclinical hyperpolarized MRI and PET studies of tumor metabolism and treatment response Metabolomics and stable isotope tracing experiments Mechanistic studies using mouse models, mammalian cell culture, and molecular and biochemical assays Integration of imaging, mass spectrometry, and biological data Maintenance of rigorous experimental records Communication of findings through manuscripts and scientific presentations A minimum two-year commitment is expected, with a preferred duration of four years to support project development and first-author publications. Please note that all appointments are for one year-periods, with reappointment on a yearly basis. Applicants must hold a PhD or equivalent doctoral degree by the start date. Candidates from a broad range of fields are encouraged to apply, including: Cancer biology Biochemistry Chemistry Biomedical engineering Physics Related disciplines We recognize that relevant experience varies across fields and will consider each applicant’s overall research background and potential. Candidates should demonstrate: Scientific initiative Strong analytical skills Strong written and verbal communication skills Ability to work independently Ability to work collaboratively in a multidisciplinary environment Relevant experience in one or more of the following is preferred: Preclinical imaging Hyperpolarized MRI, conventional MRI, or NMR spectroscopy Mass spectrometry–based metabolomics or stable isotope tracing Chemistry Mouse handling and animal experimentation Mammalian cell culture Molecular biology or biochemical methods Expertise in every area is not expected. However, willingness to work with animals and to develop complementary experimental skills is essential. The University of Pennsylvania is committed to supporting a diverse and inclusive workforce and, in most cases, is able to sponsor visas for qualified applicants. Visa sponsorship for this position is contingent upon eligibility as determined by the specific funding source and applicable regulations. Applicants should submit the following materials: Curriculum vitae (CV) Research statement describing prior experience and research interests Contact information for three references Please also include: Anticipated availability/start date Expected duration of commitment"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196036": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Scholar in Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation, Stroke, and Health Promotion",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: Perelman School of Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania School of Medicine Posted: Oct 20, 2026 Dr. Kimberly Waddell in the Department of Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation at the University of Pennsylvania is seeking a highly motivated Postdoctoral Fellow to join an NIH and VA-funded research program focused on strategies to improve physical activity and long-term disease management after stroke. Stroke is a leading cause of complex, long-term disability. Individuals receive most rehabilitation services within the first few weeks or months after stroke, after which they must manage their health and recovery with limited professional support. Dr. Waddell’s lab addresses this gap by studying how to leverage behavioral science and scalable, remote technologies to promote daily physical activity after stroke. Her research portfolio incorporates behavioral economic principles, social network support, and pragmatic clinical trial design to sustain long-term health behavior change. This specific postdoctoral position is primarily tied to a newly funded NCCIH R01 clinical trial examining the effects of gamification and social support—with and without mindfulness health coaching—for increasing daily physical activity and improving patient-reported outcomes after stroke. The postdoctoral fellow will play a key role in a collaborative, highly interdisciplinary research program investigating health promotion interventions post-stroke. The fellow will leverage Penn’s world-class health services and behavioral science ecosystem—including access to cutting-edge research centers, cross-departmental collaborations, and structured mentorship—to build an independent academic career and lead high-impact trial execution. Position is open to U.S. permanent residents and citizens only due to VA requirements. Research Opportunities: Depending on the post-doctoral fellow’s interests and experience, projects may include: 1.      Supporting the implementation of a pragmatic clinical trial to increase physical activity post-stroke. 2.      Examine how different behavioral economic and integrative health approaches effect daily steps and patient reported outcomes after stroke. 3.      Developing innovative approaches to understanding the intersection of behavior change, neurorehabilitation, integrative health, and digital technologies 4.      Developing complementary independent research questions and manuscripts based on the postdoctoral fellow’s own scientific interests 5.      Conducting qualitative research with clinical trial participants to understand participant experiences that will shape future research designs Training and Mentorship: The postdoctoral fellow will receive close mentorship in neurorehabilitation, health promotion, behavior change, and clinical trial design and management. The position is designed to support the post-doctoral fellow’s development toward an independent research career, with opportunities to lead publications, present findings at national and international meetings, develop grant applications, and pursue independent research questions. The fellow will be part of a highly collaborative, interdisciplinary research environment spanning rehabilitation medicine, neurology, epidemiology, biostatistics, and hospital medicine. There will also be opportunities to collaborate with investigators across institutions. The start date is flexible and can be negotiated based on the selected candidate's timeline. Applicants should have: PhD, MD, or equivalent doctoral degree in Rehabilitation Sciences, Physical or Occupational Therapy, Behavioral Economics, Public Health, Health Psychology, Kinesiology, or a related field. Strong interest in stroke recovery, digital health technologies, behavior change, integrative health, and/or clinical trial design. Demonstrated track record of peer-reviewed publication and strong quantitative/analytical skills. Experience with statistical programming (e.g., R, SAS) Strong scientific writing and communication skills Excellent communication, organizational, and collaborative skills. Applicants should submit a cover letter, CV, research statement (2 pages maximum), and contact information for two references. Materials should be submitted through interfolio. Any questions, or follow up inquiries, can be emailed to Dr. Kimberly Waddell (Kimberly.Waddell@pennmedicine.upenn.edu). Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195922": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow - Implementation Science and Dementia Public Health",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: School of Nursing Postdoctoral Location: Philadelphia, PA (University of Pennsylvania School of Nursing) Posted: Oct 19, 2026 Join the University of Pennsylvania School of Nursing research team as a Postdoctoral Fellow and help state, local and tribal public health programs put proven dementia strategies into practice. The fellow will build and run the implementation science behind the ADRD Public Health Adoption Accelerator (CDC-RFA-DP-26-0227, Component 3): a national registry of which public health sites are taking which Healthy Brain Initiative (HBI) Road Map actions, and a coaching program that helps sites take more of them. The role offers high-impact research with a multidisciplinary team, direct work with health departments and tribal programs across the country, and mentored preparation for an independent research career. The Postdoctoral Fellow will engage in a range of research activities, primarily focused on: • Prioritization and Implementation Strategy Registry (35%). The fellow will lead the coding manual and environmental scan that score 30+ BOLD-funded sites against the 24 State and Local Road Map actions or the 13 actions in the Road Map for American Indian and Alaska Native Peoples (coded separately), and maintain the Registry as sites are re-rated over time. • Coaching strategy and evaluation (30%) . Working with the TA coach the fellow will specify tiered coaching (light, standard, intensive) as named implementation strategies (ERIC), design coaching logs, and evaluate coaching dose, fidelity and implementation outcomes. • Launchpad needs assessment (15%) . The fellow will build the baseline rating instrument, code barriers and facilitators using CFIR 2.0, and help tribal partners adapt measures in line with tribal data sovereignty. • Dissemination (10%) . The fellow will lead manuscripts, conference abstracts and practice briefs, including how-to guides for Road Map actions with few existing models. • 10% - Protected time to conduct and disseminate independent research. Mentorship will be provided to foster the scientist's professional growth, including mentored grant writing toward independence. Responsibilities: • Independent review of 30+ BOLD sites for an environmental scan. • Identify exemplar sites for each Road Map action and load coded data for the Registry launch • Build the Launchpad baseline needs assessment and summarize site readiness by action. • Apply implementation frameworks such as CFIR, ERIC, RE-AIM and Proctor's implementation outcomes across the project. • Maintain the action-by-strategy coaching menu and design coaching logs; analyze coaching dose, fidelity and outcomes. • Partner with state, local and tribal health department staff, the TA coach and advisory groups to keep tools practical for public health settings; follow tribal protocols and data sovereignty agreements. • Support day-to-day project administration with Project Director and PI, including timelines, deliverables and compliance with CDC funding requirements. • Supervise and mentor graduate research assistants who assist with document extraction and coding. • Manage and analyze qualitative and quantitative data using appropriate software and methods. • Contribute to conference abstracts and peer-reviewed manuscripts through lead and co-authorship; present at scientific and public health practice meetings. • Collaborate on the development of external grant proposals. Position Details: Approximate Start and End Dates : This is a full-time, fixed-term, grant-funded position expected to run from January 2027 (or when the position is filled) through December 2030, based on the availability of continued grant funding and performance. Appointment Type : Full-time, fixed-term, grant-funded position. Location : University of Pennsylvania School of Nursing, Philadelphia, PA. Hybrid options. Contract and Salary : The annual base salary for this position is $67,000. This is a full-time, fixed-term position for 1 year with potential for renewal based on performance and continued grant funding. • A PhD in public health or equivalent doctoral degree in nursing, implementation science, health services research, gerontology, health policy or a related field by the start date. • Training or applied experience with implementation science frameworks (e.g., CFIR, ERIC, RE-AIM, Proctor's implementation outcomes). • Experience with systematic document review or qualitative coding, including building codebooks and assessing inter-rater reliability. • Mixed-methods skills, including qualitative analysis software (e.g., NVivo, Dedoose, ATLAS.ti) and a statistical package (e.g., R, Stata, SAS). • Experience working with state, local or tribal health departments, or with CDC-funded programs, is strongly preferred. • A background in aging, dementia, caregiving or chronic disease prevention is strongly preferred. • Experience partnering with American Indian or Alaska Native communities and familiarity with tribal data sovereignty principles is preferred. • Experience with technical assistance, learning collaboratives or facilitation, and with database or dashboard tools (e.g., REDCap, Airtable, Tableau), is preferred. • Excellent scientific writing and oral communication skills, with a demonstrated ability to prepare manuscripts and present findings clearly. • A track record of scholarly productivity, including peer-reviewed publications and conference presentations. • Ability to work independently, take initiative and manage multiple tasks. • Strong interpersonal and collaborative skills for work within a research team and with public health partners. Interested applicants are invited to submit the following application materials: 1. Cover Letter : A letter outlining your research interests in implementation science, relevant qualifications, and skills that align with this position. 2. Curriculum Vitae (CV) : A comprehensive CV including education, research experience, publications, presentations and other relevant professional activities. 3. Representative Writing Sample : A sample of scholarly writing, such as a published peer-reviewed manuscript, representative of the work described in this position. 4. List of Three References . Application Deadline : Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled. Work Authorization : Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States at the time employment begins."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196310": {
      "title": "Two Postdoctoral Scholar Positions, Pathobiology Dept, Dr. Nicola Mason Lab",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: School of Veterinary Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania - School of Veterinary Medicine Posted: Oct 23, 2026 The Mason Lab in the Department of Pathobiology, at the University of Pennsylvania (Penn) is recruiting Postdoctoral Scholars to join our team within the Comparative Immunotherapy Program. Our work focuses on developing comparative immunotherapeutic approaches to study correlative biomarkers of immune response in canine patients with spontaneous disease processes including cancer and autoimmunity. Our comparative approach leverages naturally occurring diseases in client owned, pet dogs to inform human and veterinary medicine. Our lab has a particular interest in engineered cell-based therapeutics (e.g. CAR-T, TIL therapeutics), checkpoint inhibitors and bi-specific T cell engagers. In collaboration with partner labs at the University of Pennsylvania, we are working to identify biomarkers of immune response within canine patient populations, including phenotypic and functional characteristics of various immune cell populations and their interactions with immune suppressive and stromal elements within the tumor microenvironment, serum metabolites and gut microbiome. Some of the techniques our group applies are: - Flow cytometry, immunohistochemistry, ELISA, ELISpot, cytotoxicity assays - CRISPR/Cas9 editing and gene transfer technologies (lentiviral, retroviral, electroporation) - Advanced sequencing technologies, at single cell and bulk levels. - Seahorse flux assays, LC/MS analysis - Clinical in vivo assessments (client owned animals with spontaneous disease processes) We aim to advance: - novel immunotherapeutics for cancer and autoimmunity both in companion animal and human species - baseline markers of prognosis for patient stratification to immunotherapeutic clinical trials - novel technologies applied to veterinary species to address outstanding biological questions centered on immune response and immunological fitness Disease areas of focus include, but are not limited to B cell lymphoma, osteosarcoma, histiocytic sarcoma, squamous cell carcinoma, and auto-immunity including pemphigus and hemolytic anemia. The training/mentorship opportunities are in the following areas (among others): - Developing engineered autologous and allogeneic cell-based therapies for assessment in canine patients with cancer or autoimmunity. - Developing novel biologics centered on bi-specific T cell engagers and identifying correlative biomarkers of response in canine patients - Investigate mechanisms of resistance to checkpoint inhibitors in solid tumors - Studying cancer-immune cell interactions within the primary and metastatic tumor microenvironment using functional biology approaches & advanced in-situ sequencing/data analytics. Prospective trainees can expect the following: Mentorship, Freedom, Resources, Exciting Science, & Fun. Further details can be found here: Mason Lab (1) A proven record of publication (this can include bioRxiv and/or arXiv papers, or about to publish), (2) Skills in the following areas are required: molecular biology, cell culture, and animal work, computational skills in one or more of the following areas are desirable but not required: next generation sequencing data analysis, statistics, and machine learning. Start date and term are negotiable. Candidates should have a Ph.D. and a demonstrated interest in comparative immunology and immunobiology Applicants are required to submit the following materials through Interfolio: 1) CV 2) Cover letter 3) A list of 3 referees Questions or follow up inquiries can be emailed to Dr. Mason at nmason@vet.upenn.edu"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196103": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: The Wharton School: The Wharton School - Business Economics and Public Policy Location: Philadelphia, PA Posted: Oct 22, 2026 The Business Economics and Public Policy Department of The Wharton School at the University of Pennsylvania invites applications for a Postdoctoral Researcher with the Wharton Workforce Futures Lab under Professor Corinne Low. The position will be for an initial appointment of one year with a possible renewal based on satisfactory performance. The scholar would develop projects related to making workplaces better for parents, eliminating rigid gender roles at home, and debunking narratives that restrict women’s workplace progress together with Professor Low. Due to the funding model of this position, the scholar will be advancing coauthored work with Professor Low and other lab affiliates. An ideal candidate is interested in going on to a tenure track academic position and developing a stronger research portfolio over the course of the post-doc. Applicants with existing research in the area of workplace innovation and gender economics, along with  experience with field or lab experiments, as well as strong coding skills, are encouraged to apply. About the Lab Wharton’s Workforce Futures Lab will produce, test, and share rigorous research on what makes workplaces work for all. In partnership with organizations, the Lab will study practical workplace innovations and rapidly translate pivotal academic findings into tools that decision-makers can use. The Workforce Futures Lab has been named one of the 18 awardees of the $60 million Workplace Innovation Now (WIN) Challenge , receiving a $2.5 million grant to advance an innovative solution to help women thrive and everyone win in a rapidly changing workplace. Candidates should show outstanding capacity for research, as well as excellent communication skills. Applicants must have a Ph.D. in Economics or a related field from an accredited institution. Applications will be considered as they are received. The position is expected to begin as of July 1, 2027; an earlier start date may be considered. Please email workforcefutures@wharton.upenn.edu with any questions. Application Requirements: To apply, submit the following materials through our online application system: ·      Cover letter ·      Curriculum vitae ·      Research statement ·      Job Market paper ·      Other selected paper(s) and/or publication(s) if available · Contact information for three references who will automatically receive requests for letters of recommendation"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196157": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - Academic Neuro-Oncologist - AC Track",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Neurology Location: Philadelphia, PA Posted: Oct 27, 2026 Deadline: Oct 27, 2028 The Department of Neurology at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for an Assistant Professor position in the non-tenure academic clinician track. Applicants must have an M.D. or equivalent degree. Preffered applicants willl have ABPN Board Certified in Neurology, UCNS certified or eligible. Teaching responsibilities may include instruction of medical students, Neurology Residents, Neuro-Oncology Fellows, nurses, and advanced practice providers. We function as a highly cohesive division with an expectation of camaraderie, teamwork, and professionalism. Clinical responsibilities may include management of patients with primary brain and metastatic brain tumors and neurological complications of cancer. Expertise in management of Neurofibromatosis is highly valued. This is an exciting opportunity to collaborate with a high volume, diverse, and medically complex patient population. Candidate will have the opportunity to participate in clinical research and clinical trials to expand the portfolio of cutting-edge trials at the brain tumor program at the University of Pennsylvania. The University of Pennsylvania is searching for a highly motivated, academic neuro-oncologist to join the rapidly expanding Brain Tumor program at the Abramson Cancer Center. The successful candidate will be joining an established program multidisciplinary group of highly collaborative physician team, specialized nurses, and pharmacists and a center with state-of-the-art facilities. Please send your CV along with a letter of interest via email to: Richard Phillips, MD PhD, Chief, Division of Neuro-Oncology: richard.phillips@pennmedicine.upenn.edu"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195778": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, AC Track, Division of Allergy and Immunology",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Pediatrics: Perelman School of Medicine - Pediatrics - Allergy and Immunology Location: Philadelphia, PA Posted: Oct 26, 2026 Deadline: Oct 26, 2028 Children's Hospital of Philadelphia and the Division of Allergy/Immunology in the Department of Pediatrics at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seek candidates for several Assistant Professor positions in the non-tenure academic clinician track. Applicants must have an M.D. or equivalent degree. Board certified/eligible in Allergy and Immunology required. Teaching responsibilities may include high quality teaching of CHOP fellows and residents. Clinical responsibilities may include developing a regional referral base in Allergy and Immunology. Practice sites include CHOP community pediatric specialty care offices in Exton, PA, Lancaster, PA or a combination of locations. Inpatient call is rotated."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194294": {
      "title": "Research Associate    Shi Lab",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Cancer Biology Location: Kelley Research Building, 421Curie Blvd, Philadelphia, PA 19104 Posted: Oct 26, 2026 Deadline: Oct 26, 2028 Developing CRISPR technology for delivery genome engineering toolkits in cancer cells and primary cells.  To achieve this, the candidate will employ a variety of techniques, encompassing molecular biology, next-generation sequencing, and multiplex data analysis. Applicants will have the opportunity to enhance critical thinking skills, become acquainted with CRISPR genome editing literature and experimental techniques, prepare manuscripts, present research findings at national and international meetings, mentor junior scientists, and partake in career development training programs offered by the University. Specialty and expertise in genome editing, CRISPR, primary cell culture techniques, molecular biology, next-generation sequencing, basic bioinformatics Candidate should have a a Ph.D. and minimum of 3-5 years postdoctoral experience.   Candidate should have specialty and expertise in genome editing, CRISPR, primary cell culture techniques, molecular biology, next-generation sequencing, and basic bioinformatics. N/A"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196281": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow – Bone Marrow Adipocyte Biology and Maternal Skeletal Health",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: Perelman School of Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania School of Medicine Posted: Oct 23, 2026 A postdoctoral fellow position is available in the McKay Orthopaedic Research Laboratory, Department of Orthopaedic Surgery at the University of Pennsylvania, under the mentorship of X. Sherry Liu, PhD, to investigate the role of bone marrow adipocytes (BMAds) in skeletal adaptation during lactation. Lactation induces profound and rapidly reversible changes in both the skeleton and bone marrow adipose tissue to meet the high metabolic and calcium demands of milk production. Our research seeks to understand the functional role of BMAds during this process. The project integrates transgenic mouse models, in vivo skeletal imaging, bone histomorphometry, cell and molecular biology, and metabolic approaches to investigate interactions among BMAds and bone cells. The successful candidate will work closely with Dr. X. Sherry Liu and will be part of a highly collaborative and interdisciplinary musculoskeletal research community at Penn. The project includes collaborations with investigators with complementary expertise in skeletal biology and metabolism, including Ling Qin, PhD (UPenn McKay Lab), and Fanxin Long, PhD (CHOP), and provides access to the broad research and training environment of the Penn Center for Musculoskeletal Disorders (PCMD). The fellow will have opportunities to develop independent research directions in bone–adipose interactions, skeletal metabolism, and women’s musculoskeletal health, with strong mentorship toward an independent research career. This position is open to both U.S. citizens and foreign nationals. Candidates requiring visa sponsorship are welcome to apply. Candidates should have a PhD, MD/PhD, or equivalent degree in bone biology, adipose biology, metabolism, physiology, bioengineering, or a related field. Experience with mouse models, molecular/cellular biology, histology, imaging, or metabolic assays is desirable. We are particularly interested in candidates who are highly motivated, collaborative, and interested in developing an independent research career. Applications should be sent through Interfolio using the link below and include: (1) cover letter, (2) curriculum vitae ; and (3) contact information for three references."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196441": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Positions in Functional Transcriptomics, Cell Signaling, and Cancer Immunotherapy",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Postdoctoral Positions: Perelman School of Medicine Postdoctoral Location: University of Pennsylvania - Perelman School of Medicine Posted: Oct 27, 2026 The Thomas-Tikhonenko laboratory is recruiting postdoctoral fellows to pursue newly funded basic and translational research projects aimed at elucidating the role of aberrant mRNA splicing in defining cancer vulnerabilities and identifying new targets for therapy . Our research spans hematologic malignancies and solid tumors, combining mechanistic studies with the development of novel immunotherapies. Please visit the lab website for ongoing projects, recent publications, and current members. We are located in the Colket Translational Research Building of Children's Hospital of Philadelphia, which shares a contiguous campus with the University of Pennsylvania's Perelman School of Medicine. This setting provides ample opportunities for interactions with the NCI-designated Abramson Cancer Center, the Institute for Immunology and Immune Health, the Institute for Biomedical Informatics, and other research programs. We seek ambitious scientists who thrive on challenges, enjoy working in a highly collaborative and dynamic research environment, and are eager to make their marks. Strong candidates will hold PhD, MD, DVM, or equivalent doctoral degrees and have demonstrated expertise in at least one of the following fields: RNA biology, cancer genetics/genomics, cell signaling, immunotherapy, or bioinformatics. Recent graduates with strong research accomplishments in adjacent areas are also encouraged to apply. Eligibility for federal training grants is a plus but not a requirement. Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until all positions are filled. Applications should be sent through Interfolio using the link below and include: 1) C over Letter succinctly describing past accomplishments and current research interests ; 2) C urriculum vitae; 3) C ontact information f or three professional references."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196284": {
      "title": "Doctor of Nursing Practice Program Senior or Advanced Senior Lecturer",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: School of Nursing: School of Nursing - Biobehavioral Health Sciences Location: Philadelphia, PA Posted: Oct 23, 2026 The University of Pennsylvania School of Nursing , one of 12 Schools in a highly ranked institution, is dedicated to the integration of interdisciplinary research, education, and practice. The Biobehavioral Health Sciences (BHS) Department in the School of Nursing seeks candidates for a full-time Senior or Advanced Senior Lecturer position in the Doctor of Nursing Practice program with experience in systems transformation and value-based practice in quality improvement. Qualifications: The Senior or Advanced Senior Lecturer will have primary teaching responsibilities in the DNP program and consultative teaching responsibilities across entry-to-practice and graduate programs. Interested parties must have experience in academic teaching using multiple modalities (didactic, online) and experience in leading and mentoring practice-focused interprofessional/disciplinary systems transformation initiatives in healthcare. Demonstrated ability to facilitate the creation and sustainment of change at the organizational and systems levels, strong analytical skills, and the ability to assimilate complex information from numerous sources are required. Excellent writing skills are needed. Strong interpersonal and organizational skills are essential. There will be opportunities to substantially contribute to continuous curricular innovation and evaluation of the DNP program. Therefore, experience in curriculum development/evaluation is preferred. Valid RN license in Pennsylvania is required. Candidates who are not yet licensed but are interested are welcome to apply if they are eligible for licensure and certifications. Doctoral-level preparation is required to apply (DNP, PhD, etc.). Interested candidates should submit their CV and Cover Letter."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196442": {
      "title": "AC Track Faculty Position, Palliative Oncology – Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Department of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Medicine - Hematology/Oncology Location: Philadelphia, PA Posted: Oct 28, 2026 Deadline: Oct 28, 2028 The Division of Hematology-Oncology in the Department of Medicine at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for several Assistant Professor positions in the non-tenure academic clinician track. Expertise is required in the specific area of palliative oncology. Candidates must have completed an accredited Hospice and Palliative Medicine Fellowship. Applicants must have an M.D. or equivalent degree. Board certification or eligibility in Hospice and Palliative Medicine is required. Teaching responsibilities may include palliative care and other subspecialty fellows and residents who rotate through the Palliative and Supportive Oncology Clinic and have a strong background and interest in education to further the Division’s commitment to equip front-line oncology clinicians and oncology trainees with basic palliative care skills in serious illness communication and symptom management. The academic clinician track requires 100 credits per year of active and high-quality teaching. Clinical responsibilities may include excellent clinical skills in providing ambulatory palliative care consultation for cancer patients in close partnership with each patient’s oncology team. The successful candidate will have opportunities to partner with leadership in the Division of Hematology and Oncology and the Cancer Service Line to lead quality improvement initiatives aimed at improving end-of-life outcomes for cancer patients throughout Penn Medicine."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196182": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Clinical Medicine- General Endocrinology with a focus on obesity medicine",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Department of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Medicine - Endocrinology, Diabetes and Metabolism Location: Philadelphia, PA Posted: Oct 28, 2026 Deadline: Oct 28, 2028 The Division of Endocrinology, Diabetes and Metabolism in the Department of Medicine at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for several Assistant Professor positions in the non-tenure academic clinician track. Expertise is required in the specific area of non-surgical weight management and/or obesity medicine. Applicants must have an M.D. degree. Candidates must be board certified or board eligible in Endocrinology, Diabetes and Metabolism. Teaching responsibilities may include conducting clinical and education activities. Selected candidates will have the opportunity to teach in the endocrine fellowship and internal medicine residency programs in the Perelman School of Medicine. The academic clinician track requires 100 credits per year of active and high-quality teaching. Clinical responsibilities may include providing clinical care for patients with Endocrine disorders focusing on obesity medicine. The new physician will be an integral member of the Division of Endocrinology, Diabetes and Metabolism. Selected candidates will have demonstrated excellent qualifications in education and clinical care."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194395": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Tenure Track, Basic and Translational Research in Pediatric Cancer",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Pediatrics: Perelman School of Medicine - Pediatrics - Oncology Location: Philadelphia, PA Posted: Oct 28, 2026 Deadline: Oct 28, 2028 Children's Hospital of Philadelphia and the Division of Oncology in the Department of Pediatrics at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seek candidates for an Assistant Professor position in the tenure track. Expertise is required in the specific area of basic and translational research focused on pediatric cancer. Applicants must have an M.D. or M.D./Ph.D. or equivalent degree. Board certified or eligible in pediatric hematology/oncology. Teaching responsibilities may include teaching and research training of medical students, graduate students, residents, medical fellows in pediatric hematology/oncology or other disciplines, and post-doctoral fellows. Clinical responsibilities may include serving as attending physician participating in the inpatient and outpatient care of children, adolescents and young adults with pediatric cancer. Research or scholarship responsibilities may include directing an independent NIH-funded research program focused on basic and translational research directly related to pediatric cancer. Candidates should have a track record of successful extramural funding."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196459": {
      "title": "Breast Imaging - Academic Clinician - Junior Rank",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Radiology Location: Philadelphia, PA Posted: Oct 28, 2026 Deadline: Oct 28, 2028 The Department of Radiology at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for several Assistant Professor positions in the non-tenure academic clinician track. Expertise is required in the specific area of Breast Imaging. Applicants must have an M.D. or M.D./Ph.D. or equivalent degree. Applicants must be American Board of Radiology (ABR) certified or board-eligible in Diagnostic Radiology and have subspecialty training in Breast Imaging or equivalent experience. Teaching responsibilities may include daily clinical supervision of residents and breast imaging fellows and lecturing to medical students, radiology residents, fellows and trainees from other departments. Clinical responsibilities may include ing the most advanced breast imaging technology available to provide individually tailored, comprehensive evaluations for the early and precise detection and diagnosis of breast diseases. The successful candidate will conduct high-quality interventional breast procedures, including stereotactic, ultrasound-guided, and MRI-guided biopsies. Penn Radiology provides clinical services for the Hospital of the University of Pennsylvania, Penn Presbyterian Medical Center, Pennsylvania Hospital, Philadelphia Veterans Affairs Medical Center, and affiliated outpatient imaging centers in Pennsylvania, New Jersey, and Delaware. The clinical mission of the Division of Breast Imaging is to provide individually tailored comprehensive evaluations for the early detection and diagnosis of breast diseases. Our Breast Imaging Division, accredited by the American College of Radiology, provides screening and problem-solving mammography, breast MRI, and multimodality intervention for outpatients at the Hospital of the University of Pennsylvania and its satellite locations."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195068": {
      "title": "Cancer Survivorship Research Assistant Professor, Clinician Educator, Pediatric Oncology",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Pediatrics: Perelman School of Medicine - Pediatrics - Oncology Location: Philadelphia, PA Posted: Oct 28, 2026 Deadline: Oct 28, 2028 The Division of Oncology in the Department of Pediatrics at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for an Assistant Professor position in the non-tenure clinician educator track. Expertise is required in the specific area of pediatric cancer survivorship research. Applicants must have an M.D. or M.D./Ph.D. or equivalent degree. Board-certified or board-eligible in pediatric hematology/oncology. Teaching responsibilities may include didactic and clinical instruction of medical students, residents, post-doctoral fellows, instructors, and/or clinical research assistants and educational liaison with advanced practice providers. Clinical responsibilities may include inpatient and outpatient care focused on children, adolescents, and young adults with cancer at the Philadelphia University City Hospital and active involvement in fellowship training and mentoring. A major focus of outpatient care will be on long-term survivors of pediatric cancer. The candidate must have at least two years of experience as an attending physician after completion of fellowship training. Research or scholarship responsibilities may include expanding clinical research focused on survivorship outcomes for children, adolescents, and young adults previously treated for cancer. The successful applicant will be academically productive, collaborative, and able to expand our already robust research enterprise. The successful candidate must have an established record of peer-reviewed scholarship and extramural grant funding and is expected to have greater than or equal to 50% independent research effort funding at the time of appointment."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196461": {
      "title": "Cardiovascular Imaging - Academic Clinician - Junior Rank",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Radiology Location: Philadelphia, PA Posted: Oct 28, 2026 Deadline: Oct 28, 2028 The Department of Radiology at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for several Assistant Professor positions in the non-tenure academic clinician track. Applicants must have an M.D. or M.D./Ph.D. or equivalent degree. Applicants must be American Board of Radiology certified or eligible with appropriate subspecialty training. Teaching responsibilities may include daily clinical supervision of residents and cardiovascular imaging fellows and lecturing to medical students, radiology residents, fellows and trainees from other departments. Clinical responsibilities may include interpreting in all aspects of cardiovascular radiology, including cardiac CT, cardiac MRI, and vascular CTA and MRA. The successful candidate will also participate in shared after-hours and weekend coverage. The Penn Radiology Cardiovascular Imaging Division offers specialized expertise to the world-renowned Penn Aorta Program. This division handles a high volume and variety of cases, including coronary computed tomography angiography (CTA), CT scans for structural heart disease, TAVR (transcatheter aortic valve replacement), TMVR (transcatheter mitral valve replacement), and TTVR (transcatheter tricuspid valve replacement). Additionally, cardiac MRI is conducted for various clinical indications. The radiologists collaborate closely with cardiologists to provide comprehensive care. Penn Radiology offers clinical services at several locations: the Hospital of the University of Pennsylvania, Penn Presbyterian Medical Center, Pennsylvania Hospital, Philadelphia Veterans Affairs Medical Center, and affiliated outpatient imaging centers in Pennsylvania, New Jersey, and Delaware."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196241": {
      "title": "Senior Rank Standing Faculty in Experimental Psychiatry: Sleep and Chronobiology Division",
      "description": "Department: University of Pennsylvania: Perelman School of Medicine: Perelman School of Medicine - Psychiatry Location: Philadelphia, PA Posted: Oct 28, 2026 Deadline: Oct 28, 2028 The Department of Psychiatry at the Perelman School of Medicine at the University of Pennsylvania seeks candidates for an Associate or Full Professor position in either the non-tenure clinician educator track or the tenure track. Expertise is required in the specific area of the effects of sleep loss, circadian disruption, and environmental stressors on human cognition, behavior, and health. Applicants with experience in one or more of the following are encouraged to apply: neurobehavioral and cognitive assessment; human performance in isolated, confined, and extreme environments, including spaceflight and spaceflight analogs; environmental exposures such as noise; wearable and mobile health technologies; and occupational fatigue and safety. Applicants must have an M.D./Ph.D or equivalent degree. Teaching responsibilities may include teaching and mentoring medical students, graduate students, psychiatry residents, and postdoctoral fellows, as well as lecture opportunities within the department and the Perelman School of Medicine, including courses on sleep, circadian rhythms, and behavioral neuroscience. Clinical responsibilities may include for candidates with appropriate clinical training and licensure, clinical evaluation and treatment of adults with sleep, circadian, or other psychiatric disorders (e.g., behavioral sleep medicine). Research or scholarship responsibilities may include developing collaborative and independent research programs on how sleep loss, circadian misalignment, and environmental stressors (e.g., noise, isolation and confinement, spaceflight) affect human cognition, behavior, and health in laboratory, field, operational, and spaceflight or spaceflight-analog settings. Scholarship includes developing and maintaining a program of extramurally funded research (e.g., NIH, NASA, FAA, DoD) and collaborating with the Unit for Experimental Psychiatry on new studies and grant applications. Candidates should have an established record of independent, extramurally funded research and peer-reviewed publications commensurate with rank, and a strong interest in interdisciplinary collaboration. The position is based in the Unit for Experimental Psychiatry (www.med.upenn.edu/uep), whose research spans sleep and circadian science, environmental noise and health, occupational fatigue, and astronaut behavioral health, and which operates the Chronobiology Isolation Laboratory at the Hospital of the University of Pennsylvania. Rank will be commensurate with experience."
    }
  },
